(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e1a.9547533c84.js"]={"1":[{"id":"sdl01_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define neuroanatomical directional terms and planes of section.","batch":1,"stem":"In standard neuroanatomical terminology, which direction refers to a location closer to the midline of the brain or spinal cord?","choices":{"A":"Lateral","B":"Rostral","C":"Medial","D":"Caudal","E":"Dorsal"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Medial describes a structure's position relative to the midline of the neuraxis, with structures closer to midline described as more medial. Lateral is the opposite term, denoting distance from midline. Rostral and caudal describe positions along the long axis of the neuraxis, toward the nose or tail, respectively. Dorsal describes a position toward the back surface of the neuraxis, not distance from midline.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Directional terms are absolute relative to the neuraxis, not gravity, so a patient positioned supine still has a dorsal brainstem surface that faces posteriorly. COMLEX: Correct use of medial, lateral, rostral, and caudal is essential for describing physical exam and imaging findings precisely.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — medullary landmark identification along the rostrocaudal axis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define neuroanatomical directional terms and planes of section.","batch":1,"stem":"Because of the cephalic flexure that occurs during embryonic development, which pair of directional terms points in different absolute directions in the forebrain compared to the brainstem?","choices":{"A":"Dorsal and ventral, because the forebrain axis bends at the cephalic flexure","B":"Medial and lateral, because both axes are defined identically in every CNS region","C":"Superior and inferior, because these terms are not standard neuraxis terms","D":"Proximal and distal, because these terms apply only to peripheral nerves","E":"Ipsilateral and contralateral, because these terms describe sidedness rather than axis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The forebrain rotates at the cephalic flexure during development so that its rostrocaudal axis runs from the frontal to occipital pole, creating a sharp bend relative to the straight axis of the brainstem and spinal cord. Because of this bend, dorsal and ventral point in different absolute directions in the forebrain compared to the brainstem, even though both terms remain defined relative to the neuraxis. Medial and lateral are defined identically throughout the CNS regardless of the flexure. Superior and inferior are body-axis terms rather than standard neuraxis terms, and proximal and distal describe positions along a limb or peripheral nerve.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The forebrain-brainstem angulation at the midbrain-thalamus junction is a classic source of confusion when interpreting sagittal MRI. COMLEX: Recognizing this flexure helps correctly localize lesions described as dorsal or ventral on imaging reports.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — medullary landmark identification along the rostrocaudal axis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define neuroanatomical directional terms and planes of section.","batch":1,"stem":"Which imaging plane is oriented nearly perpendicular to the long axis of the forebrain but runs nearly parallel to the long axis of the brainstem and spinal cord?","choices":{"A":"Axial plane","B":"Sagittal plane","C":"Oblique plane","D":"Coronal plane","E":"Parasagittal plane"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Coronal images are cut perpendicular to the rostrocaudal axis of the forebrain, so a coronal section of the cerebral hemispheres shows true cross-sections of forebrain structures; because the brainstem axis is angled relative to the forebrain, that same coronal plane runs nearly parallel to the brainstem long axis and cuts it more longitudinally. Axial planes run parallel to the forebrain's rostrocaudal axis and nearly perpendicular to the brainstem, so they cross-section the brainstem. Sagittal and parasagittal planes divide the brain into right and left portions. Oblique planes are angled arbitrarily and are not one of the three standard orthogonal planes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Recognizing why a coronal MRI slice shows the brainstem in a nearly longitudinal cut, rather than cross-section, is a common source of imaging confusion. COMLEX: Understanding standard imaging planes is required before interpreting any CT or MRI vignette.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — medullary landmark identification along the rostrocaudal axis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguish the central and peripheral nervous system organization.","batch":1,"stem":"Which structures together compose the central nervous system?","choices":{"A":"The brain and spinal cord","B":"The spinal cord and spinal nerves","C":"The brain and cranial nerves","D":"The autonomic ganglia and peripheral nerves","E":"The dorsal root ganglia and peripheral nerves"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The central nervous system consists of the brain and spinal cord, both protected within the skull and vertebral column. The peripheral nervous system consists of the cranial nerves, spinal nerves, and peripheral ganglia that connect the CNS to the rest of the body. Spinal nerves, cranial nerves, autonomic ganglia, and dorsal root ganglia are all peripheral structures, not central structures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The CNS versus PNS distinction determines which cells are affected by disease, since oligodendrocytes myelinate CNS axons while Schwann cells myelinate PNS axons. COMLEX: This distinction is foundational for localizing whether a lesion is central or peripheral on physical exam.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 1 — dura, arachnoid, and pia mater as CNS-specific meningeal layers","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguish the central and peripheral nervous system organization.","batch":1,"stem":"The posterior root of a spinal nerve carries which type of information into the spinal cord?","choices":{"A":"Motor commands to skeletal muscle","B":"Visceromotor commands to smooth muscle","C":"Motor commands to cardiac muscle","D":"Descending commands from the cerebral cortex","E":"Sensory information from the periphery"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The posterior, or dorsal, root of a spinal nerve is afferent and carries sensory information from peripheral receptors into the spinal cord. The anterior, or ventral, root is efferent and carries motor impulses away from the spinal cord to skeletal muscle, and visceromotor fibers reach smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, and glands through separate autonomic pathways. Descending commands from the cerebral cortex travel in corticospinal fibers within the white matter of the cord, not through the posterior root.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dorsal root ganglia contain pseudounipolar sensory neuron cell bodies and are a classic site of herpes zoster reactivation. COMLEX: Testing dermatomal sensation assesses posterior root and dorsal root ganglion integrity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 1 — dura, arachnoid, and pia mater as CNS-specific meningeal layers","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguish the central and peripheral nervous system organization.","batch":1,"stem":"Which term describes the functional division of the nervous system that innervates smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, and glandular epithelium?","choices":{"A":"The somatic motor nervous system","B":"The visceromotor, or autonomic, nervous system","C":"The special sensory nervous system","D":"The general sensory nervous system","E":"The enteric sensory nervous system"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The visceromotor nervous system, also called the autonomic nervous system, has components in both the CNS and PNS and innervates smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, and glandular epithelium, regulating functions normally outside conscious control. The somatic motor system innervates skeletal muscle instead. Special and general sensory divisions carry afferent, not efferent, information, and enteric sensory fibers are a subset of visceral sensory input, not the entire visceromotor efferent system.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The autonomic nervous system's sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions are frequently tested through their opposing effects on heart rate, pupils, and gut motility. COMLEX: Viscerosomatic reflexes and Chapman points relate directly to visceromotor pathway anatomy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 1 — dura, arachnoid, and pia mater as CNS-specific meningeal layers","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the five primary and secondary brain vesicles and their adult derivatives.","batch":1,"stem":"The prosencephalon, one of the three primary brain vesicles, gives rise to which two secondary vesicles?","choices":{"A":"The metencephalon and myelencephalon","B":"The mesencephalon and metencephalon","C":"The telencephalon and mesencephalon","D":"The telencephalon and diencephalon","E":"The diencephalon and myelencephalon"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The prosencephalon, or forebrain vesicle, divides into the telencephalon, which forms the cerebral hemispheres and basal ganglia, and the diencephalon, which forms the thalamus, hypothalamus, and pineal gland. The rhombencephalon, or hindbrain vesicle, divides into the metencephalon, forming the pons and cerebellum, and the myelencephalon, forming the medulla. The mesencephalon remains a single vesicle and forms the midbrain without further subdividing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The prosencephalon to telencephalon plus diencephalon split is a classic embryology board question, often paired with lateral and third ventricle derivation. First Aid mnemonic for ordering all five secondary vesicles: telencephalon is first, diencephalon is second, and the rest fall in alphabetical order, mesencephalon, metencephalon, myelencephalon. COMLEX: Knowing vesicle derivatives helps predict which adult structures are affected by neural tube defects.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 8 — neural tube cavity derivatives forming the adult ventricular system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the five primary and secondary brain vesicles and their adult derivatives.","batch":1,"stem":"Which secondary brain vesicle gives rise to the pons and cerebellum in the adult brain?","choices":{"A":"The myelencephalon","B":"The diencephalon","C":"The telencephalon","D":"The mesencephalon","E":"The metencephalon"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The metencephalon is a secondary vesicle derived from the rhombencephalon and gives rise to the pons and cerebellum. The myelencephalon, the other rhombencephalon derivative, forms the medulla. The diencephalon and telencephalon are derivatives of the prosencephalon and form the thalamus and hypothalamus, and the cerebral hemispheres, respectively. The mesencephalon is a primary vesicle that remains undivided and forms the midbrain.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The pons and cerebellum share a common metencephalic origin, which explains their close anatomic relationship in the posterior fossa. COMLEX: Posterior fossa masses in children, such as medulloblastoma, arise from structures derived from the metencephalon and myelencephalon.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 8 — neural tube cavity derivatives forming the adult ventricular system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the five primary and secondary brain vesicles and their adult derivatives.","batch":1,"stem":"Which adult structure develops from the mesencephalon, the only primary vesicle that does not subdivide into two secondary vesicles?","choices":{"A":"The pons","B":"The cerebellum","C":"The midbrain","D":"The medulla","E":"The thalamus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The mesencephalon is one of the three primary brain vesicles and remains a single structure rather than dividing into two secondary vesicles, giving rise directly to the adult midbrain. The pons and cerebellum arise from the metencephalon, the medulla arises from the myelencephalon, and the thalamus arises from the diencephalon.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The mesencephalon's failure to subdivide is a frequently tested exception in brain vesicle questions. COMLEX: The cerebral aqueduct, which can become obstructed to cause noncommunicating hydrocephalus, runs through the midbrain derived from the mesencephalon.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 8 — neural tube cavity derivatives forming the adult ventricular system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate gray matter and white matter structurally and functionally.","batch":1,"stem":"Gray matter in the central nervous system is composed primarily of which structures?","choices":{"A":"Myelinated axon bundles traveling between regions","B":"Neuronal cell bodies and their dendrites","C":"Unmyelinated axon bundles traveling within a nucleus","D":"Glial cell processes forming the blood brain barrier","E":"Ependymal cells lining the ventricular system"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Gray matter consists of neuronal cell bodies, or perikarya, along with their dendrites and associated synapses, giving it a darker gray appearance in fresh tissue because it lacks the lipid-rich myelin found in white matter. White matter consists of bundles of myelinated axons that carry information between regions. Glial processes forming the blood brain barrier and ependymal cells lining the ventricles are supportive structures rather than the defining components of gray matter.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gray matter accumulates in the cerebral cortex, deep nuclei, and spinal cord horns, correlating with functional processing centers. COMLEX: Distinguishing gray from white matter pathology helps localize lesions on physical exam and imaging.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — spinal cord gray matter horns versus white matter columns","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate gray matter and white matter structurally and functionally.","batch":1,"stem":"Functionally, white matter tracts of the central nervous system primarily serve which purpose?","choices":{"A":"Synthesizing and releasing neurotransmitter directly into the bloodstream","B":"Generating cerebrospinal fluid within the ventricular system","C":"Conducting electrical signals between distant regions of gray matter","D":"Producing new neurons throughout adult life","E":"Regulating blood flow through autoregulation of arterioles"},"correct":"C","explanation":"White matter is composed of myelinated axon bundles whose primary function is to rapidly conduct action potentials between distant regions of gray matter, connecting cortical areas to each other and to subcortical structures. White matter does not synthesize or release neurotransmitter into the bloodstream, generate cerebrospinal fluid, produce new neurons, or directly regulate cerebral blood flow, all functions of other specialized structures such as the choroid plexus or vascular endothelium.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Demyelinating diseases such as multiple sclerosis selectively damage white matter conduction while relatively sparing gray matter cell bodies. COMLEX: White matter lesions on MRI correlate with disconnection syndromes rather than focal cortical deficits.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — spinal cord gray matter horns versus white matter columns","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate gray matter and white matter structurally and functionally.","batch":1,"stem":"Which term describes a named, function-specific cluster of neuronal cell bodies located within the central nervous system?","choices":{"A":"A ganglion","B":"A plexus","C":"A fascicle","D":"A tract","E":"A nucleus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Within the central nervous system, a named cluster of functionally related neuronal cell bodies is called a nucleus. Outside the central nervous system, an analogous cluster of cell bodies is called a ganglion. A plexus refers to a network of interweaving nerves or vessels, a fascicle is a bundle of axons within a peripheral nerve, and a tract is a bundle of axons within the central nervous system, all describing axonal groupings rather than cell body clusters.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Nucleus versus ganglion is a classic vocabulary distinction, with the caudate nucleus and dorsal root ganglion serving as canonical CNS and PNS examples. COMLEX: Correct terminology use is tested directly in anatomy-based board vignettes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — spinal cord gray matter horns versus white matter columns","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply anatomical terminology to clinical lesion localization, including ipsilateral and contralateral deficits.","batch":1,"stem":"Neurologic deficits found on the same side of the head and the same side of the body most strongly suggest a lesion located where?","choices":{"A":"In the cerebral hemisphere","B":"In the brainstem","C":"In the spinal cord","D":"At the neuromuscular junction","E":"In a peripheral nerve plexus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Deficits located on the same side of the head and body, meaning uncrossed deficits, generally indicate a lesion in the cerebral hemisphere, since corticospinal and corticobulbar fibers have not yet crossed at that level. Brainstem lesions typically produce crossed, or alternating, deficits, with cranial nerve findings on one side and body findings on the opposite side. Spinal cord lesions typically produce deficits confined to the body without cranial nerve involvement, and neuromuscular junction or peripheral nerve plexus lesions produce patterns specific to the innervated muscles rather than a hemibody pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The uncrossed versus crossed deficit rule is a rapid first step in localizing lesions along the neuraxis. COMLEX: This rule underlies the stepwise localization algorithm used throughout the neurologic examination.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 6 — crossed deficits in medial versus lateral medullary syndrome","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply anatomical terminology to clinical lesion localization, including ipsilateral and contralateral deficits.","batch":1,"stem":"A crossed, or alternating, neurologic deficit pattern, with cranial nerve findings on one side and body findings on the opposite side, most strongly localizes a lesion to which region?","choices":{"A":"The cerebral cortex","B":"The internal capsule","C":"The spinal cord","D":"The brainstem","E":"The basal nuclei"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A crossed deficit pattern, in which a cranial nerve finding occurs on one side and a long tract motor or sensory finding occurs on the opposite side of the body, is a hallmark of a brainstem lesion, because cranial nerve nuclei at that level have not yet crossed while the corticospinal and sensory fibers passing through that level have already crossed or will cross more caudally. Cortical, internal capsule, and basal nuclei lesions typically produce uncrossed, same-side deficits, and spinal cord lesions do not involve cranial nerves at all.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crossed deficits are the classic examination finding in brainstem stroke syndromes such as Weber and Wallenberg syndrome. COMLEX: Recognizing a crossed pattern quickly narrows localization to the brainstem before further subdividing by level.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 6 — crossed deficits in medial versus lateral medullary syndrome","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply anatomical terminology to clinical lesion localization, including ipsilateral and contralateral deficits.","batch":1,"stem":"Cranial nerve signs are generally considered more useful than long tract motor or sensory signs for lesion localization because they provide what advantage?","choices":{"A":"A more precise indication of whether the lesion is vascular or neoplastic","B":"A more precise indication of symptom duration and onset","C":"A more precise indication of whether the deficit is a symptom or a sign","D":"A more precise indication of overall disease severity","E":"A more precise indication of the specific brainstem level involved"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Cranial nerve nuclei and fascicles are confined to specific, well-defined levels of the brainstem, so a cranial nerve deficit points to a narrow segment of the midbrain, pons, or medulla. Long tracts such as the corticospinal tract traverse the entire brainstem, so a long tract sign alone cannot distinguish which level is affected. Cranial nerve findings do not by themselves indicate lesion etiology, timing, whether a finding is a symptom versus a sign, or overall severity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Combining a cranial nerve deficit with a contralateral long tract sign is the classic method for precisely localizing brainstem lesions. COMLEX: This principle is directly applied when localizing brainstem strokes in clinical vignettes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 6 — crossed deficits in medial versus lateral medullary syndrome","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Interpret cross-sectional neuroimaging using anatomical landmarks.","batch":1,"stem":"On a noncontrast CT scan of the head, an acute subarachnoid hemorrhage typically appears as which finding relative to the surrounding brain?","choices":{"A":"Hypodense, or darker than the surrounding brain","B":"Isodense, or the same shade as the surrounding brain","C":"Hyperintense on T1-weighted imaging only","D":"Hyperdense, or whiter than the surrounding brain","E":"Hypointense on T2-weighted imaging only"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Acute subarachnoid hemorrhage contains fresh clotted blood, which attenuates more x-rays than surrounding brain tissue and cerebrospinal fluid, so it appears hyperdense, or whiter, on noncontrast CT. An area of ischemia, by contrast, is typically hypodense on CT, and a lesion the same shade as surrounding brain would be isodense. Density and intensity are CT and MRI terms respectively, so hyperintense and hypointense describe MRI findings, not CT appearance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CT is the fastest and most sensitive early imaging study for acute subarachnoid hemorrhage, appearing hyperdense within the basal cisterns and sulci. COMLEX: Recognizing hyperdense blood on CT is essential for urgent management decisions in suspected aneurysm rupture.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — ventricular enlargement patterns on CT and MRI","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Interpret cross-sectional neuroimaging using anatomical landmarks.","batch":1,"stem":"On a T2-weighted MRI sequence, cerebrospinal fluid characteristically appears as which finding?","choices":{"A":"Bright, or hyperintense","B":"Dark, or hypointense","C":"Isointense with gray matter","D":"Isointense with white matter","E":"Isodense with bone"},"correct":"A","explanation":"On T2-weighted MRI sequences, cerebrospinal fluid appears bright, or hyperintense, which makes T2 sequences useful for evaluating the ventricles and detecting edema, which is also hyperintense. On T1-weighted sequences, cerebrospinal fluid instead appears dark, or hypointense. Isointense with gray or white matter and isodense with bone are not accurate descriptions of CSF appearance on standard T2 imaging.","boardPrep":"Step 1: T2-bright CSF makes T2-weighted or FLAIR sequences the preferred choice for detecting periventricular white matter lesions such as those seen in multiple sclerosis. COMLEX: T1 versus T2 CSF appearance is a frequently tested imaging fact for identifying sequence type.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — ventricular enlargement patterns on CT and MRI","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Interpret cross-sectional neuroimaging using anatomical landmarks.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient is placed supine in an MRI scanner for a brain study. In this position, which surface of the brainstem and spinal cord faces downward?","choices":{"A":"The anterior, or ventral, surface","B":"The posterior, or dorsal, surface","C":"The superior surface","D":"The lateral surface","E":"The medial surface"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In the supine, face up position used for standard brain and spine imaging, the posterior, or dorsal, surface of the brainstem and spinal cord and the occipital pole of the cerebral hemispheres face downward, while the anterior, or ventral, surface and the frontal pole face upward. Superior, lateral, and medial are not the terms used to describe the surface facing the table in a supine patient, since anatomic directions in the CNS are defined relative to the neuraxis rather than gravity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Remembering that anatomic directions are absolute relative to the neuraxis, not to gravity or patient position, prevents misreading imaging orientation. COMLEX: Correctly orienting a supine patient's anatomy is essential before interpreting any cross-sectional image.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — ventricular enlargement patterns on CT and MRI","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q19","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Relate structural organization to functional sensory and motor systems, including somatic, visceral, general, and special categories.","batch":1,"stem":"Which functional category describes sensory fibers that carry pain, temperature, and touch information from the skin and skeletal muscle?","choices":{"A":"General somatic afferent","B":"General visceral afferent","C":"Special somatic afferent","D":"Special visceral afferent","E":"General visceral efferent"},"correct":"A","explanation":"General somatic afferent fibers carry pain, temperature, touch, and proprioceptive information from the skin, skeletal muscle, and joints, and are considered general because the sensation is widely distributed and somatic because it arises from the body wall rather than internal organs. General visceral afferent fibers instead carry sensory information from internal organs. Special somatic and special visceral afferent fibers carry specialized sensory modalities such as vision, hearing, taste, and smell rather than general body wall sensation. General visceral efferent fibers are motor, not sensory, and innervate smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, and glands.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The functional component categories are essential for predicting which cranial nerve nuclei are affected by a given brainstem lesion. COMLEX: These categories also explain why a single cranial nerve can carry multiple, distinct types of information.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 2 — cranial nerve functional component categories","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q20","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Relate structural organization to functional sensory and motor systems, including somatic, visceral, general, and special categories.","batch":1,"stem":"Which functional category best describes motor fibers that innervate smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, and glandular epithelium?","choices":{"A":"General somatic efferent","B":"Special visceral efferent","C":"General visceral efferent","D":"General visceral afferent","E":"Special somatic afferent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"General visceral efferent fibers are the motor limb of the autonomic nervous system and innervate smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, and glandular epithelium. General somatic efferent fibers instead innervate skeletal muscle derived from somites. Special visceral efferent fibers innervate skeletal muscle derived from the branchial or pharyngeal arches, such as the muscles of mastication and facial expression. General visceral afferent and special somatic afferent fibers are sensory categories, not motor categories.","boardPrep":"Step 1: General visceral efferent fibers form the preganglionic limb of both sympathetic and parasympathetic outflow. COMLEX: Recognizing GVE fiber distribution helps explain autonomic symptoms in brainstem and spinal cord lesions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 2 — cranial nerve functional component categories","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q21","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Relate structural organization to functional sensory and motor systems, including somatic, visceral, general, and special categories.","batch":1,"stem":"Taste sensation from the tongue is classified under which functional sensory category?","choices":{"A":"General visceral afferent","B":"Special visceral afferent","C":"General somatic afferent","D":"Special somatic afferent","E":"General visceral efferent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Taste is classified as special visceral afferent because it is a specialized sensory modality, making it special, and it is carried by fibers associated with structures of visceral, or pharyngeal arch, embryologic origin. General visceral afferent fibers instead carry non-specialized sensations such as stretch, nausea, and distension from internal organs. General somatic afferent carries body wall pain, temperature, and touch, and special somatic afferent carries vision and hearing. General visceral efferent is a motor category, not a sensory one.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Taste fibers as special visceral afferent is a classic board fact tested alongside the specific cranial nerves, VII, IX, and X, that carry taste. COMLEX: This classification explains why taste loss can localize a lesion to the chorda tympani, glossopharyngeal, or vagus nerve.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 2 — cranial nerve functional component categories","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q22","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define neuroanatomical directional terms and planes of section.","batch":2,"stem":"A neurosurgery resident planning a posterior fossa approach wants a single MRI image that shows the corpus callosum, the cerebral aqueduct, the fourth ventricle, and the full rostrocaudal length of the brainstem together in one view. Which imaging plane should be requested?","choices":{"A":"An axial plane at the level of the bodies of the lateral ventricles","B":"A coronal plane passing through the mammillary bodies","C":"A midsagittal plane from the frontal pole to the occipital pole","D":"A parasagittal plane passing through the insula and lentiform nucleus","E":"An oblique axial plane angled along the floor of the orbits"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A sagittal image is made at, or parallel to, the midsagittal plane, which runs through the midline of the head from rostral to caudal along the frontal-to-occipital axis. Cut exactly at the midline, it displays the unpaired midline structures in continuity: the corpus callosum, third ventricle, cerebral aqueduct, fourth ventricle, and the whole brainstem from midbrain to medulla. An axial slice at the level of the lateral ventricle bodies shows only forebrain structures, and lower axial slices cut the brainstem in cross-section, one level at a time. A coronal slice through the mammillary bodies is perpendicular to the forebrain axis and shows only a short, longitudinally cut piece of brainstem. A parasagittal slice through the insula lies far lateral to every midline structure listed, and an oblique axial slice along the orbital floor still crosses the brainstem at a single level.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know which structures each plane shows: midsagittal for midline structures in continuity (corpus callosum, aqueduct, fourth ventricle, brainstem), axial for brainstem cross-sections, coronal for forebrain cross-sections. COMLEX: Midsagittal MRI is the standard view for judging Chiari malformation, aqueductal stenosis, and posterior fossa masses because the whole midline pathway appears on one image.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — Ventricular system components and their neuroanatomic relationships, seen together on a midsagittal image","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q23","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define neuroanatomical directional terms and planes of section.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has a lesion localized to the midbrain based on his neurologic examination. His physician describes the lesion as involving structures that are rostral to the pons and caudal to the diencephalon. Which of the following directional relationships is being used correctly in this description?","choices":{"A":"Rostral and caudal are used to describe relative position along the coronal imaging plane","B":"Rostral and caudal are used to describe relative position along the long axis of the neuraxis","C":"Rostral and caudal are used interchangeably with medial and lateral in brainstem anatomy","D":"Rostral and caudal describe distance from the ventricular system rather than axial position","E":"Rostral and caudal describe the depth of a structure from the pial surface"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Rostral and caudal describe the relative position of a structure along the long, rostrocaudal axis of the neuraxis, toward the nose or toward the tail respectively; using the midbrain as the reference point, it is rostral to the pons and caudal to the diencephalon along this axis. These terms are not defined by the coronal plane, are distinct from medial and lateral, and do not describe distance from the ventricular system or depth from the pial surface, which are unrelated anatomic relationships.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Correctly applying rostral and caudal to brainstem structures is required to interpret sagittal MRI descriptions in vignette stems. COMLEX: Precise directional language is expected when describing lesion location in a written neurologic assessment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — Midbrain tectum, tegmentum, and basis pedunculi organization","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q24","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define neuroanatomical directional terms and planes of section.","batch":2,"stem":"A neuroradiologist reviewing a sagittal MRI describes the third ventricle as lying posterior to the anterior commissure and anterior to the pineal gland, using the diencephalon as the frame of reference. Which principle of directional terminology is illustrated by this description?","choices":{"A":"Directional terms only apply when the brain is sectioned in the axial plane","B":"Directional terms require the cerebellum to be used as the fixed reference point","C":"Directional terms are only valid when applied to spinal cord structures","D":"Directional terms are relative to a chosen reference structure along the neuraxis","E":"Directional terms change meaning depending on whether the patient is supine or prone"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Directional terms such as anterior and posterior describe the relative position of structures to one another along the neuraxis, and any structure, such as the diencephalon in this example, can serve as the reference point from which nearby structures are described. These terms remain valid regardless of imaging plane, are not tied to the cerebellum as a mandatory reference, apply throughout the CNS including the spinal cord and forebrain, and do not change meaning based on patient position because they are defined relative to the neuraxis, not gravity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Choosing an appropriate reference structure is a routine part of describing diencephalic and third ventricular anatomy on imaging. COMLEX: Accurate directional description of periventricular structures supports correct localization of hypothalamic and thalamic lesions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 1 — Diencephalon boundaries relative to the third ventricle","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q25","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguish the central and peripheral nervous system organization.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman presents with sudden severe headache. Lumbar puncture reveals xanthochromic cerebrospinal fluid, confirming a diagnosis of subarachnoid hemorrhage. The bleeding is described as occurring within a space bounded by two of the three meningeal layers unique to the central nervous system covering. Which structures form the boundaries of this space?","choices":{"A":"The dura mater and arachnoid mater","B":"The periosteum and dura mater","C":"The arachnoid mater and pia mater","D":"The pia mater and the spinal cord parenchyma","E":"The epineurium and perineurium of the spinal nerve"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The subarachnoid space, where cerebrospinal fluid circulates and where subarachnoid hemorrhage occurs, lies between the arachnoid mater and the pia mater, both meningeal layers covering the central nervous system. The dura mater and arachnoid mater instead border the subdural space, and the periosteum and dura mater border the epidural space in the cranium. The epineurium and perineurium are connective tissue coverings of peripheral nerves, part of the PNS rather than the CNS meningeal system.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Xanthochromic CSF from lysed red blood cells is the classic confirmatory finding for subarachnoid hemorrhage when CT is negative. COMLEX: Understanding meningeal layer anatomy is required to correctly interpret CSF findings on lumbar puncture.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — Dura, arachnoid, and pia mater as CNS-specific meningeal layers","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q26","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguish the central and peripheral nervous system organization.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man undergoes epidural anesthesia for a lumbar spine procedure. The anesthesiologist explains that, unlike the cranial epidural space, the spinal epidural space is a true, fat-filled space that can be readily accessed with a needle. Which structural difference between the CNS coverings at these two levels best explains this distinction?","choices":{"A":"In the spine, the arachnoid mater is fused to the vertebral periosteum, whereas in the skull it is a separate layer","B":"In the spine, the pia mater is absent, whereas in the skull it fully covers the brain","C":"In the spine, the subarachnoid space does not contain cerebrospinal fluid, whereas in the skull it does","D":"In the spine, a true epidural space separates the dura from the periosteum, whereas in the skull the two are fused","E":"In the spine, there is no dura mater present, whereas in the skull the dura is a distinct layer"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cranially, the dura mater is fused to the inner periosteum of the skull, so there is no true epidural space under normal conditions, and epidural hematomas there occur only after the dura is stripped away from bone by arterial bleeding. In the vertebral canal, the dura mater is separated from the vertebral periosteum by a true, fat-filled epidural space that anesthesiologists can access directly for epidural anesthesia. The arachnoid and pia mater are present at both levels with the same relationships, and the subarachnoid space contains cerebrospinal fluid throughout the CNS.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The presence of a true spinal epidural space but not a true cranial epidural space is a classic anatomic distinction tested on meningeal anatomy questions. COMLEX: This anatomy directly underlies the safety and technique of epidural anesthesia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — Cranial versus spinal epidural space anatomy","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q27","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguish the central and peripheral nervous system organization.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy is found to have a right-sided lesion following head trauma, and examination reveals loss of pain and temperature sensation on the left side of his body but preserved strength. His physician is attempting to determine whether the responsible lesion is within the central nervous system or the peripheral nervous system. Which anatomic fact would most directly support a central nervous system localization?","choices":{"A":"The lesion involves CNS tracts myelinated by oligodendrocytes, not Schwann cells","B":"The lesion involves a mixed nerve containing both sensory and motor fibers","C":"The lesion involves a dorsal root ganglion containing pseudounipolar neurons","D":"The lesion involves an autonomic ganglion outside the vertebral column","E":"The lesion involves a peripheral nerve plexus formed by multiple spinal nerve roots"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Central nervous system white matter tracts are myelinated by oligodendrocytes, whereas peripheral nervous system axons are myelinated by Schwann cells, so identifying oligodendrocyte-derived myelin on a lesion supports CNS localization. Mixed nerves, dorsal root ganglia, autonomic ganglia, and peripheral nerve plexuses are all peripheral nervous system structures and would instead support a PNS localization for the lesion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Oligodendrocytes versus Schwann cells is a classic distinguishing fact between CNS and PNS myelination, relevant to diseases such as multiple sclerosis versus Guillain-Barre syndrome. COMLEX: This distinction helps differentiate central from peripheral causes of sensory loss on exam.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 1 — Cranial nerve brainstem levels and the CN XI spinal accessory exception","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q28","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the five primary and secondary brain vesicles and their adult derivatives.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-day-old neonate is found to have absence of the cerebral hemispheres and basal ganglia on prenatal imaging, with preservation of brainstem structures. This pattern is most consistent with failed development of which embryonic vesicle?","choices":{"A":"The diencephalon","B":"The telencephalon","C":"The mesencephalon","D":"The myelencephalon","E":"The metencephalon"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The telencephalon is the secondary vesicle that gives rise to the cerebral hemispheres and most of the basal nuclei, so failed development of the telencephalon would selectively eliminate the cerebral hemispheres while sparing brainstem structures. The diencephalon gives rise to the thalamus and hypothalamus, the mesencephalon gives rise to the midbrain, and the metencephalon and myelencephalon give rise to the pons, cerebellum, and medulla respectively, all brainstem or hindbrain-related structures preserved in this case.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Severe telencephalic maldevelopment is seen in conditions such as holoprosencephaly, often associated with midline facial anomalies. COMLEX: Recognizing which vesicle corresponds to absent structures on prenatal imaging aids in prognostic counseling.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 8 — Neural tube cavity derivatives forming the adult ventricular system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q29","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the five primary and secondary brain vesicles and their adult derivatives.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with obstructive hydrocephalus is found to have dilation of both lateral ventricles and the third ventricle, with a normal-sized fourth ventricle. The obstruction is most likely located within a structure derived from which embryonic vesicle?","choices":{"A":"The telencephalon, at the level of the lateral ventricle","B":"The diencephalon, at the level of the third ventricle","C":"The myelencephalon, at the level of the fourth ventricle","D":"The metencephalon, at the level of the fourth ventricle","E":"The mesencephalon, at the level of the cerebral aqueduct"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Dilation of both lateral ventricles and the third ventricle with a normal fourth ventricle indicates obstruction at the cerebral aqueduct, which lies within the midbrain and is derived from the mesencephalon. If the obstruction were within the lateral or third ventricles themselves, those structures would be directly involved by the mass or defect rather than passively dilated. Obstruction at the fourth ventricle, derived from the myelencephalon and metencephalon, would instead cause dilation of all four ventricular spaces including the fourth ventricle.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Aqueductal stenosis is a classic cause of noncommunicating hydrocephalus with a characteristic ventricular dilation pattern sparing the fourth ventricle. COMLEX: This vesicle-derivative reasoning is directly tested when correlating imaging patterns with the site of CSF obstruction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — Neural tube, neural crest, and surface ectoderm derivatives","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q30","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the five primary and secondary brain vesicles and their adult derivatives.","batch":2,"stem":"A genetic counselor explains to a family that a fetal anomaly affecting the pineal gland and hypothalamus, with sparing of the cerebral hemispheres, localizes to abnormal development of a single embryonic vesicle. Which vesicle is most consistent with this pattern?","choices":{"A":"The telencephalon","B":"The mesencephalon","C":"The diencephalon","D":"The metencephalon","E":"The myelencephalon"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The diencephalon is the secondary vesicle that gives rise to the thalamus, hypothalamus, and pineal gland, so an anomaly selectively affecting the pineal gland and hypothalamus while sparing the cerebral hemispheres is most consistent with abnormal diencephalic development. The telencephalon instead gives rise to the cerebral hemispheres, which are described as spared, and the mesencephalon, metencephalon, and myelencephalon give rise to midbrain, pontocerebellar, and medullary structures unrelated to the pineal gland or hypothalamus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pineal region tumors, such as pineoblastoma and germinoma, arise from diencephalic derivatives and can cause Parinaud syndrome by compressing adjacent midbrain structures. COMLEX: Diencephalic derivative knowledge supports localization of hypothalamic-pituitary axis disorders.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 1 — Diencephalon subdivisions and their relation to the third ventricle","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q31","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate gray matter and white matter structurally and functionally.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man develops progressive weakness after a spinal cord infarct. Imaging shows a lesion confined to a butterfly-shaped region in the center of the spinal cord on axial section, sparing the surrounding peripheral rim. Which type of tissue is most likely damaged by this centrally located lesion?","choices":{"A":"White matter, containing ascending sensory tracts","B":"White matter, containing descending motor tracts","C":"The dural sac surrounding the spinal cord","D":"Gray matter, containing neuronal cell bodies","E":"The dorsal root ganglia adjacent to the spinal cord"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In the spinal cord, gray matter forms a central, butterfly-shaped region containing the neuronal cell bodies of the anterior and posterior horns, while white matter forms the surrounding peripheral rim containing ascending and descending tracts. A lesion confined to the central butterfly-shaped region therefore damages gray matter rather than the peripheral white matter tracts. The dural sac and dorsal root ganglia are structures outside the spinal cord parenchyma itself and would not appear as part of this central lesion pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The central, gray matter distribution described here is classic for conditions such as syringomyelia and anterior spinal artery infarction affecting the central cord. COMLEX: Recognizing gray versus white matter distribution on axial cord imaging is essential for localizing cord syndromes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — Spinal cord gray matter horns versus white matter columns","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q32","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate gray matter and white matter structurally and functionally.","batch":2,"stem":"A neuropathologist examines a stroke specimen and identifies a region where neuronal cell bodies predominate, organized in six distinct laminae, with dense input from thalamocortical afferents concentrated in one particular layer. Which broad category of tissue is being examined?","choices":{"A":"Gray matter, specifically the layered cerebral cortex","B":"White matter, specifically the corona radiata","C":"White matter, specifically the internal capsule","D":"Gray matter, specifically the internal capsule","E":"White matter, specifically the corpus callosum"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The tissue described, organized into six distinct neuronal laminae with thalamocortical input concentrated in a specific layer, corresponds to the layered cerebral cortex, which is gray matter composed of neuronal cell bodies. The corona radiata, internal capsule, and corpus callosum are all white matter structures composed of myelinated axons rather than layered neuronal cell bodies, and the internal capsule is never classified as gray matter.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Layer IV of the cerebral cortex, receiving dense thalamocortical input, is a frequently tested detail in cortical lamination questions. COMLEX: Understanding cortical gray matter organization supports interpretation of cortical stroke and seizure localization.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — telencephalic components, including the six-layered neocortex and the input/output roles of layers IV, V, VI","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q33","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate gray matter and white matter structurally and functionally.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient develops a small lacunar infarct within the posterior limb of the internal capsule, resulting in pure motor hemiparesis without cortical sensory or language deficits. Which structural feature of the internal capsule best explains why a single small lesion here produces such a dense deficit?","choices":{"A":"The internal capsule contains a dense concentration of cortical gray matter neuronal cell bodies","B":"The internal capsule densely concentrates corticospinal and other projection fibers into a compact white matter bundle","C":"The internal capsule is composed primarily of association fibers connecting adjacent gyri","D":"The internal capsule is composed primarily of commissural fibers connecting the two hemispheres","E":"The internal capsule contains the primary somatosensory and language cortex"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The internal capsule is a compact white matter structure that concentrates projection fibers, including corticospinal, corticobulbar, and thalamocortical fibers, into a small anatomic space as they travel between the cerebral cortex and lower brain regions. Because these fibers are so tightly packed, even a small lacunar infarct can damage a disproportionately large number of fibers, producing dense, pure motor or pure sensory deficits without cortical signs. The internal capsule does not contain cortical gray matter, association fibers, commissural fibers, or the cortex itself, which are separate structures elsewhere in the hemisphere.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pure motor hemiparesis from a lacunar stroke in the posterior limb of the internal capsule is a classic vascular neurology vignette. COMLEX: This fiber-concentration concept explains why small subcortical infarcts can produce disproportionately severe deficits.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — Association, commissural, and projection white matter fiber classification","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q34","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply anatomical terminology to clinical lesion localization, including ipsilateral and contralateral deficits.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old man presents with left facial weakness involving the forehead and eye closure, and right-sided arm and leg weakness. His physician suspects a single lesion explains both findings. Which localization principle is most consistent with this crossed pattern?","choices":{"A":"The lesion is likely in the brainstem, where cranial nuclei sit beside uncrossed motor tracts","B":"The lesion is likely in the cerebral cortex, where all descending motor fibers remain uncrossed","C":"The lesion is likely in the spinal cord, where cranial nerve fibers do not travel","D":"The lesion is likely in a peripheral nerve plexus affecting both sides simultaneously","E":"The lesion is likely at the neuromuscular junction, producing bilateral symmetric weakness"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A crossed, or alternating, pattern with cranial nerve findings on one side and body findings on the opposite side is the hallmark of a brainstem lesion. Here the lesion damages the left facial nucleus or its exiting fibers, producing an ipsilateral lower motor neuron palsy of the whole hemiface, together with the adjacent corticospinal tract, which has not yet decussated and therefore produces contralateral body weakness. A cortical lesion affects face and body on the same side, spinal cord lesions do not produce cranial nerve findings, and peripheral nerve plexus or neuromuscular junction disease does not produce a crossed hemibody pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: This crossed facial and body pattern is classic for a caudal pontine lesion involving the facial nucleus or fascicles and the adjacent corticospinal tract. COMLEX: Recognizing crossed findings quickly narrows localization to the brainstem in a timed vignette.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 6 — Medial versus lateral medullary syndrome crossed deficit patterns","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q35","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply anatomical terminology to clinical lesion localization, including ipsilateral and contralateral deficits.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman sustains a penetrating injury to the right half of the spinal cord at the T6 level. On examination two weeks later, she has loss of vibration and position sense and weakness below T6 on the right, with loss of pain and temperature sensation beginning a few levels below T6 on the left. Which localization principle best explains the side of the pain and temperature loss?","choices":{"A":"Dorsal column fibers cross near their level of entry, so contralateral vibration loss occurs below a hemicord lesion","B":"Corticospinal fibers cross near their level of entry, producing contralateral weakness below a hemicord lesion","C":"Spinothalamic fibers remain uncrossed throughout the spinal cord, producing ipsilateral pain and temperature loss","D":"Dorsal column fibers remain uncrossed throughout the spinal cord, producing ipsilateral vibration loss","E":"Spinothalamic fibers cross near their level of entry, so contralateral pain and temperature loss occurs below a hemicord lesion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This presentation describes Brown-Sequard syndrome, a hemicord lesion in which the dorsal columns and corticospinal tract, already crossed at higher levels or uncrossed until far above the lesion, produce ipsilateral loss of vibration, position sense, and motor strength below the lesion, while spinothalamic tract fibers, which cross near their level of entry into the spinal cord via the anterior white commissure, produce contralateral loss of pain and temperature sensation a few levels below the lesion. The dorsal column and corticospinal fibers do not cross at the spinal cord level, so their deficits remain ipsilateral, consistent with the findings on the right in this vignette.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Brown-Sequard syndrome is one of the most heavily tested spinal cord syndromes, testing the different crossing points of the dorsal column and spinothalamic pathways. COMLEX: This ipsilateral versus contralateral pattern is essential for correctly localizing hemicord injuries on exam.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 30 Obj 6 — Brown-Sequard syndrome ipsilateral and contralateral deficit pattern","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q36","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply anatomical terminology to clinical lesion localization, including ipsilateral and contralateral deficits.","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old woman presents with sudden right arm and leg weakness and numbness that is worse in the arm than the leg, along with expressive language difficulty. Which vascular territory and hemisphere localization is most consistent with these findings?","choices":{"A":"Right middle cerebral artery territory, producing contralateral arm-predominant deficits and neglect","B":"Left anterior cerebral artery territory, producing contralateral leg-predominant deficits","C":"Left middle cerebral artery territory, producing contralateral arm-predominant deficits and language impairment","D":"Left posterior cerebral artery territory, producing contralateral visual field loss","E":"Right posterior inferior cerebellar artery territory, producing ipsilateral limb ataxia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Because the corticospinal and corticobulbar fibers cross before or at the level of the spinal cord, right-sided body deficits localize the lesion to the left hemisphere, and arm-predominant weakness with expressive language difficulty is characteristic of left middle cerebral artery territory infarction involving the lateral cortex and Broca's area. A right middle cerebral artery infarct would produce left-sided deficits with neglect rather than language impairment in most right-handed patients. Anterior cerebral artery infarcts produce leg-predominant deficits, posterior cerebral artery infarcts produce visual field loss, and posterior inferior cerebellar artery infarcts produce ipsilateral cerebellar and lower brainstem signs rather than contralateral hemiparesis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Arm-predominant weakness with aphasia localizing to left MCA territory is one of the most frequently tested stroke localization patterns. COMLEX: Applying contralateral body, ipsilateral hemisphere localization is fundamental to the stroke localization algorithm.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — MCA, ACA, and PCA territory deficits used for stroke localization","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q37","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Interpret cross-sectional neuroimaging using anatomical landmarks.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old right-handed man has sudden weakness of the right face and arm and difficulty finding words. A medical student reviewing his noncontrast axial head CT sees the hypodense infarct on the right side of the displayed image and asks whether the scan belongs to another patient. Which of the following best resolves the student's concern?","choices":{"A":"Language is lateralized to the right hemisphere in most right-handed adults","B":"Corticospinal fibers stay uncrossed until the cervical cord, so the lesion is ipsilateral","C":"Coronal images reverse left and right, whereas axial images keep true orientation","D":"Acute infarcts project to the side opposite their true location from beam hardening","E":"Axial images are viewed from the patient's feet, so image right is the patient's left"},"correct":"E","explanation":"By convention, axial CT and MRI images are viewed as though the clinician stands at the patient's feet looking toward the head, and coronal images as though facing the patient; in both views the patient's left side lies on the observer's right. A left middle cerebral artery infarct causing right face and arm weakness with word-finding difficulty therefore appears on the right of the displayed image, which is expected and not a labeling error. Language is lateralized to the left hemisphere in nearly all right-handed people. Corticospinal fibers cross at the pyramidal decussation in the caudal medulla, so a hemispheric lesion causes contralateral weakness. The left-right convention is the same for coronal and axial images, not reversed between them. No CT artifact relocates an infarct to the opposite hemisphere.","boardPrep":"Step 1: On axial and coronal CT and MRI, the patient's left is on the viewer's right; image questions often hinge on reading laterality correctly. COMLEX: Confirm laterality on the image against the examination before any intervention, because a misread side can lead to a wrong-side procedure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — MCA territory deficits whose hemisphere must be matched to the side shown on the image","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q38","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Interpret cross-sectional neuroimaging using anatomical landmarks.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after a motorcycle collision with brief loss of consciousness followed by a lucid interval and subsequent rapid neurologic decline. Noncontrast CT shows a lens-shaped, hyperdense collection that does not cross cranial suture lines. Which anatomic space contains this collection?","choices":{"A":"The subdural space, between the dura mater and the arachnoid mater","B":"The epidural space, between the dura mater and the inner skull","C":"The subarachnoid space, between the arachnoid mater and the pia mater","D":"The intraparenchymal space, within the brain substance itself","E":"The intraventricular space, within the ventricular system"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A lens-shaped, or biconvex, hyperdense collection that does not cross suture lines, following a lucid interval after trauma, is characteristic of an epidural hematoma located between the dura mater and the inner table of the skull, classically from injury to the middle meningeal artery; the tight dural attachment at sutures limits its spread there. Subdural hematomas instead appear crescent-shaped and can cross suture lines because the dura is not tightly attached to the arachnoid. Subarachnoid hemorrhage appears within the sulci and cisterns rather than as a lentiform collection, and intraparenchymal or intraventricular hemorrhage occurs within the brain substance or ventricles rather than in an extra-axial space.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The lucid interval followed by rapid decline with a biconvex, suture-limited hematoma is the classic epidural hematoma presentation tied to middle meningeal artery injury. COMLEX: Distinguishing epidural from subdural hematoma shape on CT is a frequently tested imaging skill.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 5 — Epidural, subdural, and subarachnoid hemorrhage imaging distinctions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q39","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Interpret cross-sectional neuroimaging using anatomical landmarks.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with progressive headache and new left-sided weakness undergoes contrast-enhanced brain MRI, which shows a single, well-circumscribed, extra-axial mass along the falx cerebri with dense, uniform contrast enhancement and a broad dural attachment. Which imaging characteristic most strongly supports the most likely diagnosis of meningioma over glioblastoma?","choices":{"A":"The extra-axial location with a broad dural attachment, rather than a location within the brain parenchyma","B":"The presence of uniform contrast enhancement, which is unique to meningioma among intracranial masses","C":"The presence of surrounding vasogenic edema, which is unique to meningioma among intracranial masses","D":"The single lesion pattern, which is unique to meningioma among intracranial masses","E":"The patient age, which is diagnostic for meningioma regardless of imaging appearance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Meningiomas are extra-axial tumors that arise from the meninges and characteristically show a broad dural attachment along structures such as the falx cerebri, distinguishing them from glioblastoma, an intra-axial tumor arising within the brain parenchyma itself. Uniform contrast enhancement, surrounding edema, and a solitary lesion pattern can all be seen in either meningioma or glioblastoma and are not unique to one diagnosis, and patient age alone is not diagnostic without correlating imaging location and pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Extra-axial location with dural attachment is the single most important imaging feature separating meningioma from intra-axial tumors like glioblastoma. COMLEX: This location-based reasoning is directly applied when selecting the most likely diagnosis in a tumor imaging vignette.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 48 Obj 5 — Characteristic imaging findings of major adult intracranial tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q40","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Relate structural organization to functional sensory and motor systems, including somatic, visceral, general, and special categories.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman has progressive right-sided hearing loss and unsteadiness. MRI shows a mass in the right internal acoustic meatus compressing the vestibulocochlear nerve. In the traditional functional classification of cranial nerve fibers, which category describes the fibers carrying her hearing and balance signals?","choices":{"A":"General somatic afferent","B":"Special visceral afferent","C":"General visceral afferent","D":"Special somatic afferent","E":"Special visceral efferent"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In the traditional scheme, the auditory and vestibular fibers of the eighth cranial nerve are special somatic afferent fibers, the category reserved for the special senses of hearing, balance, and vision; the vestibular part enters the brainstem and supplies the four vestibular nuclei. General somatic afferent fibers carry pain, temperature, touch, and proprioception from skin, muscle, and joints, as in the trigeminal nerve. Special visceral afferent fibers carry taste and smell, and general visceral afferent fibers carry unconscious visceral input such as carotid sinus baroreceptor signals. Special visceral efferent, or branchial motor, fibers are motor axons to pharyngeal arch muscles and carry no sensory signal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Learn the special categories as a set: special somatic afferent for hearing, balance, and vision; special visceral afferent for taste and smell; special visceral efferent for pharyngeal arch muscles. COMLEX: Unilateral sensorineural hearing loss with imbalance points to the eighth nerve at the internal acoustic meatus and calls for MRI to look for a vestibular schwannoma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 2 — Cranial nerve functional component categories","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q41","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Relate structural organization to functional sensory and motor systems, including somatic, visceral, general, and special categories.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man with long-standing hypertension develops orthostatic hypotension, and testing reveals impaired baroreceptor reflex signaling from the carotid sinus to the brainstem. Which functional pathway is primarily responsible for carrying this baroreceptor information centrally?","choices":{"A":"General visceral afferent fibers of the glossopharyngeal nerve synapsing in the nucleus solitarius","B":"General somatic afferent fibers of the glossopharyngeal nerve synapsing in the spinal trigeminal nucleus","C":"Special visceral afferent fibers of the vagus nerve synapsing in the nucleus ambiguus","D":"General visceral efferent fibers of the glossopharyngeal nerve synapsing in the inferior salivatory nucleus","E":"Special somatic afferent fibers of the vagus nerve synapsing in the vestibular nuclei"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Baroreceptor information from the carotid sinus travels centrally via general visceral afferent fibers of the glossopharyngeal nerve, which synapse in the nucleus solitarius, the primary visceral sensory relay of the brainstem that integrates baroreceptor and chemoreceptor input for cardiovascular and respiratory reflexes. General somatic afferent fibers of the glossopharyngeal nerve instead carry a small component of external ear sensation to the spinal trigeminal nucleus. Special visceral afferent fibers of the vagus nerve carry taste from the epiglottis, and general visceral efferent and special somatic afferent categories describe motor and completely different sensory modalities, not baroreceptor signaling.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The nucleus solitarius as the central relay for baroreceptor and chemoreceptor afferents is a high-yield autonomic reflex fact tested with carotid sinus massage and orthostatic hypotension vignettes. COMLEX: Understanding this pathway supports evaluation of autonomic dysfunction in cardiovascular disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 1 — Visceral afferent pathways distinguished from autonomic efferent pathways","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q42","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Relate structural organization to functional sensory and motor systems, including somatic, visceral, general, and special categories.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy with poliovirus infection develops asymmetric flaccid paralysis with muscle atrophy and fasciculations, but sensation remains completely intact. Which functional fiber category is selectively destroyed by this infection?","choices":{"A":"General somatic afferent fibers of the dorsal root ganglia","B":"General visceral efferent fibers of the intermediolateral cell column","C":"Special visceral efferent fibers of the brainstem branchial motor nuclei","D":"General visceral afferent fibers of the dorsal root ganglia","E":"General somatic efferent fibers of the anterior horn cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Poliovirus selectively infects and destroys the anterior horn cells of the spinal cord, which contain general somatic efferent motor neurons innervating skeletal muscle, producing flaccid paralysis, atrophy, and fasciculations with completely spared sensation because sensory pathways are not affected. General somatic afferent and general visceral afferent fibers of the dorsal root ganglia carry sensation, which remains intact in this patient. General visceral efferent fibers of the intermediolateral cell column provide autonomic innervation, and special visceral efferent fibers innervate branchial arch-derived muscles in the brainstem, neither of which is the primary target of poliovirus in the spinal cord.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Poliovirus selectively destroying anterior horn general somatic efferent neurons while sparing sensation is a classic lower motor neuron disease vignette. COMLEX: This pure motor, flaccid, areflexic pattern with intact sensation localizes disease specifically to the anterior horn cell.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — Anterior horn cell as the lower motor neuron final common pathway","isHighYield":false}],"2":[{"id":"sdl02_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain how ion concentration gradients and membrane permeability generate the resting membrane potential.","batch":1,"stem":"Which combination of factors is primarily responsible for establishing the neuronal resting membrane potential near -70 mV?","choices":{"A":"High resting Na+ permeability through voltage-gated Na+ channels combined with the inward Na+ concentration gradient","B":"High resting K+ permeability through K2P leak channels combined with the outward K+ concentration gradient","C":"Equal permeability to Na+, K+, and Cl- combined with active extrusion of Ca2+ from the cell","D":"High resting Cl- permeability through ligand-gated channels combined with the outward Cl- concentration gradient","E":"Continuous ATP hydrolysis by the Na+/K+ ATPase acting alone, independent of any resting ion permeability"},"correct":"B","explanation":"K2P leak channels are constitutively open and dominate resting permeability, so K+ diffuses down its steep outward concentration gradient, leaving impermeant intracellular anions behind and generating a negative interior. This diffusion continues until the resulting electrical force nearly balances the chemical force, setting the resting potential close to the K+ equilibrium potential. Na+ permeability at rest is comparatively low, so it contributes only a small depolarizing pull. Cl- is often near equilibrium and the pump contributes only a modest additional hyperpolarization rather than establishing the potential outright.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The resting membrane potential sits close to the K+ equilibrium potential, not the Na+ equilibrium potential, because resting K+ permeability vastly exceeds resting Na+ permeability. COMLEX: Understanding which ion dominates resting permeability is foundational for predicting how electrolyte disturbances shift nerve and muscle excitability.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — EPSPs and IPSPs as graded shifts riding on the neuron's resting ionic gradients","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain how ion concentration gradients and membrane permeability generate the resting membrane potential.","batch":1,"stem":"A neuron's resting membrane is far more permeable to K+ than to Na+. What is the direct electrochemical consequence of this selective permeability on intracellular charge?","choices":{"A":"K+ diffuses down its concentration gradient out of the cell, leaving behind impermeant intracellular anions and generating a net negative internal charge","B":"Na+ diffuses down its concentration gradient into the cell, leaving behind impermeant intracellular anions and generating a net negative internal charge","C":"K+ is actively pumped into the cell against its concentration gradient, generating a net positive internal charge","D":"Cl- diffuses down its concentration gradient into the cell, leaving behind impermeant intracellular cations and generating a net positive internal charge","E":"Impermeant intracellular proteins are actively extruded from the cell, generating a net negative internal charge"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because K+ permeability dominates, K+ ions passively diffuse out of the cell along their concentration gradient. This efflux of positive charge leaves the large, impermeant intracellular anionic proteins uncompensated, producing a net negative charge inside the cell relative to outside. Na+ permeability is comparatively low, so Na+ influx does not drive the resting potential. The impermeant proteins themselves never leave the cell; they simply remain behind as the source of fixed negative charge.","boardPrep":"Step 1: This K+ efflux mechanism is the textbook explanation for why the resting membrane potential is negative and why hyperkalemia depolarizes cells. COMLEX: This concept underlies interpretation of any vignette involving abnormal serum potassium and neuromuscular symptoms.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — EPSPs and IPSPs as graded shifts riding on the neuron's resting ionic gradients","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain how ion concentration gradients and membrane permeability generate the resting membrane potential.","batch":1,"stem":"Impermeant intracellular protein anions contribute to the resting membrane potential primarily through which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"They actively transport K+ out of the cell using ATP hydrolysis to maintain electroneutrality","B":"They bind directly to Na+ channels, holding the channels closed at rest to maintain electroneutrality","C":"They diffuse freely across the membrane, equally distributing negative charge between the intracellular and extracellular compartments","D":"They cannot cross the membrane, so their fixed negative charge requires offsetting K+ efflux to maintain electroneutrality","E":"They catalyze the conversion of intracellular Na+ into K+ to maintain electroneutrality"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Intracellular proteins are large, negatively charged, and unable to cross the lipid bilayer, so they constitute a fixed pool of impermeant anionic charge. To approach electroneutrality, permeant cations such as K+ must redistribute, with K+ efflux along its concentration gradient leaving the cell interior negative relative to the exterior. Proteins do not actively transport ions, do not bind and gate Na+ channels as part of this mechanism, and cannot diffuse across the membrane themselves.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Impermeant intracellular anions are the classic explanation for why cells would swell osmotically without active countermeasures, tying membrane potential generation to cell volume regulation. COMLEX: This principle underlies why metabolically compromised cells swell when ionic homeostasis fails.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — EPSPs and IPSPs as graded shifts riding on the neuron's resting ionic gradients","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Use the Nernst concept qualitatively to predict the direction an ion will move when channels open.","batch":1,"stem":"Using the Nernst concept, if the equilibrium potential for Na+ is +70 mV and the resting membrane potential is -70 mV, what happens immediately after voltage-gated Na+ channels open?","choices":{"A":"Na+ moves out of the cell because the membrane potential is far from the Na+ equilibrium potential, driving depolarization","B":"Na+ moves into the cell because the membrane potential is far from the Na+ equilibrium potential, driving depolarization","C":"Na+ movement stops immediately because the membrane potential already equals the Na+ equilibrium potential","D":"Na+ moves into the cell only until the membrane potential reaches 0 mV, then it reverses direction","E":"Na+ distribution is unaffected by channel opening because Na+ permeability does not alter net ion flow"},"correct":"B","explanation":"When Na+ channels open, ions move to drive the membrane potential toward that ion's Nernst equilibrium potential. Because the resting potential of -70 mV is far below the Na+ equilibrium potential of +70 mV, both the electrical and chemical gradients favor Na+ influx, producing rapid depolarization. Na+ would only stop moving once the membrane potential reached +70 mV, which does not occur in a normal action potential because other conductances intervene first. Channel opening always permits net flux when the membrane potential differs from the ion's equilibrium potential.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Predicting ion flow direction from the gap between resting potential and equilibrium potential is a core Nernst-based reasoning skill tested repeatedly on physiology questions. COMLEX: This reasoning generalizes to any electrolyte-channel vignette, including cardiac and neuromuscular presentations.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 3 — ion movements through the DCML and spinothalamic pathways predicted by the same electrochemical logic","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Use the Nernst concept qualitatively to predict the direction an ion will move when channels open.","batch":1,"stem":"The equilibrium potential for K+ is approximately -86 mV. If the resting membrane potential is -70 mV and additional K+ channels open, which direction will K+ move and what will happen to the membrane potential?","choices":{"A":"K+ will move into the cell, driving the membrane potential more positive toward 0 mV","B":"K+ will move out of the cell, driving the membrane potential more positive toward 0 mV","C":"K+ movement will cease immediately because the membrane is already near the K+ equilibrium potential","D":"K+ will move into the cell, driving the membrane potential more negative toward -86 mV","E":"K+ will move out of the cell, driving the membrane potential more negative toward -86 mV"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Because the resting potential of -70 mV is less negative than the K+ equilibrium potential of -86 mV, opening additional K+ channels allows K+ to continue diffusing out of the cell along its concentration gradient, pulling the membrane potential toward -86 mV. This produces hyperpolarization rather than depolarization. K+ influx would only occur if the membrane were more negative than -86 mV, which is not the case here, and movement does not cease simply because the two values are numerically close.","boardPrep":"Step 1: This exact reasoning explains the afterhyperpolarization following an action potential, when persistent K+ conductance briefly overshoots resting potential. COMLEX: Recognizing hyperpolarizing versus depolarizing ion movements is essential for predicting drug and toxin effects on excitability.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 3 — ion movements through the DCML and spinothalamic pathways predicted by the same electrochemical logic","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Use the Nernst concept qualitatively to predict the direction an ion will move when channels open.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the Nernst concept, an ion sitting at a membrane potential exactly equal to its own equilibrium potential will demonstrate which behavior when its channels open?","choices":{"A":"Rapid net movement into the cell, because opening channels always produces net flux regardless of driving force","B":"Rapid net movement out of the cell, because the concentration gradient always dominates the electrical gradient","C":"Oscillating movement into and out of the cell, alternating direction every millisecond","D":"No net movement, because the electrical and chemical driving forces on that ion are exactly balanced","E":"Net movement determined solely by the number of open channels, independent of the electrochemical gradient"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The equilibrium potential for an ion is, by definition, the membrane voltage at which the electrical force and the chemical concentration gradient acting on that ion are equal and opposite, producing zero net flux even when channels are open. Opening more channels increases the rate of individual ion crossings in both directions but does not create net movement when the driving force is zero. Concentration gradients do not automatically dominate electrical gradients, and channel number affects conductance, not the direction or presence of net flux at equilibrium.","boardPrep":"Step 1: This is the operational definition of an equilibrium, or Nernst, potential and is frequently tested by asking what happens at V_m = V_ion. COMLEX: This concept is essential for interpreting why chloride channels can be electrically silent when V_Cl equals V_m in certain neurons.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 3 — ion movements through the DCML and spinothalamic pathways predicted by the same electrochemical logic","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the roles of K+ leak channels, voltage-gated Na+ channels, voltage-gated K+ channels, and the Na+/K+ ATPase.","batch":1,"stem":"Which channel type is constitutively open at rest, is voltage-independent, and is chiefly responsible for establishing the resting membrane potential?","choices":{"A":"Voltage-gated Na+ channels in the closed but available state","B":"Voltage-gated K+ channels in the fully activated state","C":"Mechanosensitive Piezo channels responding to membrane stretch","D":"Ligand-gated Ca2+ channels awaiting neurotransmitter binding","E":"K+ leak channels of the K2P tandem pore domain family"},"correct":"E","explanation":"K2P tandem pore domain channels are voltage-independent and remain open at rest, giving the resting membrane its dominant K+ permeability and setting the resting potential near the K+ equilibrium potential. Voltage-gated Na+ and K+ channels are closed at rest and only activate with depolarization. Ligand-gated Ca2+ channels require neurotransmitter binding, and Piezo channels require mechanical stretch, neither of which is present under baseline resting conditions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: K2P channels are a frequently tested distractor-buster because students often default to voltage-gated channels for every resting-state question. COMLEX: Recognizing voltage-independent baseline conductance is key to distinguishing resting physiology from active signaling.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — voltage-gated Ca2+ channels as the next channel family activated once the axonal action potential invades the presynaptic terminal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the roles of K+ leak channels, voltage-gated Na+ channels, voltage-gated K+ channels, and the Na+/K+ ATPase.","batch":1,"stem":"Which channel's rapid opening in response to depolarization produces the regenerative upstroke of the action potential, followed by inactivation that helps terminate it?","choices":{"A":"Voltage-gated K+ channels","B":"Voltage-gated Na+ channels","C":"K+ leak channels","D":"Voltage-gated Ca2+ channels","E":"Ligand-gated Cl- channels"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Voltage-gated Na+ channels open rapidly upon depolarization, producing the feed-forward influx of Na+ that generates the fast upstroke of the action potential, and then transition into an inactivated state within milliseconds, contributing to the end of the upstroke. Voltage-gated K+ channels open more slowly and mediate repolarization rather than the upstroke. K+ leak channels are voltage-independent, voltage-gated Ca2+ channels dominate slower action potentials rather than fast axonal spikes, and ligand-gated Cl- channels require neurotransmitter binding.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The three-state model of the Na+ channel, closed, open, and inactivated, is essential for understanding local anesthetic action, toxin effects, and channelopathies. COMLEX: This mechanism underlies the electrophysiologic basis of nerve conduction studies used in clinical evaluation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — voltage-gated Ca2+ channels as the next channel family activated once the axonal action potential invades the presynaptic terminal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the roles of K+ leak channels, voltage-gated Na+ channels, voltage-gated K+ channels, and the Na+/K+ ATPase.","batch":1,"stem":"Which mechanism actively restores and maintains the steep transmembrane concentration gradients for Na+ and K+ required for repeated neuronal excitability?","choices":{"A":"The Na+/K+ ATPase, which uses ATP to extrude three Na+ ions and import two K+ ions per cycle","B":"Voltage-gated K+ channels, which passively return two K+ ions to the cell for every Na+ ion extruded","C":"K+ leak channels, which use facilitated diffusion to actively pump Na+ out of the cell","D":"Voltage-gated Na+ channels, which actively transport Na+ back out of the cell after inactivation","E":"Passive diffusion alone, which gradually equilibrates Na+ and K+ concentrations across the membrane"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The Na+/K+ ATPase is the primary active transporter that continuously restores the ionic gradients dissipated by channel-mediated Na+ influx and K+ efflux during repeated activity, using ATP hydrolysis to move three Na+ out and two K+ in per cycle, and is electrogenic because of this unequal exchange. Channels only permit passive, energy-independent movement down existing gradients and cannot restore them. Passive diffusion alone would eventually eliminate the gradients rather than maintain them.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The Na+/K+ ATPase is a classic target of cardiac glycosides like digoxin, which indirectly raise intracellular Ca2+ through effects on the related Na+/Ca2+ exchanger. COMLEX: Ongoing active transport explains why cellular energy failure, as in ischemia, rapidly destabilizes membrane excitability.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — voltage-gated Ca2+ channels as the next channel family activated once the axonal action potential invades the presynaptic terminal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish graded potentials from action potentials.","batch":1,"stem":"Which property distinguishes a graded potential from an action potential?","choices":{"A":"A graded potential propagates without decrement over long distances, whereas an action potential decays rapidly with distance","B":"A graded potential is generated only in myelinated axons, whereas an action potential occurs only in unmyelinated axons","C":"A graded potential requires voltage-gated Na+ channels, whereas an action potential requires only passive ion leak","D":"A graded potential's amplitude varies with stimulus strength, whereas an action potential is all-or-none at threshold","E":"A graded potential always depolarizes the membrane, whereas an action potential always hyperpolarizes the membrane"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Graded potentials, such as generator and synaptic potentials, scale continuously with stimulus intensity and decay passively with distance and time. Action potentials, by contrast, are all-or-none regenerative events whose amplitude does not vary once threshold is crossed, and they propagate without decrement along the axon. Myelination status does not define which potential type occurs, voltage-gated Na+ channels are required for action potentials rather than graded potentials, and graded potentials can be either depolarizing or hyperpolarizing depending on the underlying conductance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The all-or-none property of the action potential versus the continuously variable amplitude of graded potentials is one of the most frequently tested physiology distinctions. COMLEX: This distinction underlies how sensory intensity is encoded as action potential frequency rather than amplitude.","crossRef":"📎 Sensory deficit patterns that begin as graded receptor potentials before recruiting propagating action potentials","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish graded potentials from action potentials.","batch":1,"stem":"A receptor potential in a sensory neuron summates with additional stimuli and decays as it spreads passively from its site of origin. Which term describes this type of signal?","choices":{"A":"An action potential","B":"A graded potential","C":"A compound action potential","D":"A regenerative potential","E":"An all-or-none potential"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A graded potential, such as a receptor or generator potential, is defined by its ability to summate temporally and spatially and by its passive, decremental spread away from its origin. Action potentials, compound action potentials, and other regenerative or all-or-none potentials do not decay with distance because they are actively propagated rather than passively spread. Summation and passive decay are the two hallmark features that identify a graded potential in a vignette or description.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Recognizing the descriptive language of summation and decay as markers of a graded potential is a common way boards test this concept indirectly. COMLEX: Sensory receptor function in the physical exam, such as vibratory sense testing, depends on generator potentials reaching threshold.","crossRef":"📎 Sensory deficit patterns that begin as graded receptor potentials before recruiting propagating action potentials","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish graded potentials from action potentials.","batch":1,"stem":"What allows an action potential, unlike a graded potential, to travel the length of a meter-long axon without losing amplitude?","choices":{"A":"Its self-regenerating, feed-forward activation of voltage-gated Na+ channels at each successive point along the membrane","B":"Its passive electrotonic spread governed only by the axon's length constant","C":"Its dependence on continuous synaptic input distributed along the entire length of the axon","D":"Its amplitude being proportional to the strength of the initiating stimulus at every point","E":"Its reliance on simple diffusion of neurotransmitter along the axonal membrane"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The action potential is self-regenerating because depolarization at one point opens local voltage-gated Na+ channels, and the resulting current depolarizes the immediately adjacent membrane to threshold, repeating the process continuously down the axon without loss of amplitude. Purely passive electrotonic spread, which governs graded potentials, decays with distance according to the length constant and cannot sustain amplitude over a meter. Action potentials do not require ongoing synaptic input or neurotransmitter diffusion along the axon, and their amplitude is fixed rather than stimulus-dependent once triggered.","boardPrep":"Step 1: This feed-forward, regenerative mechanism explains why axonal conduction is described as non-decremental, unlike dendritic or synaptic signaling. COMLEX: Long-tract signs in a neurologic exam depend on reliable non-decremental transmission along myelinated central and peripheral axons.","crossRef":"📎 Sensory deficit patterns that begin as graded receptor potentials before recruiting propagating action potentials","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain threshold, depolarization, repolarization, hyperpolarization, absolute refractory period, and relative refractory period.","batch":1,"stem":"Which term describes the membrane voltage at which inward Na+ current becomes self-sustaining and triggers a full action potential?","choices":{"A":"Resting potential","B":"Equilibrium potential","C":"Overshoot","D":"Afterhyperpolarization","E":"Threshold"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Threshold is the specific membrane voltage at which the rate of Na+ channel opening finally outpaces the damping effects of K+ and Cl- conductance and Na+ channel inactivation, allowing the feed-forward depolarization to become self-sustaining and produce a full action potential. Resting potential describes the baseline voltage before stimulation, equilibrium potential describes the voltage at which a specific ion has zero net flux, overshoot describes the brief positive peak of the action potential, and afterhyperpolarization describes the transient dip below resting potential that follows.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Threshold is a dynamic balance point, not a fixed anatomic property, and can be altered by extracellular calcium, temperature, and channel mutations. COMLEX: Local anesthetics and channelopathies are frequently tested through their effect on raising or lowering threshold.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — the arriving action potential that triggers the sequence of neurotransmitter release at a chemical synapse","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain threshold, depolarization, repolarization, hyperpolarization, absolute refractory period, and relative refractory period.","batch":1,"stem":"During which phase of the action potential do voltage-gated Na+ channels inactivate while voltage-gated K+ channels open, returning the membrane toward its resting value?","choices":{"A":"Depolarization","B":"Hyperpolarization","C":"Repolarization","D":"Accommodation","E":"Threshold"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Repolarization is defined by the combination of Na+ channel inactivation, which halts further inward current, and delayed opening of voltage-gated K+ channels, whose outward current drives the membrane potential back toward resting levels. Depolarization is the preceding phase dominated by Na+ influx, hyperpolarization describes the potential briefly overshooting past resting potential, accommodation describes loss of excitability during a slow subthreshold stimulus, and threshold describes the triggering voltage rather than a phase of recovery.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Confusing repolarization with hyperpolarization is a classic error; repolarization only returns the membrane to baseline, while hyperpolarization goes beyond it. COMLEX: Precise terminology matters when describing electrophysiologic findings in a written assessment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — the arriving action potential that triggers the sequence of neurotransmitter release at a chemical synapse","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain threshold, depolarization, repolarization, hyperpolarization, absolute refractory period, and relative refractory period.","batch":1,"stem":"Immediately following an action potential, a second stimulus of normal strength cannot trigger another action potential because most voltage-gated Na+ channels are inactivated. What is this period called?","choices":{"A":"The absolute refractory period","B":"The relative refractory period","C":"The latent period","D":"The accommodation period","E":"The afterdepolarization period"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The absolute refractory period occurs immediately after an action potential, when nearly all voltage-gated Na+ channels remain in the inactivated state and cannot be reopened regardless of stimulus strength, making a second action potential impossible. The relative refractory period follows later, when a stronger-than-normal stimulus can still trigger a response as channels gradually return to the closed, available state. The latent period and accommodation period describe unrelated aspects of nerve excitability, and afterdepolarization is not the standard term for this recovery interval.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The absolute refractory period sets the theoretical maximum firing frequency of a neuron and is a favorite numeric reasoning question. COMLEX: This concept explains why action potentials propagate unidirectionally away from their site of initiation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — the arriving action potential that triggers the sequence of neurotransmitter release at a chemical synapse","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Classify peripheral nerve fibers by type (Aα, Aδ, C) and predict conduction velocity based on diameter and myelination.","batch":1,"stem":"Which peripheral nerve fiber type is the largest in diameter, is heavily myelinated, and conducts the fastest, carrying muscle spindle and Golgi tendon organ proprioception and somatic motor commands?","choices":{"A":"Aδ fibers, small thinly myelinated fibers","B":"C fibers, unmyelinated slow fibers","C":"Aα fibers, the largest myelinated class","D":"B fibers, autonomic preganglionic fibers","E":"Unmyelinated group IV fibers carrying motor commands"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Aα fibers are the largest, at roughly 13 to 20 micrometers in diameter, are heavily myelinated, and conduct at the fastest velocities, typically 80 to 120 meters per second, carrying proprioceptive input from primary spindle endings and Golgi tendon organs and alpha motor output; discriminative touch and vibration travel mainly in the slightly smaller Aβ fibers. Aδ fibers are smaller and thinly myelinated, conducting fast pain and temperature signals at intermediate speed. C fibers are unmyelinated and slowest, and B fibers are lightly myelinated preganglionic autonomic fibers, neither of which carries somatic motor or proprioceptive information.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The diameter-velocity relationship for Aα fibers is a foundational fact for interpreting nerve conduction studies and localizing large-fiber neuropathies. COMLEX: Joint-position testing on physical exam probes large Group I/II proprioceptive afferents, and vibration testing probes large Aβ afferents.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 7 — dermatomes, myotomes, and reflexes that depend on the conduction properties of specific fiber types","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Classify peripheral nerve fibers by type (Aα, Aδ, C) and predict conduction velocity based on diameter and myelination.","batch":1,"stem":"Which peripheral nerve fiber type is small in diameter, thinly myelinated, and conducts sharp, well-localized pain and temperature sensation at intermediate velocity?","choices":{"A":"Aα fibers, large heavily myelinated fibers","B":"Aδ fibers, small thinly myelinated fibers","C":"C fibers, unmyelinated slow fibers","D":"Aβ fibers carrying discriminative touch","E":"Group Ia fibers from muscle spindles"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Aδ fibers are small, roughly 1 to 5 micrometers in diameter, thinly myelinated, and conduct at intermediate velocities of about 5 to 30 meters per second, mediating the fast, sharp, well-localized first pain sensation along with temperature and some mechanoreceptor input. Aα fibers are much larger and faster, carrying proprioception and somatic motor output rather than pain. C fibers are unmyelinated and slower, mediating dull, poorly localized second pain. Aβ fibers and group Ia muscle spindle afferents are large, fast, myelinated fibers unrelated to pain transmission.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The fast versus slow pain pathway distinction, Aδ versus C fibers, is tested through classic descriptions of sharp initial pain followed by dull, aching pain. COMLEX: Distinguishing fiber types helps interpret quantitative sensory testing in peripheral neuropathy evaluation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 7 — dermatomes, myotomes, and reflexes that depend on the conduction properties of specific fiber types","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Classify peripheral nerve fibers by type (Aα, Aδ, C) and predict conduction velocity based on diameter and myelination.","batch":1,"stem":"Which peripheral nerve fiber type is unmyelinated, has the smallest diameter, and conducts the slowest, carrying dull, poorly localized pain and temperature sensation?","choices":{"A":"Aδ fibers, small thinly myelinated fibers","B":"Aα fibers, large heavily myelinated fibers","C":"Group Ib fibers from Golgi tendon organs","D":"Group II fibers from secondary muscle spindle afferents","E":"C fibers, unmyelinated slow fibers"},"correct":"E","explanation":"C fibers are unmyelinated, have the smallest diameter of roughly 0.2 to 1.5 micrometers, and conduct at the slowest velocities of about 0.5 to 2.0 meters per second, mediating dull, poorly localized second pain along with temperature and mechanoreceptive signals. Aδ fibers are faster and thinly myelinated, mediating fast first pain, while Aα fibers are the fastest and largest, unrelated to pain. Group Ib and group II fibers are myelinated proprioceptive afferents from tendon organs and secondary spindle endings, not pain fibers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: C fiber conduction velocity is the classic anchor value for the slow end of the peripheral nerve spectrum on physiology exams. COMLEX: Loss of C fiber function contributes to the painless injuries seen in diabetic and other small-fiber neuropathies.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 7 — dermatomes, myotomes, and reflexes that depend on the conduction properties of specific fiber types","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q19","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe how axon diameter, myelin, internodal distance, and temperature affect conduction velocity.","batch":1,"stem":"Increasing axon diameter increases conduction velocity primarily by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"It increases the number of voltage-gated Na+ channels expressed per unit length of internodal membrane","B":"It decreases the extracellular resistance surrounding the axon","C":"It increases the cross-sectional area of the conductive cytoplasm, lowering the internal electrical resistance to current flow","D":"It shortens the distance between sequential nodes of Ranvier","E":"It increases the local tissue temperature surrounding the axon"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A larger axon diameter increases the cross-sectional area of the cytoplasmic core, which lowers the internal longitudinal resistance to current flow and allows depolarizing current to spread farther and faster along the axon before decaying, increasing conduction velocity. Internodal membrane, not axoplasm, is where Na+ channels are excluded, and channel density along the internode is not the relevant variable. Extracellular resistance, internodal spacing, and local temperature are separate factors that are not directly changed simply by increasing axon diameter.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The diameter-resistance relationship explains why unmyelinated conduction velocity scales roughly linearly with diameter, at about 1.7 meters per second per micron. COMLEX: This principle underlies why large-fiber neuropathies and small-fiber neuropathies produce different patterns of nerve conduction study findings.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 6 — CSF and nerve conduction findings in demyelinating and inflammatory neuropathies such as MS and Guillain-Barre syndrome","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q20","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe how axon diameter, myelin, internodal distance, and temperature affect conduction velocity.","batch":1,"stem":"Myelin increases conduction velocity chiefly by which combined electrical effect on the internodal membrane?","choices":{"A":"Decreasing membrane resistance and increasing membrane capacitance, which shortens the length constant","B":"Increasing the number of voltage-gated Na+ channels along the entire internodal membrane","C":"Eliminating the need for nodes of Ranvier entirely along the axon","D":"Increasing extracellular potassium concentration surrounding the axon","E":"Increasing membrane resistance and decreasing membrane capacitance, which lengthens the length constant and permits saltatory conduction"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Myelin wraps the internodal membrane in multiple insulating lipid bilayers, which increases transmembrane resistance and decreases capacitance, together lengthening the length constant so that depolarizing current spreads passively and efficiently to the next node. This allows the action potential to jump from node to node, called saltatory conduction, dramatically increasing velocity. Myelin does not increase internodal Na+ channel density, which in fact remains essentially absent under myelin, and nodes of Ranvier remain essential rather than eliminated.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The length constant equation and its dependence on resistance and capacitance is a classic quantitative physiology concept tested through myelin's electrical effects. COMLEX: Understanding saltatory conduction explains the profound conduction slowing seen in demyelinating diseases.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 6 — CSF and nerve conduction findings in demyelinating and inflammatory neuropathies such as MS and Guillain-Barre syndrome","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q21","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe how axon diameter, myelin, internodal distance, and temperature affect conduction velocity.","batch":1,"stem":"How does a decrease in tissue temperature, such as local cooling, generally affect nerve conduction velocity?","choices":{"A":"It speeds conduction velocity by reducing membrane capacitance below normal levels","B":"It has no effect on conduction velocity because channel gating is temperature-independent","C":"It speeds conduction velocity by increasing membrane permeability to all ions equally","D":"It slows conduction velocity by slowing the gating kinetics of voltage-gated channels","E":"It slows conduction velocity only in unmyelinated fibers, leaving myelinated fibers unaffected"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Lower temperature reduces the kinetic energy of molecules and slows the rate of the chemical and conformational reactions that govern voltage-gated channel opening and closing, which slows the rise time and propagation of the action potential and reduces conduction velocity. Cooling does not meaningfully reduce capacitance in a way that speeds conduction, channel gating is strongly temperature-dependent rather than independent, and cooling does not indiscriminately increase permeability. This slowing effect occurs in both myelinated and unmyelinated fibers, not selectively in one type.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Temperature-dependent channel kinetics explain both the slowing seen with local cooling and the paradoxical worsening of MS symptoms with heat, called Uhthoff's phenomenon. COMLEX: Local cryotherapy exploits this slowing effect for temporary analgesia in musculoskeletal injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 6 — CSF and nerve conduction findings in demyelinating and inflammatory neuropathies such as MS and Guillain-Barre syndrome","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q22","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Apply electrochemical principles to high-yield clinical conditions such as demyelination, Uhthoff's phenomenon, local anesthetic use-dependent block, Na+ channel toxins, hyperkalemia, and hypokalemia.","batch":1,"stem":"In multiple sclerosis, loss of the myelin sheath impairs saltatory conduction through which combination of electrochemical mechanisms?","choices":{"A":"Exposed internodal membrane lacks Na+ channels, membrane capacitance rises while resistance falls, and previously insulated K+ channels oppose the nodal current","B":"Exposed internodal membrane gains excess Na+ channels, membrane resistance rises while capacitance falls, and K+ channels are permanently inactivated","C":"The nodes of Ranvier are physically destroyed, leaving only internodal membrane capable of generating current","D":"Oligodendrocytes begin actively pumping Na+ out of the axon, preventing any depolarization from occurring","E":"Schwann cells replace oligodendrocytes and produce a thicker, less conductive myelin sheath"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Demyelination exposes internodal membrane that naturally lacks voltage-gated Na+ channels, so it cannot regenerate the action potential, while loss of insulation increases capacitance and decreases resistance, shortening the length constant. At the same time, voltage-activated K+ channels normally sequestered under the myelin near the nodes become exposed, and their outward current opposes the inward nodal Na+ current. Together these three factors, not the reverse changes, prevent the signal from reaching the next node with sufficient amplitude to trigger a new action potential.","boardPrep":"Step 1: All three mechanisms of demyelinating conduction failure are classically tested together as a multi-part explanation for MS symptoms. COMLEX: MS is a named COMLEX clinical presentation, and this mechanism underlies the electrophysiologic basis of evoked potential testing used in diagnosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 6 — meningeal anatomy applied to lumbar puncture, spinal anesthesia, and epidural anesthesia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q23","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Apply electrochemical principles to high-yield clinical conditions such as demyelination, Uhthoff's phenomenon, local anesthetic use-dependent block, Na+ channel toxins, hyperkalemia, and hypokalemia.","batch":1,"stem":"Local anesthetics such as lidocaine preferentially block rapidly firing pain fibers before slower-firing motor fibers because of which property?","choices":{"A":"Resting-state selectivity, in which the drug binds only to closed Na+ channels found predominantly in pain fibers","B":"Diameter-dependent diffusion, in which the drug penetrates only the thinnest fibers regardless of firing frequency","C":"Myelin-dependent exclusion, in which the drug is physically prevented from reaching any myelinated fiber","D":"Use-dependent block, in which the drug binds open and inactivated Na+ channels of rapidly firing fibers","E":"Temperature-dependent binding, in which the drug binds preferentially only at lower local tissue temperatures"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Local anesthetics have low affinity for the resting, closed state of the Na+ channel but high affinity for the open and inactivated states, so channels that cycle through these states more frequently, as occurs in high-frequency-firing Aδ and C pain fibers, accumulate drug binding faster than slower-firing motor fibers, producing use-dependent block. The drug does not bind selectively to closed channels, its access does depend partly on lipid solubility but is not exclusively diameter-limited in this selective way, and myelin does not simply exclude the drug from all myelinated fibers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Use-dependent block explains the clinical order of sensory loss during regional anesthesia, with pain and temperature lost before touch and motor function. COMLEX: This mechanism is directly relevant to epidural and spinal anesthesia technique and expected onset order of blockade.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 6 — meningeal anatomy applied to lumbar puncture, spinal anesthesia, and epidural anesthesia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q24","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Apply electrochemical principles to high-yield clinical conditions such as demyelination, Uhthoff's phenomenon, local anesthetic use-dependent block, Na+ channel toxins, hyperkalemia, and hypokalemia.","batch":1,"stem":"Marine toxins such as tetrodotoxin and saxitoxin cause paralysis and numbness through which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"They bind the intracellular pore of the voltage-gated Na+ channel, preventing channel inactivation and causing continuous depolarization","B":"They activate voltage-gated K+ channels, causing sustained hyperpolarization of the axonal membrane","C":"They bind the extracellular pore of the voltage-gated Na+ channel, blocking Na+ influx without altering the resting potential","D":"They inhibit the Na+/K+ ATPase directly, causing a gradual, non-electrical loss of ionic gradients","E":"They dissolve the myelin sheath, producing an acute demyelinating polyneuropathy"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Tetrodotoxin and saxitoxin bind the extracellular mouth of the voltage-gated Na+ channel pore, physically occluding it and preventing Na+ influx even when the channel would otherwise open, which halts action potential generation without changing the resting membrane potential and produces numbness and paralysis. This is distinct from toxins such as batrachotoxin and scorpion venom, which impair Na+ channel inactivation (scorpion venom also shifts activation to more negative voltages), causing hyperexcitability rather than block. These marine toxins do not act on K+ channels, the Na+/K+ ATPase, or the myelin sheath.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tetrodotoxin and saxitoxin are classic pore blockers, contrasted with batrachotoxin and scorpion venom as inactivation inhibitors, a favorite paired-toxin comparison question. COMLEX: Pufferfish and shellfish poisoning vignettes rely on recognizing this pore-blocking mechanism.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 6 — meningeal anatomy applied to lumbar puncture, spinal anesthesia, and epidural anesthesia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q25","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain how ion concentration gradients and membrane permeability generate the resting membrane potential.","batch":2,"stem":"A research scientist studies an isolated neuron in tissue culture and applies ouabain, a specific inhibitor of the Na+/K+ ATPase, while confirming that K2P leak channels and all voltage-gated channels remain fully functional. Over several hours, the resting membrane potential slowly depolarizes toward 0 mV. Which of the following best explains this delayed depolarization?","choices":{"A":"Ouabain directly opens voltage-gated Na+ channels, producing an immediate action potential-like depolarization","B":"Ouabain increases K+ leak channel permeability, driving the membrane potential toward the K+ equilibrium potential","C":"Ouabain has no effect on membrane potential because K2P channels alone are sufficient to maintain resting potential indefinitely","D":"Without Na+ extrusion, the gradients driving K+ efflux through leak channels slowly dissipate, eroding the resting potential","E":"Ouabain converts the resting potential into a graded potential that decays passively along the membrane"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The Na+/K+ ATPase is not the immediate source of the resting potential, since ion gradients are initially large enough that leak channel diffusion alone sets the voltage; however, over time, unopposed passive leak of Na+ in and K+ out gradually collapses these gradients when the pump can no longer restore them. As the concentration gradients shrink, the driving force for K+ efflux weakens, and the membrane slowly depolarizes toward 0 mV. Ouabain does not directly gate any channel, does not increase K+ permeability, and leak channels alone cannot maintain gradients indefinitely without active transport.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ouabain and related cardiac glycosides are classically tested for their effect on the Na+/K+ ATPase and downstream Na+/Ca2+ exchange, but this vignette isolates the pump's slower role in maintaining resting gradients. COMLEX: This concept explains why cellular energy failure during ischemia produces delayed but progressive loss of membrane potential.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 4 — resting ionic gradients as the baseline from which EPSPs and IPSPs are generated","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q26","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain how ion concentration gradients and membrane permeability generate the resting membrane potential.","batch":2,"stem":"A physiologist bathes an isolated axon in an extracellular solution from which all K+ has been removed, while intracellular K+ concentration and all channel and pump function remain normal. Compared to baseline, the resting membrane potential becomes markedly more negative than the usual physiologic value. Which of the following best explains this finding?","choices":{"A":"Removing extracellular K+ inactivates the K2P leak channels, abolishing all resting membrane conductance","B":"Removing extracellular K+ activates voltage-gated Na+ channels, producing sustained depolarization","C":"Removing extracellular K+ steepens the outward K+ gradient, driving the resting potential more negative","D":"Removing extracellular K+ has no effect on membrane potential because Na+ permeability dominates at rest","E":"Removing extracellular K+ triggers immediate cell lysis due to unopposed osmotic water influx"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Because the resting membrane potential tracks closely with the K+ equilibrium potential, and the Nernst equation shows that lowering extracellular K+ concentration makes that equilibrium potential more negative, the resting potential becomes correspondingly more hyperpolarized. K2P channels remain open and conductive regardless of the extracellular K+ concentration; they are not inactivated by this maneuver. Voltage-gated Na+ channels are not activated by removing extracellular K+, K+ permeability rather than Na+ permeability dominates the resting state, and this maneuver alone would not cause immediate lysis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: This experiment is the classic proof that the resting potential tracks the K+ equilibrium potential, directly testable with the Nernst equation. COMLEX: The same logic explains why severe hypokalemia produces membrane hyperpolarization and its associated clinical findings.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 6 — extracellular ionic composition of CSF shaping the resting environment of central neurons","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q27","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain how ion concentration gradients and membrane permeability generate the resting membrane potential.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has a rare genetic disorder in which neurons produce markedly reduced quantities of large intracellular anionic proteins, while ion channel and pump function are otherwise entirely normal. Which of the following best predicts the effect on this neuron's resting membrane potential and osmotic behavior compared to a normal neuron?","choices":{"A":"The resting membrane potential would become more negative and the cell would be more prone to progressive water influx","B":"The resting membrane potential would be unaffected because intracellular proteins play no role in electroneutrality","C":"The cell would immediately depolarize to 0 mV because K2P channels would close permanently","D":"The cell would lose the ability to generate action potentials entirely, independent of any channel function","E":"The resting membrane potential would become less negative and the cell would be less prone to progressive water influx"},"correct":"E","explanation":"With fewer fixed intracellular anionic proteins, less K+ efflux is needed to balance the reduced negative charge, so the resting potential would be somewhat less negative than normal, and the osmotic pull generated by impermeant intracellular protein would also be reduced, lessening the tendency for water to enter the cell. Intracellular proteins are a major determinant of both electroneutrality and oncotic pressure, so their absence is not neutral. K2P channel gating is voltage-independent and unrelated to protein content, and channel function, stated as normal here, would still support action potential generation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Linking intracellular protein content to both membrane potential and osmotic cell swelling ties together the electrical and osmotic themes of this SDL. COMLEX: This principle explains why metabolically compromised or protein-depleted cells are especially prone to cytotoxic edema.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — gray matter horns as the anatomic site of the neuronal cell bodies whose ionic and protein content sets resting potential","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q28","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Use the Nernst concept qualitatively to predict the direction an ion will move when channels open.","batch":2,"stem":"A physiologist studies a neuron with a resting membrane potential of -70 mV and a chloride equilibrium potential of -78 mV. When Cl- channels are experimentally opened, which of the following describes the resulting ion movement and its effect on the membrane potential?","choices":{"A":"Cl- will move out of the cell, making the membrane potential more positive, because the membrane potential is less negative than the Cl- equilibrium potential","B":"Cl- will move into the cell, making the membrane potential more positive, because the membrane potential is more negative than the Cl- equilibrium potential","C":"Cl- will move into the cell, making the membrane potential more negative, because the membrane potential is less negative than the Cl- equilibrium potential","D":"Cl- movement will cease immediately because the membrane potential already equals the Cl- equilibrium potential","E":"Cl- will move out of the cell, making the membrane potential more negative, because the chemical gradient dominates the electrical gradient"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Because the resting potential of -70 mV is less negative than the Cl- equilibrium potential of -78 mV, opening Cl- channels allows negatively charged chloride ions to move into the cell down their electrochemical gradient, which drives the membrane potential toward -78 mV, making it more negative and harder to depolarize. Cl- would only move out if the resting potential were more negative than -78 mV, and the two values here are not equal, so movement does not cease. The Nernst concept evaluates the combined electrochemical gradient, not the chemical gradient alone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: This exact scenario models the mechanism of inhibitory postsynaptic potentials produced by GABA-A and glycine receptor opening. COMLEX: Understanding chloride-driven inhibition is essential for interpreting the mechanism of benzodiazepines and barbiturates.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 4 — chloride influx through GABA-A and glycine receptors producing inhibitory postsynaptic potentials","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q29","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Use the Nernst concept qualitatively to predict the direction an ion will move when channels open.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher records from a presynaptic terminal at the peak of an action potential, when the inside of the terminal sits at +30 mV. Intracellular free Ca2+ is about 0.0001 mM, extracellular Ca2+ is about 1.2 mM, and the course table lists the Ca2+ Nernst value as about +126 mV. Voltage-gated Ca2+ channels then open. Which statement about the driving force on Ca2+ at this moment is correct?","choices":{"A":"It points outward, because the positive interior repels the divalent cation","B":"It is zero, because a +30 mV peak already exceeds the Ca2+ Nernst value","C":"It points inward but vanishes at 0 mV, so influx has already stopped","D":"It points inward at about 96 mV, so Ca2+ influx continues despite the peak","E":"It points inward and outward equally, so the two fluxes balance at the peak"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Driving force is the difference between the voltage across the membrane and the ion's Nernst value. With intracellular free Ca2+ roughly ten-thousand-fold lower than extracellular Ca2+, the Ca2+ Nernst value is about +126 mV, so at +30 mV the terminal is still about 96 mV short of it and Ca2+ keeps flowing in. A positive interior does oppose cation entry electrically, but that force is much smaller than the steep chemical gradient, so the net force is not outward. +30 mV is well below +126 mV, so the force is not zero. Zero millivolts has no special meaning for Ca2+, because its force disappears at +126 mV, and the inward and outward fluxes balance only at the Nernst value itself.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The Ca2+ equilibrium potential (about +126 mV per the course table) is the most positive of the major ions, so Ca2+ enters whenever its channels open, even at the peak of a spike. COMLEX: This inward Ca2+ entry at the terminal is what Lambert-Eaton antibodies against presynaptic voltage-gated Ca2+ channels reduce.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — Presynaptic voltage-gated Ca2+ channels and the Ca2+ entry that triggers vesicle fusion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q30","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Use the Nernst concept qualitatively to predict the direction an ion will move when channels open.","batch":2,"stem":"A physiology student models a neuron at rest, -70 mV, in which all Na+ channels are suddenly opened, with an Na+ equilibrium potential of +70 mV. The student predicts the membrane will depolarize sharply toward +70 mV but never fully reach it, even though Na+ conductance is very high. Which of the following best explains why the membrane potential does not reach the full Na+ equilibrium potential?","choices":{"A":"Because Na+ channels open completely and remain open indefinitely once activated, capping the potential at 0 mV","B":"Because the Nernst equation predicts the membrane can never exceed 0 mV under any condition","C":"Because extracellular Na+ becomes completely depleted during a single action potential","D":"Because the Na+ equilibrium potential itself falls to 0 mV once channels open","E":"Because K+ and Cl- conductances persist, the membrane nears but never reaches the Na+ equilibrium potential"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The actual membrane potential during depolarization reflects the weighted contribution of all permeant ions, not Na+ alone, so ongoing K+ and Cl- conductances continue to pull the membrane away from the pure Na+ equilibrium potential, preventing it from fully reaching +70 mV even when Na+ permeability is very high. Na+ channels do not remain open indefinitely, since they inactivate within milliseconds. The Nernst equation describes single-ion equilibria and does not itself cap the composite membrane potential at 0 mV, extracellular Na+ is not meaningfully depleted by one action potential, and the Na+ equilibrium potential itself does not change with channel opening.","boardPrep":"Step 1: This reasoning connects the single-ion Nernst equation to the multi-ion Goldman-Hodgkin-Katz relationship used to calculate the actual composite membrane potential. COMLEX: Recognizing that no single ion fully dictates membrane voltage is essential for interpreting mixed electrolyte disturbances.","crossRef":"📖 Sensory deficits reflecting incomplete depolarization when competing ionic conductances persist","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q31","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the roles of K+ leak channels, voltage-gated Na+ channels, voltage-gated K+ channels, and the Na+/K+ ATPase.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher exposes cultured neurons to a novel marine toxin that selectively and completely blocks K2P leak channels without affecting voltage-gated Na+ channels, voltage-gated K+ channels, or the Na+/K+ ATPase. Which of the following best predicts the effect on resting membrane potential and neuronal excitability?","choices":{"A":"The resting membrane potential would hyperpolarize toward the K+ equilibrium potential, and the cell would become less excitable","B":"The resting membrane potential would be unaffected because voltage-gated channels alone determine resting potential","C":"The resting membrane potential would depolarize toward the Na+ equilibrium potential, and the cell would become hyperexcitable due to being closer to threshold","D":"The cell would be unable to generate any action potential because K2P channels are required for the action potential upstroke","E":"The Na+/K+ ATPase would immediately compensate, restoring a completely normal resting potential"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Blocking K2P leak channels removes the dominant resting K+ conductance that normally holds the membrane near the K+ equilibrium potential, so the relatively small residual Na+ permeability becomes proportionally more influential, shifting the resting potential toward depolarization and bringing the cell closer to threshold, producing hyperexcitability. Loss of K2P conductance would not hyperpolarize the cell, and voltage-gated channels are closed at rest and do not set the baseline potential. K2P channels contribute to the resting state, not the action potential upstroke, which depends on voltage-gated Na+ channels, and the Na+/K+ ATPase cannot instantaneously restore a lost passive conductance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: This experiment reinforces that K2P channels, not voltage-gated channels, set the resting potential, a frequently confused point on physiology exams. COMLEX: Predicting excitability changes from selective channel blockade is a useful reasoning skill for toxin- and drug-based vignettes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — voltage-gated Ca2+ channel activation once a depolarizing action potential reaches the presynaptic terminal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q32","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the roles of K+ leak channels, voltage-gated Na+ channels, voltage-gated K+ channels, and the Na+/K+ ATPase.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient has a rare inherited mutation causing voltage-gated Na+ channels to fail to enter the inactivated state after opening, so the channels remain persistently activated once triggered. Which of the following best predicts the resulting effect on the action potential and neuronal firing?","choices":{"A":"The action potential would terminate normally because voltage-gated K+ channels alone are sufficient for repolarization","B":"Sustained Na+ influx would prevent normal repolarization, producing prolonged depolarization and repetitive or ineffective firing","C":"The resting membrane potential would become more negative due to unopposed K+ efflux","D":"The neuron would be unable to reach threshold because Na+ channels would remain permanently closed","E":"The Na+/K+ ATPase would immediately reverse its transport direction to compensate for the mutation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Normal repolarization depends on the timely inactivation of Na+ channels combined with delayed K+ channel opening; if Na+ channels fail to inactivate, the persistent inward Na+ current opposes the outward K+ current, prolonging depolarization and producing abnormal, repetitive, or ultimately ineffective firing as the membrane cannot reliably return to a resting, excitable state. K+ channels alone cannot overcome ongoing unopposed Na+ influx, the resting potential would not become more negative under sustained Na+ influx, the mutation described involves failure to inactivate rather than failure to open, and the Na+/K+ ATPase does not reverse its transport direction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: This mutation models channelopathies such as paramyotonia congenita and long QT syndrome type 3, both caused by impaired Na+ channel inactivation. COMLEX: Recognizing slowed inactivation as the shared mechanism across these conditions links seemingly unrelated neuromuscular and cardiac presentations.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 1 — sequence from arriving action potential to neurotransmitter release, disrupted by abnormal presynaptic Na+ channel kinetics","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q33","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the roles of K+ leak channels, voltage-gated Na+ channels, voltage-gated K+ channels, and the Na+/K+ ATPase.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher applies a drug that selectively and irreversibly inhibits the Na+/K+ ATPase in an isolated peripheral nerve, while confirming that all channel proteins remain structurally and functionally normal. Which of the following best describes the expected short-term versus long-term effect on nerve excitability?","choices":{"A":"The resting potential immediately collapses to 0 mV because the pump alone maintains the resting potential moment to moment","B":"Action potentials become larger in amplitude immediately after pump inhibition due to unopposed channel activity","C":"The neuron becomes permanently hyperpolarized because pump inhibition increases K+ leak channel activity","D":"The neuron loses the ability to conduct any action potential within seconds of pump inhibition","E":"Little early change because gradients are large, then a gradual loss of excitability as the gradients slowly dissipate"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Because the resting potential is set moment to moment by existing ion gradients and open channel conductances rather than by the pump directly, acute pump inhibition has little immediate effect, but because every action potential slightly dissipates the Na+ and K+ gradients that the pump normally restores, prolonged inhibition eventually erodes these gradients and impairs excitability. The resting potential does not instantly collapse, action potential amplitude is set by channel conductance rather than pump activity and would not immediately increase, pump inhibition does not increase K+ leak channel activity, and conduction does not fail within seconds given large initial ion reserves.","boardPrep":"Step 1: This distinction between moment-to-moment channel-driven excitability and pump-dependent long-term gradient maintenance is a classic higher-order physiology reasoning point. COMLEX: This concept underlies the delayed, rather than instantaneous, neurologic deterioration seen in metabolic energy failure states.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 6 — restoration of normal nerve excitability as a local anesthetic and its transient channel effects wear off","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q34","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish graded potentials from action potentials.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher records from a dendrite near a subthreshold synaptic input and observes a depolarization whose amplitude decreases smoothly with recording distance from the synapse and whose peak size scales with the amount of neurotransmitter applied. Which of the following best describes this recorded signal?","choices":{"A":"A propagating action potential that decays with distance due to progressive Na+ channel inactivation","B":"A compound action potential produced by summation of many separate all-or-none events","C":"An afterhyperpolarization following a burst of action potentials at the axon hillock","D":"A graded postsynaptic potential that decays with distance and scales with stimulus strength","E":"A saltatory potential that jumps between nodes of Ranvier without decrement"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A signal whose amplitude scales continuously with stimulus strength and decays passively with distance is, by definition, a graded potential, in this case a postsynaptic potential generated by neurotransmitter binding. True action potentials do not decay with distance because they are actively regenerated, and their amplitude is fixed rather than proportional to stimulus strength. A compound action potential reflects summed signals from many separate axons rather than a single graded event, an afterhyperpolarization is a specific recovery phase following action potential firing, and saltatory conduction describes propagation between nodes in myelinated axons, not passive dendritic spread.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Recognizing amplitude-distance decay paired with stimulus-dependent scaling is the key pattern for identifying a graded potential in an experimental description. COMLEX: Synaptic integration at the dendrite depends entirely on these decremental, summating graded potentials reaching the axon hillock.","crossRef":"📖 Receptor and generator potentials that must summate before crossing threshold in intact sensory pathways","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q35","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish graded potentials from action potentials.","batch":2,"stem":"A physiologist finds that as stimulus intensity to a sensory receptor increases, the amplitude of the initial generator potential increases proportionally, but once the axon fires, every recorded spike has identical amplitude regardless of stimulus intensity, with only firing frequency changing. Which of the following best explains the constant spike amplitude?","choices":{"A":"Action potentials are graded events whose amplitude simply saturates at high stimulus intensities","B":"Action potentials are all-or-none events whose amplitude is set by the axon, not by stimulus strength","C":"Action potentials require a minimum stimulus duration rather than a minimum stimulus amplitude","D":"Action potentials decrease in amplitude as firing frequency increases due to progressive channel fatigue","E":"Action potentials are generated only at the sensory receptor itself, not propagated along the axon"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Once threshold is crossed, the action potential's amplitude is determined by the fixed properties of the axon's voltage-gated Na+ and K+ conductances rather than by the intensity of the initiating stimulus, which is why every spike looks identical regardless of stimulus strength, with stimulus intensity instead encoded as changes in firing frequency. Action potentials are not graded events and do not simply saturate, threshold depends on both stimulus amplitude and duration rather than duration alone, amplitude does not progressively decline with frequency under normal conditions, and action potentials propagate along the axon rather than remaining confined to the receptor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The principle that stimulus intensity is encoded as firing frequency, not action potential amplitude, is a foundational sensory physiology concept. COMLEX: This principle explains why quantitative sensory testing measures firing rate or perceptual threshold rather than any electrical amplitude at the bedside.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 4 — spatial and temporal summation of graded synaptic potentials that ultimately encode stimulus intensity as spike frequency","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q36","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish graded potentials from action potentials.","batch":2,"stem":"A physiologist simultaneously triggers a subthreshold excitatory postsynaptic potential at a dendrite and an action potential at a nearby axon, then compares the two signals recorded 1 cm away from each site of origin. Which of the following best describes the expected difference in fidelity of transmission over that distance?","choices":{"A":"The graded potential would have decayed substantially by 1 cm, whereas the action potential would arrive with its amplitude fully intact","B":"Both signals would arrive with identical amplitude, because distance does not affect either graded or action potentials","C":"The action potential would have decayed substantially by 1 cm, whereas the graded potential would arrive with its amplitude fully intact","D":"Both signals would decay equally, because both rely solely on passive electrotonic spread","E":"Neither signal could travel 1 cm without external amplification from additional synapses"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Graded potentials spread passively and decay according to the neuron's length constant, so a subthreshold postsynaptic potential would lose most of its amplitude well before reaching a point 1 cm away, while the action potential is actively regenerated at each point along the axon and therefore arrives at 1 cm with essentially undiminished amplitude. Both signals do not behave identically with distance, the action potential does not decay the way described, both do not rely solely on passive spread since the action potential is regenerative, and action potentials routinely travel far greater distances than 1 cm without requiring additional synapses.","boardPrep":"Step 1: This side-by-side comparison is a classic way boards test the decrement-versus-no-decrement distinction between graded and action potentials. COMLEX: Long-tract neurologic signs depend on this fidelity of action potential transmission over substantial anatomic distances.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — white matter tracts as the anatomic substrate over which non-decrementing action potentials travel long distances","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q37","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain threshold, depolarization, repolarization, hyperpolarization, absolute refractory period, and relative refractory period.","batch":2,"stem":"In a physiology lab, a stimulus that steps the membrane of an isolated axon rapidly to 10 mV above threshold triggers an action potential. The same axon is then given a slowly rising current ramp that carries the membrane to 20 mV above the usual threshold over several hundred milliseconds, and no action potential occurs. Which of the following best explains the failure of the slow ramp?","choices":{"A":"The slow ramp opened voltage-gated K+ channels before any Na+ channels could respond","B":"The slow ramp lowered the Na+ equilibrium potential below the threshold voltage","C":"The axon entered its absolute refractory period from a preceding spontaneous spike","D":"The slow ramp activated the Na+/K+ ATPase, which hyperpolarized the membrane","E":"Na+ channels inactivated as fast as they opened, so too few were open at one time"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This is accommodation. A slowly applied depolarization opens Na+ channels at a rate too low for enough of them to be open at the same moment to start the regenerative upstroke; meanwhile the opened channels pass into the inactivated state. If the slow depolarization lasts long enough, nearly all Na+ channels become inactivated and no action potential is possible even well above the usual threshold, which is why a nerve under prolonged pressure goes to sleep. Voltage-gated K+ channels open with a delay and do not explain why the rapid step fired. The Na+ equilibrium potential is set by the Na+ concentration gradient and does not fall with a ramp. Nothing in the setup produced a preceding spike, and the electrogenic pump contributes only a few millivolts, too little to block firing at 20 mV above threshold.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Accommodation follows from the three Na+ channel states (closed, open, inactivated): the rate of depolarization matters, not only its size. COMLEX: A limb that falls asleep after prolonged pressure on a nerve is the bedside example of accommodation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — Voltage-gated Na+ channel opening and inactivation, the two competing processes that decide whether a slow stimulus fires","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q38","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain threshold, depolarization, repolarization, hyperpolarization, absolute refractory period, and relative refractory period.","batch":2,"stem":"In a healthy volunteer, when a second stimulus is delivered 3 milliseconds after a prior action potential, a larger-than-normal stimulus is required to trigger a second response. Which of the following best describes this phase and its underlying mechanism?","choices":{"A":"The relative refractory period, during which some Na+ channels remain inactivated and persistent K+ efflux increases the threshold needed to reach firing","B":"The absolute refractory period, during which no stimulus of any strength could trigger a second action potential","C":"The accommodation phase, during which a slow depolarization inactivates channels without any preceding action potential","D":"The overshoot phase, during which the membrane potential is transiently positive","E":"The undershoot phase caused entirely by continued voltage-gated Na+ channel activation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"At 3 milliseconds after the first action potential, enough Na+ channels have returned to the closed, available state that a second action potential is possible, but because some channels remain inactivated and residual K+ conductance keeps the membrane closer to hyperpolarized, a stronger-than-normal stimulus is needed to reach threshold, defining the relative refractory period. The absolute refractory period would make any second stimulus impossible regardless of strength, accommodation refers to a distinct phenomenon involving slow subthreshold stimuli rather than post-action potential recovery, overshoot describes the peak of the first action potential itself, and the undershoot is driven by K+, not Na+, channel activity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The relative refractory period explains why maximal voluntary firing rates are lower than the theoretical maximum set by the absolute refractory period alone. COMLEX: Recognizing this graded recovery of excitability is useful when interpreting repetitive nerve stimulation studies.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 5 — sensory level determination that depends on reliably propagated action potentials across successive spinal segments","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q39","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain threshold, depolarization, repolarization, hyperpolarization, absolute refractory period, and relative refractory period.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher notes that a particular neuron shows a brief hyperpolarizing afterpotential following each action potential, during which the cell is transiently harder to excite than at normal resting potential. Which of the following best explains this observation?","choices":{"A":"Continued Na+ influx through voltage-gated Na+ channels after repolarization drives the membrane transiently more positive than resting potential","B":"The Na+/K+ ATPase reverses direction transiently, pumping Na+ into the cell and K+ out","C":"Continued K+ efflux through voltage-gated K+ channels after repolarization drives the membrane transiently more negative than resting potential","D":"Cl- channels close completely, eliminating all inhibitory conductance during this period","E":"Voltage-gated Ca2+ channels open maximally, driving sustained depolarization beyond threshold"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Voltage-gated K+ channels remain open briefly after the membrane has returned to resting potential, allowing continued K+ efflux that transiently hyperpolarizes the cell beyond its normal resting value, and because the membrane is farther from threshold during this afterhyperpolarization, excitability is reduced. Continued Na+ influx would depolarize rather than hyperpolarize the cell, the Na+/K+ ATPase does not reverse its transport direction under normal physiologic conditions, closing Cl- channels would not by itself produce this hyperpolarizing afterpotential, and Ca2+ channel opening would depolarize rather than hyperpolarize the membrane.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The afterhyperpolarization is a classic feature of pacemaker and repetitively firing neurons and helps set the interspike interval. COMLEX: This mechanism underlies the anode break phenomenon and the regular firing pattern of intrinsically rhythmic neurons.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 4 — hyperpolarizing inhibitory postsynaptic potentials that similarly reduce neuronal excitability","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q40","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Classify peripheral nerve fibers by type (Aα, Aδ, C) and predict conduction velocity based on diameter and myelination.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with diabetic peripheral neuropathy undergoes a nerve conduction study of the median nerve. Stimulating at low intensity produces a single waveform at short latency corresponding to a conduction velocity of approximately 80 to 120 meters per second. Which of the following fiber types produced this earliest waveform?","choices":{"A":"Aα fibers, the largest and most heavily myelinated peripheral fibers","B":"Aδ fibers, small and thinly myelinated fibers","C":"C fibers, unmyelinated fibers","D":"B fibers, preganglionic autonomic fibers","E":"Group IV fibers, unmyelinated nociceptive afferents"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A conduction velocity of 80 to 120 meters per second is characteristic of Aα fibers, the largest and most heavily myelinated peripheral nerve fibers, and because larger fibers have the lowest activation threshold, they are recruited first at low stimulus intensity, producing the earliest waveform. Aδ and C fibers conduct far more slowly and would appear at longer latencies with higher stimulus intensity. B fibers are lightly myelinated autonomic fibers with intermediate velocity, and group IV fibers are synonymous with unmyelinated C fibers, neither matching this velocity range.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Nerve conduction studies exploit the diameter-threshold relationship, with large fibers recruited first at low stimulus intensity, a concept directly tested here. COMLEX: Compression neuropathies, such as carpal tunnel syndrome, are diagnosed by detecting slowed Aα fiber conduction across the compressed segment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 7 — dermatomal and myotomal localization that depends on the specific fiber types carrying sensory and motor signals","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q41","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Classify peripheral nerve fibers by type (Aα, Aδ, C) and predict conduction velocity based on diameter and myelination.","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old woman sustains a minor burn injury and reports a sharp, well-localized pricking pain felt almost immediately at the site, followed several seconds later by a dull, diffuse burning sensation that persists. Which of the following fiber types mediate the first and second components of this pain, respectively?","choices":{"A":"C fibers mediate the fast, sharp first pain, and Aδ fibers mediate the slow, dull second pain","B":"Aα fibers mediate the fast, sharp first pain, and Aδ fibers mediate the slow, dull second pain","C":"Aδ fibers mediate the fast, sharp first pain, and C fibers mediate the slow, dull second pain","D":"Aδ fibers mediate both the first and second pain sensations equally","E":"C fibers mediate both the first and second pain sensations equally"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The classic double pain response to a noxious stimulus reflects the two different nociceptive fiber types: thinly myelinated Aδ fibers conduct rapidly enough to produce the immediate, sharp, well-localized first pain, while unmyelinated C fibers conduct much more slowly, producing the delayed, dull, poorly localized second pain that lingers. Aα fibers are large, fast, myelinated fibers dedicated to proprioception and somatic motor output rather than pain, and neither fiber type alone accounts for both temporally distinct components of this response.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The fast-slow double pain response is a frequently tested clinical correlate of Aδ versus C fiber conduction velocity differences. COMLEX: This distinction is relevant when counseling patients on the expected time course of pain following acute tissue injury.","crossRef":"📖 Sensory deficit patterns used to distinguish spinothalamic from dorsal column dysfunction at the bedside","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q42","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Classify peripheral nerve fibers by type (Aα, Aδ, C) and predict conduction velocity based on diameter and myelination.","batch":2,"stem":"A physiologist measures conduction velocity in three isolated peripheral nerve fibers of differing diameter and myelination and records velocities of approximately 1 meter per second, 15 meters per second, and 100 meters per second. Which of the following correctly classifies these three fibers from slowest to fastest?","choices":{"A":"The 1 meter per second fiber is an Aα fiber, the 15 meter per second fiber is an Aδ fiber, and the 100 meter per second fiber is a C fiber","B":"The 1 meter per second fiber is a C fiber, the 15 meter per second fiber is an Aδ fiber, and the 100 meter per second fiber is an Aα fiber","C":"The 1 meter per second fiber is an Aδ fiber, the 15 meter per second fiber is a C fiber, and the 100 meter per second fiber is an Aα fiber","D":"All three velocities are consistent with C fibers of varying myelin thickness","E":"All three velocities are consistent with Aα fibers of varying internodal distance"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The three measured velocities correspond precisely to the expected ranges for the three classic peripheral fiber types: approximately 0.5 to 2.0 meters per second for unmyelinated C fibers, approximately 5 to 30 meters per second for thinly myelinated Aδ fibers, and approximately 80 to 120 meters per second for the largest, heavily myelinated Aα fibers. The other orderings reverse or scramble this relationship, and C fibers are by definition unmyelinated, so myelin thickness cannot explain variation among them; likewise, Aα fibers do not span a range as wide as 1 to 100 meters per second based on internodal distance alone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Matching specific conduction velocity ranges to fiber type is a frequently tested numeric recall item on physiology exams. COMLEX: Recognizing these ranges allows correct interpretation of nerve conduction study reports listing measured velocities.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 5 — clinical lesion localization that depends on identifying which specific fiber type is disrupted by injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q43","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe how axon diameter, myelin, internodal distance, and temperature affect conduction velocity.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with Guillain-Barre syndrome has segmental demyelination of peripheral nerves. Nerve conduction studies of the peroneal nerve show markedly reduced conduction velocity despite a structurally normal axon diameter. Which of the following best explains this finding?","choices":{"A":"Loss of myelin insulation decreases membrane resistance and increases capacitance, shortening the length constant and impairing conduction","B":"Loss of myelin insulation increases membrane resistance and decreases capacitance, lengthening the length constant and improving conduction","C":"Axonal diameter has decreased proportionally to compensate for demyelination, slowing conduction","D":"Voltage-gated Na+ channel density has increased along the entire internodal membrane, slowing conduction","E":"The nodes of Ranvier have fused together, eliminating the need for saltatory conduction entirely"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Segmental demyelination strips away the insulating effect of myelin, which decreases membrane resistance and increases capacitance, shortening the length constant so that depolarizing current generated at one node decays before reliably reaching the next, slowing or blocking saltatory conduction even though the axon itself remains structurally intact. The opposite changes, increased resistance and decreased capacitance, are what myelin normally provides and would improve rather than impair conduction. Axon diameter is stated to be normal in this vignette, Na+ channel density does not increase along demyelinated internodal membrane, and nodes of Ranvier do not fuse in demyelinating disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guillain-Barre syndrome is a classic acute demyelinating polyneuropathy tested through its electrophysiologic hallmark of slowed conduction velocity with relatively preserved axon structure. COMLEX: GBS is a named COMLEX clinical presentation frequently paired with ascending weakness and areflexia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 6 — characteristic CSF albuminocytologic dissociation that supports a diagnosis of Guillain-Barre syndrome","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q44","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe how axon diameter, myelin, internodal distance, and temperature affect conduction velocity.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher compares two healthy myelinated axons of equal length and equal diameter and myelin thickness. Axon 1 has short internodal distances, and axon 2 has long internodal distances. Which of the following correctly identifies which axon conducts faster and why?","choices":{"A":"Axon 1 conducts faster because more frequent nodes allow more opportunities for signal regeneration per unit length","B":"Axon 2 conducts faster because the action potential can passively bridge a longer stretch of insulated membrane before needing to regenerate at the next node","C":"Both axons conduct at identical velocities because internodal distance does not affect conduction speed","D":"Axon 2 conducts slower because longer internodes increase the total membrane capacitance of the axon","E":"Axon 1 conducts faster because shorter internodes increase the total number of voltage-gated Na+ channels available"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because equal diameter and myelin thickness give both axons the same length constant, a longer internodal distance allows the passively spreading depolarization to cover more physical distance before needing to regenerate at the next node, so fewer regeneration events are needed per unit length and the impulse travels faster overall. More frequent nodes do not speed conduction; each regeneration event adds a small time delay, so more nodes per unit length actually slows conduction. Internodal distance is a genuine determinant of velocity, and longer internodes do not meaningfully increase total axon capacitance in a way that slows conduction, since capacitance per unit length is unchanged.","boardPrep":"Step 1: This principle explains why remyelinated axons, which regenerate with shorter internodes, conduct more slowly than the original nerve even after recovery. COMLEX: Recognizing residual slowing after remyelination helps interpret persistent mild conduction deficits in recovered demyelinating neuropathy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 6 — vascular supply and structural integrity of long central tracts that depend on preserved internodal architecture","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q45","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe how axon diameter, myelin, internodal distance, and temperature affect conduction velocity.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman is found hypothermic outdoors with a core temperature of 28 degrees Celsius. Peripheral nerve conduction studies performed after rewarming to normal baseline show that conduction velocity had been markedly slowed during the hypothermic period despite structurally normal nerves. Which of the following best explains this temperature-dependent slowing?","choices":{"A":"Lower temperature increases voltage-gated Na+ channel density along the axon, paradoxically slowing conduction","B":"Lower temperature causes acute demyelination of peripheral nerves, mimicking a chronic neuropathy","C":"Lower temperature increases membrane capacitance sufficiently to halt all axonal conduction permanently","D":"Lower temperature slows voltage-gated channel opening and closing, reducing the speed of impulse propagation","E":"Lower temperature has no physiologic effect on channel kinetics, and any slowing reflects only nerve compression"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ion channel gating is a temperature-sensitive chemical and conformational process, so reduced tissue temperature slows the opening and closing kinetics of voltage-gated Na+ and K+ channels, reducing the rate of ion flux during each action potential and slowing overall conduction velocity, an effect that reverses with rewarming as described in this structurally normal nerve. Cold does not increase channel density, does not cause structural demyelination, and does not permanently halt conduction in a reversible hypothermic patient. Temperature-dependent channel kinetics, not nerve compression, is the physiologic explanation for this reversible finding.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Temperature-sensitive channel kinetics explain both hypothermic slowing of conduction and the reciprocal worsening of demyelinated conduction with heat seen in Uhthoff's phenomenon. COMLEX: Severe hypothermia is a recognized cause of diffusely slowed nerve conduction that should not be mistaken for a primary neuropathy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 4 — hypothalamic thermoregulatory control that is overwhelmed and fails to protect peripheral nerve conduction during severe hypothermia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q46","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Apply electrochemical principles to high-yield clinical conditions such as demyelination, Uhthoff's phenomenon, local anesthetic use-dependent block, Na+ channel toxins, hyperkalemia, and hypokalemia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with known multiple sclerosis reports temporary worsening of visual blurring and leg weakness after a hot bath, resolving within an hour of cooling down. This is a classic description of Uhthoff's phenomenon. Which of the following best explains the electrochemical basis of this transient worsening?","choices":{"A":"Elevated temperature speeds Na+ channel inactivation, pushing marginally conducting demyelinated segments into block","B":"Elevated temperature causes acute new demyelination of previously healthy axons within minutes of heat exposure","C":"Elevated temperature permanently destroys the remaining myelinated internodes, causing irreversible deficits","D":"Elevated temperature increases voltage-gated Na+ channel density at demyelinated nodes, improving conduction transiently","E":"Elevated temperature has no established relationship to conduction in demyelinated axons and reflects unrelated fatigue"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In demyelinated axons, the safety factor for conduction is already reduced because demyelination shortens the length constant, and because voltage-gated channel kinetics, including the speed of Na+ channel inactivation, are temperature-sensitive, elevated temperature further shortens the effective open time of Na+ channels, tipping marginally conducting segments into transient conduction block; the deficit reverses once temperature normalizes. Heat does not cause new structural demyelination or permanent destruction within minutes, it does not increase channel density, and this is a well-recognized and reproducible electrophysiologic phenomenon rather than unrelated fatigue.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Uhthoff's phenomenon is a classic reversible heat-sensitive worsening seen in MS, explained by temperature effects on the already reduced safety factor of demyelinated axons. COMLEX: MS is a named COMLEX clinical presentation, and recognizing Uhthoff's phenomenon avoids mistaking a transient, heat-triggered pseudo-relapse for true disease progression.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 6 — MS-associated CSF findings, such as oligoclonal bands, that support the diagnosis in a patient with Uhthoff's phenomenon","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q47","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Apply electrochemical principles to high-yield clinical conditions such as demyelination, Uhthoff's phenomenon, local anesthetic use-dependent block, Na+ channel toxins, hyperkalemia, and hypokalemia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man sustains a crush injury to the forearm with a large hematoma. Nearby sensory nerve endings fire persistently, generating spontaneous pain even without movement, and local tissue sampling shows markedly elevated extracellular K+ near the injury site. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of this spontaneous pain?","choices":{"A":"Locally elevated extracellular K+ shifts the K+ equilibrium potential negative, hyperpolarizing nerve terminals and triggering spontaneous action potentials","B":"Locally elevated extracellular K+ inactivates all voltage-gated Na+ channels, preventing any action potential from occurring","C":"Locally elevated extracellular K+ directly activates the Na+/K+ ATPase, generating spontaneous depolarizing current","D":"Locally elevated extracellular K+ shifts the K+ equilibrium potential positive, depolarizing terminals toward threshold and causing spontaneous firing","E":"Locally elevated extracellular K+ has no effect on nerve terminal excitability because K+ leak channels are voltage-independent"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Tissue injury releases intracellular K+ into the extracellular space, and by the Nernst relationship, raising extracellular K+ makes the K+ equilibrium potential less negative, which partially depolarizes nearby nerve terminals toward threshold and can trigger spontaneous, unprovoked action potentials perceived as pain. Elevated extracellular K+ moves the equilibrium potential in the positive, not negative, direction, so hyperpolarization would not occur. Elevated K+ does not inactivate Na+ channels outright, nor does it directly activate the Na+/K+ ATPase, and although K2P channels are voltage-independent in their gating, the equilibrium potential they track is very much concentration-dependent.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Local hyperkalemia from tissue injury is a classic peripheral mechanism of injury-related pain, distinct from central pain pathways. COMLEX: This mechanism is relevant to understanding pain generation in crush injury, compartment syndrome, and other localized tissue trauma.","crossRef":"📖 Peripheral nerve and polyneuropathy sensory patterns relevant to localized nerve injury and pain","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q48","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Apply electrochemical principles to high-yield clinical conditions such as demyelination, Uhthoff's phenomenon, local anesthetic use-dependent block, Na+ channel toxins, hyperkalemia, and hypokalemia.","batch":2,"stem":"An 8-year-old boy in the ICU with severe vomiting and diarrhea develops a plasma K+ of 2.2 mEq per L and profound flaccid muscle weakness. An ECG shows a prolonged QT interval with runs of torsades de pointes. Which of the following best explains the electrochemical basis of this arrhythmia risk in severe hypokalemia?","choices":{"A":"Reduced extracellular K+ increases K+ conductance needed for repolarization, shortening the cardiac action potential and reducing arrhythmia risk","B":"Reduced extracellular K+ decreases repolarizing K+ conductance, prolonging the cardiac action potential and promoting afterdepolarizations","C":"Reduced extracellular K+ inactivates all cardiac voltage-gated Na+ channels, preventing any depolarization from occurring","D":"Reduced extracellular K+ has no effect on cardiac repolarization because the heart relies exclusively on Ca2+ conductance","E":"Reduced extracellular K+ directly increases Na+/K+ ATPase activity, shortening the cardiac action potential"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Severe hypokalemia paradoxically decreases outward K+ conductance through certain K+ channels, which is required for timely repolarization, so the cardiac action potential is prolonged, corresponding to a prolonged QT interval on ECG, and this prolonged repolarization increases the risk of early and delayed afterdepolarizations that can trigger torsades de pointes. The opposite change, increased K+ conductance shortening the action potential, does not occur in hypokalemia. Na+ channels are not globally inactivated by hypokalemia, cardiac repolarization depends substantially on K+ conductance rather than Ca2+ alone, and the Na+/K+ ATPase activity change does not directly shorten the action potential in this setting.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypokalemia-induced QT prolongation and torsades de pointes is one of the highest-yield electrolyte-arrhythmia associations tested on Step 1. COMLEX: Recognizing profound weakness with ECG changes in a child with gastrointestinal fluid losses should prompt urgent potassium repletion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 7 — neuromuscular excitability disorders such as myasthenia gravis and Lambert-Eaton syndrome that similarly present with fluctuating weakness","isHighYield":true}],"3":[{"id":"sdl03_q01","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain threshold, depolarization, repolarization, hyperpolarization, absolute refractory period, and relative refractory period.","batch":1,"stem":"An action potential started at the axon hillock travels down a motor axon to the nerve terminal and does not reverse to travel back toward the cell body. What prevents the impulse from propagating backward?","choices":{"A":"Na+ channels just behind the impulse are inactivated and cannot reopen yet","B":"Myelin covers the membrane behind the impulse and blocks all ion flow there","C":"The Na+/K+ ATPase restores resting gradients behind the impulse within microseconds","D":"Voltage-gated K+ channels are present on the leading side of the impulse","E":"Axoplasmic resistance is lower toward the terminal than toward the cell body"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Propagation is unidirectional because of the inactivated state of the Na+ channel. Once the action potential passes a stretch of membrane, the Na+ channels left behind are inactivated and refractory, so the current spreading backward cannot open them; only the closed channels ahead can respond. The impulse stops at the terminal because there are no more closed channels ahead and the ones behind are inactivated. Myelin covers internodes on both sides of every node, so it cannot favor one direction. The pump restores gradients over a much longer time and is not what blocks backward spread. Voltage-gated K+ channels terminate the spike rather than direct it, and axoplasmic resistance is not lower in one direction along a uniform axon.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The same Na+ channel inactivation explains both the absolute refractory period and one-way conduction along the axon. COMLEX: The refractory membrane trailing each impulse also sets a ceiling on firing frequency, which matters when interpreting repetitive discharges on electrodiagnostic studies.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — the same voltage-gated Na+ channel inactivation that terminates the spike and leaves the membrane behind it refractory","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q02","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain threshold, depolarization, repolarization, hyperpolarization, absolute refractory period, and relative refractory period.","batch":1,"stem":"Immediately after the peak of an action potential, the membrane voltage briefly dips below the normal resting level before settling back to baseline. What is this transient undershoot called?","choices":{"A":"Repolarization, produced by Na+ channel inactivation returning the membrane to rest","B":"Hyperpolarization, produced by K+ channels that stay open past the point of reaching resting potential","C":"Depolarization, produced by continued Na+ influx pushing the membrane further positive","D":"Threshold, the specific voltage at which a second spike could theoretically be triggered","E":"The absolute refractory period, defined by Na+ channels that cannot yet be reopened"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hyperpolarization, or the afterhyperpolarization, occurs because voltage-gated K+ channels stay open briefly after the membrane has already returned to resting potential, driving the voltage transiently more negative than baseline before K+ conductance falls back to normal. Repolarization only describes the return to resting potential, not an overshoot past it. Depolarization describes the opposite, positive-going phase, threshold is a triggering voltage rather than a post-spike event, and the absolute refractory period is a channel-state concept rather than a voltage phase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Confusing repolarization with hyperpolarization is a classic trap; only hyperpolarization goes past the resting value. COMLEX: Precise terminology for each phase is expected when describing electrophysiologic findings in written documentation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — the same voltage-gated Na+ and K+ channels whose opening and inactivation now generate each named phase of the action potential","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q03","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain threshold, depolarization, repolarization, hyperpolarization, absolute refractory period, and relative refractory period.","batch":1,"stem":"A stronger-than-normal stimulus can trigger a second action potential shortly after the first, but a normal-strength stimulus cannot. Which period is this, and why?","choices":{"A":"The absolute refractory period, because essentially all Na+ channels remain inactivated regardless of stimulus size","B":"The latent period, because the axon has not yet begun to conduct any signal","C":"The relative refractory period, when Na+ channels are still resetting and threshold remains elevated","D":"Accommodation, because a slowly rising subthreshold stimulus has raised the resting threshold permanently","E":"The overshoot period, because the membrane voltage is still above 0 mV at this point"},"correct":"C","explanation":"During the relative refractory period, most Na+ channels have returned from the inactivated to the closed, available state, but residual K+ conductance keeps the membrane hyperpolarized and threshold temporarily elevated, so only a larger-than-normal depolarizing stimulus can trigger a second spike. In the absolute refractory period, no stimulus of any strength can succeed because Na+ channels remain uniformly inactivated. Latent period and accommodation describe unrelated aspects of excitability, and the overshoot has already resolved well before this window opens.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The relative refractory period is the classic explanation for why action potential frequency has an upper physiologic limit that is not absolute. COMLEX: This graded recovery of excitability underlies the interpretation of repetitive nerve stimulation studies.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — the same voltage-gated Na+ and K+ channels whose opening and inactivation now generate each named phase of the action potential","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q04","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Classify peripheral nerve fibers by type (Aα, Aδ, C) and predict conduction velocity based on diameter and myelination.","batch":1,"stem":"In a recording of the compound action potential of a mixed peripheral nerve, C fibers are by far the most numerous axons, yet the C-fiber peak is much smaller than the A-fiber peak. What best explains this difference?","choices":{"A":"C fibers fire in an asynchronous pattern, which cancels their summed signal","B":"Each small C fiber has little membrane surface, so it adds very little current","C":"C fibers have a higher threshold, so few are recruited at any stimulus strength","D":"C-fiber action potentials have a smaller overshoot than A-fiber action potentials","E":"Myelin around C fibers insulates them from the extracellular recording electrode"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A compound action potential sums the extracellular currents of many fibers, and each fiber's contribution scales with its membrane area. The large A fibers have far more membrane per fiber and produce a large peak even though they are fewer, while the many small C fibers each contribute little current, so their peak is small and late; peak size is therefore a poor guide to fiber number. Temporal dispersion widens a slow peak but does not cancel it. C fibers do have higher thresholds, but at maximal stimulus strength they are all recruited and the peak is still small. Their spikes are all-or-none full action potentials, and C fibers are unmyelinated.","boardPrep":"Step 1: On nerve conduction studies, low-intensity stimulation recruits the largest fibers first, and peak amplitude reflects fiber size as well as number. COMLEX: Routine nerve conduction studies assess large myelinated fibers, so a small-fiber neuropathy can coexist with normal results.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — the all-or-none action potential whose summed extracellular currents form the compound action potential by fiber type","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q05","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Classify peripheral nerve fibers by type (Aα, Aδ, C) and predict conduction velocity based on diameter and myelination.","batch":1,"stem":"To test for carpal tunnel compression, an electrophysiologist stimulates the median nerve at the elbow and records from the hand 30 cm away. Assuming a normal conduction velocity for the fastest fibers of about 80 m/s, which latency is expected for the earliest peak?","choices":{"A":"About 4 milliseconds","B":"About 0.4 milliseconds","C":"About 24 milliseconds","D":"About 40 milliseconds","E":"About 2.7 seconds"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Latency equals distance divided by velocity: 0.30 m divided by 80 m/s is about 0.004 s, or about 4 ms. At low stimulus strength the earliest peak comes from the large A-alpha fibers, which have the lowest thresholds and the highest velocities. A latency of 0.4 ms would require about 750 m/s, faster than any human axon. Latencies of 24 or 40 ms correspond to about 12 or 7.5 m/s, speeds of small thinly myelinated fibers or of a severely slowed nerve. A latency of 2.7 s would mean about 0.1 m/s, slower even than C fibers. In a compression or diabetic neuropathy the latency lengthens, or conduction fails altogether.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Latency is distance divided by velocity; large myelinated A-alpha fibers conduct at roughly 80 to 120 m/s. COMLEX: Prolonged median latency across the wrist supports carpal tunnel syndrome, which commonly coexists with diabetic neuropathy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — the all-or-none action potential whose arrival time at a distant electrode reveals fiber velocity","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q06","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Classify peripheral nerve fibers by type (Aα, Aδ, C) and predict conduction velocity based on diameter and myelination.","batch":1,"stem":"Vertebrate myelinated fibers conduct at roughly 6 m/s per micrometer of diameter, whereas unmyelinated human fibers conduct at roughly 1.7 m/s per micrometer. About how fast would a 10-micrometer myelinated fiber conduct?","choices":{"A":"About 1.7 m/s","B":"About 6 m/s","C":"About 17 m/s","D":"About 60 m/s","E":"About 600 m/s"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Applying the myelinated scaling factor, 10 micrometers times about 6 m/s per micrometer gives about 60 m/s, in the range of large myelinated somatic fibers. About 17 m/s is the value for a 10-micrometer fiber without myelin, using the 1.7 m/s per micrometer unmyelinated factor. About 6 m/s and about 1.7 m/s are the per-micrometer factors themselves, not velocities for a 10-micrometer fiber. About 600 m/s is a tenfold error and exceeds any measured nerve velocity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Myelination lets velocity rise steeply with diameter, about 6 m/s per micrometer, which is why the fastest fibers are both large and myelinated. COMLEX: Loss of these large fast fibers in demyelinating neuropathy slows conduction velocity well below the expected range.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — the all-or-none action potential whose speed depends on fiber diameter and myelination","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q07","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe how axon diameter, myelin, internodal distance, and temperature affect conduction velocity.","batch":1,"stem":"In a myelinated axon, the exposed nodal membrane makes up no more than about 0.05% of the axon surface. How are voltage-gated channels arranged along this axon?","choices":{"A":"Na+ channels evenly spread along both nodes and internodes at the same low density","B":"K+ channels concentrated at the node, with Na+ channels under the myelin sheath","C":"Na+ and K+ channels both limited to the internode, sealed beneath the myelin","D":"Na+ channels absent from nodes, which depolarize passively from internodal current","E":"Na+ channels packed densely at the node, with voltage-gated K+ channels paranodal"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Because nodes are such a tiny fraction of the axon, it is economical to pack about 100-fold more voltage-activated Na+ channels there than in an unmyelinated axon. Voltage-dependent K+ channels lie in the paranodal region next to the node, normally insulated by myelin, and the node repolarizes through its high resting K+ conductance. Internodal membrane contains no Na+ channels, so an even spread along nodes and internodes is wrong. The arrangement is not reversed with K+ channels at the node. Channels sealed entirely under myelin could not regenerate the spike, and a node without Na+ channels could not produce saltatory conduction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Na+ channels cluster at nodes of Ranvier and voltage-gated K+ channels sit paranodally; demyelination exposes internodal membrane that has no Na+ channels and unmasks those K+ channels. COMLEX: K+ channel blockade with 4-aminopyridine (dalfampridine) improves walking in some MS patients by reducing that exposed K+ current.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — the voltage-gated Na+ and K+ channels whose placement along the myelinated axon sets saltatory conduction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q08","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe how axon diameter, myelin, internodal distance, and temperature affect conduction velocity.","batch":1,"stem":"Each additional wrap of myelin around an axon adds another layer of membrane between the axoplasm and the extracellular fluid. Why does adding more wraps lower the internodal capacitance rather than raise it?","choices":{"A":"Each wrap adds Na+ channels that discharge the capacitance at every internode","B":"Capacitors in series add as their reciprocals, so each wrap lowers the total","C":"Each wrap thins the axon beneath it, shrinking the area that can hold charge","D":"Myelin lipid conducts current, so additional wraps short-circuit the capacitor","E":"Capacitors in parallel add directly, so the outer wraps cancel the inner ones"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Each lipid layer acts as a capacitor, and the many wraps of myelin are capacitors in series, whose capacitances add as their reciprocals; the more wraps, the smaller the total capacitance. With little capacitance to charge and high resistance, the length constant is long and the next node reaches threshold quickly. Myelin adds no Na+ channels; internodal membrane has none. Myelin does not thin the axon, and the source of the lower capacitance is the series arrangement, not lost axonal area. Myelin lipid is an insulator, not a conductor. Parallel capacitances add directly and would raise the total, so they cannot explain the drop.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Myelin raises membrane resistance and, as capacitors in series, lowers capacitance, lengthening the length constant. COMLEX: Thin, partial remyelination after an attack of MS restores conduction incompletely because fewer wraps mean higher capacitance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the membrane's charge-separating lipid bilayer, now stacked in series by myelin to set internodal capacitance","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q09","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe how axon diameter, myelin, internodal distance, and temperature affect conduction velocity.","batch":1,"stem":"As demyelination worsens in a peripheral nerve, conduction first slows and then fails completely at the affected segment. At what point does saltatory conduction stop entirely?","choices":{"A":"When the internodal distance becomes shorter than the axon diameter","B":"When the nodal Na+ channel density rises above that of normal axons","C":"When the length constant falls below the distance to the next node","D":"When the axon diameter shrinks below the size of a typical C fiber","E":"When the resting membrane potential reaches the K+ equilibrium potential"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Demyelination lowers internodal resistance, raises capacitance, and exposes K+ channels, which together shorten the length constant, the distance over which a passive signal decays to about 37%. Once the length constant becomes shorter than the internodal distance, current from an active node decays too much to bring the next node to threshold, and transmission stops. Short internodes make conduction slower but still possible. Demyelination does not raise nodal Na+ channel density. Axon diameter is not what fails in a demyelinating lesion, and a resting potential at the K+ equilibrium potential would hyperpolarize the membrane, not by itself define conduction block.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Conduction block occurs when the length constant falls below the internodal distance. COMLEX: On nerve conduction studies, conduction block appears as a drop in response amplitude across a demyelinated segment, as in acquired demyelinating neuropathies.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the passive membrane properties that define the length constant, now compared with internodal distance","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q10","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Apply electrochemical principles to high-yield clinical conditions such as demyelination, Uhthoff's phenomenon, local anesthetic use-dependent block, Na+ channel toxins, hyperkalemia, and hypokalemia.","batch":1,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman with eclampsia receives an intravenous infusion of magnesium sulfate to prevent further seizures. By which mechanism do raised extracellular divalent cations such as Mg2+ make nerve membranes less excitable?","choices":{"A":"They enter the neuron and raise the intracellular K+ concentration","B":"They block K2P leak channels, depolarizing the resting membrane","C":"They raise the Na+ equilibrium potential above the peak of the action potential","D":"They speed recovery of Na+ channels from inactivation after each spike","E":"They neutralize surface charge, so Na+ channels sense a more negative voltage"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Phospholipids and membrane glycoproteins carry many negative surface charges. Divalent cations such as Mg2+ and Ca2+ bind tightly to these charges, neutralizing them and abolishing the negative surface potential, so a transmembrane protein such as the voltage-gated Na+ channel senses a more negative voltage and is less likely to open; the neuron is stabilized. Divalent cations do not raise intracellular K+. Blocking K2P leak channels would depolarize and excite the cell. The Na+ equilibrium potential is set by the Na+ gradient, not by Mg2+, and faster recovery from inactivation would increase, not decrease, excitability.","boardPrep":"Step 1: High Ca2+ or Mg2+ stabilizes membranes; low Ca2+ or Mg2+ causes hyperexcitability and tetany. COMLEX: Magnesium sulfate is the standard seizure prophylaxis in severe preeclampsia and eclampsia; watch for loss of deep tendon reflexes as a sign of magnesium toxicity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — voltage-gated Na+ channel gating, here shifted by the surface charge that divalent cations neutralize","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q11","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Apply electrochemical principles to high-yield clinical conditions such as demyelination, Uhthoff's phenomenon, local anesthetic use-dependent block, Na+ channel toxins, hyperkalemia, and hypokalemia.","batch":1,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman whose calcium is at the low end of normal hyperventilates during a panic attack and develops perioral tingling and carpopedal spasm. Which mechanism best explains why the hyperventilation unmasked tetany?","choices":{"A":"Respiratory alkalosis makes albumin bind more Ca2+, lowering ionized Ca2+","B":"Respiratory alkalosis raises extracellular K+, depolarizing peripheral nerves","C":"Hypocapnia directly opens voltage-gated K+ channels in motor nerve terminals","D":"Hyperventilation raises ionized Mg2+, which binds Na+ channels and opens them","E":"Respiratory alkalosis blocks acetylcholinesterase at the neuromuscular junction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ca2+ competes with H+ for binding sites on albumin. When pH rises, as in respiratory alkalosis from hyperventilation, albumin binds more Ca2+ and the ionized fraction falls. With fewer divalent cations on the nerve membrane surface, more negative charge is exposed, Na+ channels open with smaller stimuli, and borderline-low Ca2+ becomes overt tetany. Alkalosis shifts K+ into cells and lowers, not raises, extracellular K+. Opening K+ channels would hyperpolarize and stabilize nerves. A rise in Mg2+ would stabilize membranes, and alkalosis does not block acetylcholinesterase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Alkalosis lowers ionized Ca2+ without changing total Ca2+ (First Aid: rising pH makes albumin bind more Ca2+); alkalosis also worsens low-Mg2+ symptoms. COMLEX: Chvostek and Trousseau signs can be brought out in latent tetany, and slowing the breathing relieves hyperventilation-related symptoms.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — ion gradients and driving forces, applied here to divalent cation shifts that change nerve excitability","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q12","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Apply electrochemical principles to high-yield clinical conditions such as demyelination, Uhthoff's phenomenon, local anesthetic use-dependent block, Na+ channel toxins, hyperkalemia, and hypokalemia.","batch":1,"stem":"A scorpion envenomation causes severe pain, spontaneous muscle spasms, and eventual paralysis. Which mechanism best explains this toxin's effect on voltage-gated Na+ channels?","choices":{"A":"The venom physically occludes the extracellular channel pore, halting Na+ influx entirely without changing resting potential","B":"The venom permanently locks the channel in its resting, closed conformation, preventing any activation","C":"The venom competitively displaces K+ from voltage-gated K+ channels, causing sustained hyperpolarization","D":"The venom slows fast inactivation and shifts activation to more negative voltages","E":"The venom accelerates Na+/K+ ATPase turnover, rapidly restoring resting gradients after each impulse"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Scorpion venom, like batrachotoxin, impairs fast inactivation while shifting the activation threshold to more negative voltages, producing repeated, prolonged Na+ influx that keeps the membrane continuously depolarized and generates spontaneous firing, pain, spasm, and eventual exhaustion-related paralysis. This is the opposite mechanism of pore-blocking marine toxins, which halt Na+ influx entirely. The venom does not lock channels closed, does not act on K+ channels, and does not accelerate the Na+/K+ ATPase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Scorpion venom and batrachotoxin are the classic inactivation-inhibiting, hyperexcitability-producing toxins, paired on exams against pore-blocking marine toxins that cause pure numbness and paralysis. COMLEX: Recognizing this mechanism distinguishes toxin-induced hyperexcitable pain syndromes from toxin-induced flaccid numbness.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — Nernst-based prediction of ion movement direction now applied to hyperkalemia, hypokalemia, and channel-targeting toxins","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain threshold, depolarization, repolarization, hyperpolarization, absolute refractory period, and relative refractory period.","batch":2,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman with chronic vomiting presents with perioral numbness and involuntary carpal spasm when a blood pressure cuff is inflated on her arm. Serum calcium is markedly low. Which mechanism best explains her neuromuscular hyperexcitability?","choices":{"A":"Reduced extracellular Ca2+ exposes surface charge, lowering the Na+ channel threshold","B":"Reduced extracellular Ca2+ directly opens voltage-gated Na+ channels regardless of membrane voltage","C":"Reduced extracellular Ca2+ inactivates K2P leak channels, abolishing the resting membrane conductance","D":"Reduced extracellular Ca2+ raises intracellular protein concentration, increasing osmotic water entry","E":"Reduced extracellular Ca2+ shifts the Na+ equilibrium potential to a markedly less positive value"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Extracellular Ca2+ and Mg2+ normally screen the anionic surface charges of the membrane; when their concentration falls, more of that negative surface charge is exposed, which effectively makes the local transmembrane voltage sensed by Na+ channels less negative, lowering the threshold needed to trigger opening and producing tetany, the classic hypocalcemic presentation with positive Trousseau and Chvostek signs. Calcium does not directly gate Na+ channels, does not inactivate K2P channels, does not meaningfully alter intracellular protein osmotic load, and does not shift the Na+ equilibrium potential itself.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The surface-charge-screening effect of calcium is the classic explanation for why hypocalcemia causes tetany while hypercalcemia stabilizes and depresses excitability. COMLEX: Trousseau and Chvostek signs are bedside tests directly probing this threshold-lowering mechanism.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — voltage-gated Na+ channel gating, extended here to calcium's modulation of the effective activation threshold","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain threshold, depolarization, repolarization, hyperpolarization, absolute refractory period, and relative refractory period.","batch":2,"stem":"An electrophysiology researcher records a peripheral nerve fiber and measures its absolute refractory period at approximately 1 millisecond. Which of the following best estimates the theoretical maximum action potential firing frequency for this fiber?","choices":{"A":"Approximately 100 Hz, limited mainly by the speed of the Na+/K+ ATPase restoring ionic gradients","B":"Approximately 10,000 Hz, since the relative refractory period allows firing well beyond the absolute limit","C":"Unlimited, because graded potentials rather than the refractory period set the true firing ceiling","D":"Approximately 1 Hz, since the entire action potential duration, not just the refractory period, must elapse","E":"Approximately 1000 Hz, since Na+ channels must recover from inactivation before firing again"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Because a second action potential cannot occur until enough Na+ channels have exited the inactivated state, a 1-millisecond absolute refractory period sets an approximate physiologic ceiling of about 1000 impulses per second, a simple reciprocal relationship. The Na+/K+ ATPase restores gradients over a much slower timescale and does not set the refractory limit cycle by cycle. The relative refractory period only permits firing with a stronger, not faster, stimulus and does not remove the underlying ceiling, and refractoriness, not graded potential properties, is what caps frequency.","boardPrep":"Step 1: This reciprocal relationship between absolute refractory period and maximum firing rate is a favorite quantitative-reasoning question stem. COMLEX: Interpreting repetitive nerve stimulation and F-wave studies relies on this same firing-rate ceiling concept.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 4 — the all-or-none, non-decremental action potential, extended here to the frequency ceiling imposed by its refractory recovery","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain threshold, depolarization, repolarization, hyperpolarization, absolute refractory period, and relative refractory period.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man who underwent below-knee amputation two years ago reports intermittent burning pain localized to his amputated foot. Examination reveals a tender nodule at the cut end of the tibial nerve. Which mechanism best explains spontaneous firing at this site?","choices":{"A":"Abnormal clustering of voltage-gated K+ channels at the neuroma raises the local threshold, suppressing all spontaneous activity","B":"Abnormal Na+ channel clustering at the neuroma lowers local threshold, allowing spontaneous ectopic firing","C":"Regrowth of a thicker myelin sheath at the neuroma shortens the length constant, blocking further conduction entirely","D":"Loss of the Na+/K+ ATPase at the neuroma abolishes the resting potential, producing permanent depolarization block","E":"Selective regeneration of only C fibers at the neuroma eliminates all large-fiber input from the residual limb"},"correct":"B","explanation":"After axotomy, the proximal nerve stump can develop a neuroma with abnormally high, disorganized density of voltage-gated Na+ channels, which lowers the local threshold for depolarization enough that channels fire spontaneously without any actual peripheral stimulus, producing the ectopic pain characteristic of phantom limb and neuroma pain syndromes. An increase in K+ channel density would raise, not lower, excitability threshold and suppress firing. Neuromas do not develop thicker functional myelin, pump loss would produce depolarization block rather than pain-generating hyperexcitability, and selective C fiber-only regrowth is not the mechanism of ectopic neuroma discharge.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ectopic Na+ channel accumulation at a neuroma is the classic mechanism tested for phantom limb and post-traumatic neuropathic pain. COMLEX: This mechanism is the rationale for treating neuroma pain with Na+ channel-blocking agents such as topical lidocaine.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — voltage-gated Na+ channel density and distribution, extended here to pathologic ectopic channel clustering after axonal injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Classify peripheral nerve fibers by type (Aα, Aδ, C) and predict conduction velocity based on diameter and myelination.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man with a 15-year history of poorly controlled diabetes reports a several-month history of burning pain in both feet. Examination shows reduced pinprick and temperature sensation in a stocking distribution, while vibration sense and ankle reflexes remain intact. Which fiber types are preferentially affected at this stage?","choices":{"A":"Aα fibers only, the large, heavily myelinated class carrying vibration and proprioception","B":"B fibers only, the lightly myelinated preganglionic autonomic class","C":"Aδ and C fibers, the small, thinly myelinated and unmyelinated classes","D":"All fiber types equally, since diabetic neuropathy affects large and small fibers simultaneously","E":"Group Ia fibers only, the primary muscle spindle afferent class"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Early diabetic peripheral neuropathy classically produces a small-fiber pattern, preferentially damaging the small, thinly myelinated Aδ fibers and unmyelinated C fibers that carry pain and temperature, which explains the burning pain and reduced pinprick and thermal sensation with preserved vibration and reflexes that depend on large Aα fibers. Aα fibers alone are not the initially affected class here, since vibration and reflexes are preserved, B fibers govern autonomic rather than somatosensory function, and diabetic neuropathy does not typically strike all fiber types with equal severity from the outset.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The small-fiber-predominant pattern of early diabetic neuropathy, with preserved vibration and reflexes, is a classic exam distractor against large-fiber processes like B12 deficiency. COMLEX: Monofilament and pinprick testing specifically probe this small-fiber-vulnerable population.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — Na+ and K+ channel-dependent excitability, extended here to fiber-type-selective vulnerability in metabolic neuropathy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Classify peripheral nerve fibers by type (Aα, Aδ, C) and predict conduction velocity based on diameter and myelination.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman with a history of strict vegan diet and no B12 supplementation presents with an unsteady, wide-based gait and loss of vibration and joint-position sense in both feet, with pinprick and temperature sensation relatively preserved. Which fiber type is most affected by this deficiency?","choices":{"A":"Aδ fibers, the small, thinly myelinated class carrying fast pain and temperature","B":"C fibers, the unmyelinated class carrying dull pain and temperature","C":"B fibers, the lightly myelinated preganglionic autonomic class","D":"Aα fibers, the large, heavily myelinated class","E":"Group Ib fibers exclusively, the Golgi tendon organ afferent class"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Vitamin B12 deficiency classically causes subacute combined degeneration, which preferentially damages the large, heavily myelinated (Aα/Aβ) afferent fibers whose central branches form the posterior columns and carry vibration and proprioception, producing the sensory ataxia and wide-based gait described, while pain and temperature carried by smaller Aδ and C fibers are relatively spared. Aδ and C fiber loss would instead produce a small-fiber pattern with impaired pain and temperature, B fibers govern autonomic function, and Group Ib afferents alone would not account for the described vibration and proprioceptive loss.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Large-fiber-predominant loss of vibration and proprioception with preserved pain and temperature is the classic pattern of B12 deficiency and tabes dorsalis, tested repeatedly. COMLEX: Romberg testing directly assesses the large-fiber proprioceptive pathway affected in this presentation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 4 — the propagating, all-or-none action potential, extended here to selective large-fiber vulnerability in nutritional myelopathy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Classify peripheral nerve fibers by type (Aα, Aδ, C) and predict conduction velocity based on diameter and myelination.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man develops rapidly progressive, ascending symmetric limb weakness with absent deep tendon reflexes two weeks after a diarrheal illness. Pain and temperature sensation are relatively preserved while vibration sense is diminished. Which fiber class best accounts for this combination of motor and sensory findings?","choices":{"A":"Aα fibers, the large myelinated class carrying motor and proprioceptive signals","B":"Aδ fibers, the small thinly myelinated class carrying fast pain and temperature","C":"C fibers, the unmyelinated class carrying dull pain and temperature","D":"B fibers, the lightly myelinated preganglionic autonomic class","E":"Group II secondary spindle afferents exclusively, without involvement of any motor axons"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Guillain-Barre syndrome preferentially targets large, heavily myelinated Aα fibers, which carry both somatic motor commands and proprioceptive sensory input, explaining the combination of ascending weakness, areflexia, and diminished vibration sense with relatively preserved pain and temperature carried by smaller fibers. Aδ and C fibers are not the primarily targeted class here given preserved pain and temperature. B fibers govern autonomic function, which can be affected but does not explain the described motor and large-fiber sensory pattern, and Group II afferents alone would not account for the motor weakness.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Large-fiber-predominant demyelination with preserved pain and temperature but lost reflexes and vibration is a hallmark teaching point for Guillain-Barre syndrome. COMLEX: Areflexia on the deep tendon reflex exam is an early, specific clue pointing toward this large myelinated fiber process.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 4 — the all-or-none action potential requiring intact large myelinated fibers, extended here to acute demyelinating polyneuropathy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q19","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe how axon diameter, myelin, internodal distance, and temperature affect conduction velocity.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient recovering from Guillain-Barre syndrome shows clinical strength improvement, but repeat nerve conduction studies six weeks later still show mildly slowed conduction velocity compared to normal, despite the nerve now appearing remyelinated on biopsy. Which explanation best accounts for this persistent slowing?","choices":{"A":"The regenerated myelin contains excess voltage-gated Na+ channels spread throughout the internodal membrane","B":"Newly regenerated internodes are shorter and thinner, so the action potential regenerates more often","C":"The regenerated axon has a permanently reduced diameter that cannot be restored by remyelination","D":"The regenerated internodes are longer than the original segments, increasing the distance current must passively travel","E":"The regenerated myelin has lost all resistive and capacitive insulating properties despite appearing histologically normal"},"correct":"B","explanation":"During remyelination after demyelinating injury, Schwann cells lay down new internodes that are consistently shorter and thinner than the original segments, meaning the action potential must stop and regenerate at more frequent intervals along the same length of axon, which slows measured conduction velocity even though the fiber is now technically remyelinated. Regenerated internodal membrane does not gain excess Na+ channels, since these remain excluded from internodal regions, axon diameter is not the primary driver of this particular residual slowing, and shorter, not longer, internodes are the actual finding, with the new myelin retaining meaningful insulating properties rather than losing them entirely.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Persistent mild conduction slowing despite clinical recovery is explained by short, imperfect remyelinated internodes, a favorite chronic-neuropathy teaching point. COMLEX: Serial nerve conduction studies are used clinically to track this incomplete internodal recovery over time.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — K+-dominated resting cable properties, extended here to how internodal geometry after remyelination governs residual conduction velocity","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q20","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe how axon diameter, myelin, internodal distance, and temperature affect conduction velocity.","batch":2,"stem":"A physiology student compares a 10-micrometer myelinated frog motor fiber with an unmyelinated squid giant axon, both recorded at 20 °C. Which unmyelinated diameter conducts at about the same speed, roughly 20 m/s?","choices":{"A":"Roughly 500 micrometers, since unmyelinated velocity rises far more slowly with diameter","B":"Roughly 10 micrometers, since diameter alone determines velocity regardless of myelination status","C":"Roughly 50 micrometers, since myelination only doubles the effective conduction velocity","D":"Roughly 1 micrometer, since unmyelinated fibers always conduct faster than myelinated fibers of equal diameter","E":"No unmyelinated diameter can match it, since myelinated conduction velocity is theoretically unbounded"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Saltatory conduction lets a small myelinated fiber reach speeds that an unmyelinated axon attains only at enormous diameter: a 10-micrometer myelinated frog fiber conducts at about the same 20 m/s (at 20 °C) as a 500-micrometer unmyelinated squid giant axon, while occupying roughly 1/2500 of the cross-sectional area. This is why vertebrates rely on myelin rather than axon size for fast conduction. Diameter alone does not determine velocity independent of myelination, myelination provides far more than a doubling effect, unmyelinated fibers are not faster than myelinated fibers of equal diameter, and myelinated velocity, while high, is finite.","boardPrep":"Step 1: This diameter-versus-myelination comparison illustrates why myelination, not sheer axon size, is the dominant evolutionary strategy for fast vertebrate conduction. COMLEX: This concept explains why even modest demyelination produces conduction deficits disproportionate to any structural change in axon diameter.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — resting membrane cable properties set by K+ permeability, extended here to the diameter-versus-myelin efficiency comparison for conduction speed","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q21","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Describe how axon diameter, myelin, internodal distance, and temperature affect conduction velocity.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old skier sustains frostbite of the toes during a backcountry trip. On rewarming, he reports the toes were profoundly numb during the cold exposure but sensation is now returning. Which mechanism best explains the numbness experienced during the cold exposure itself?","choices":{"A":"Reduced tissue temperature permanently destroyed all voltage-gated Na+ channels in the affected axons","B":"Reduced tissue temperature increased membrane capacitance, accelerating conduction beyond normal physiologic range","C":"Reduced tissue temperature selectively demyelinated only the large-diameter Aα fibers in the toes","D":"Reduced tissue temperature slowed voltage-gated channel gating kinetics, partially blocking conduction","E":"Reduced tissue temperature activated compensatory Na+/K+ ATPase overactivity, hyperpolarizing the membrane"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cold directly slows the temperature-dependent conformational kinetics of voltage-gated Na+ and K+ channels, which lengthens the time needed to reach threshold and propagate an action potential, producing conduction slowing and, with sufficient cooling, functional block that is experienced as numbness; this effect is reversible with rewarming, consistent with returning sensation. Cold exposure of this duration does not permanently destroy channels outright, it does not accelerate conduction, it does not selectively demyelinate fibers, and it does not work through Na+/K+ ATPase overactivity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Reversible cold-induced slowing of channel kinetics is the shared mechanism behind frostbite numbness, cryotherapy analgesia, and cold-induced nerve conduction block. COMLEX: This reversible mechanism is why sensation typically recovers with rewarming unless irreversible tissue injury has also occurred.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — voltage-gated Na+ and K+ channel gating kinetics, extended here to their temperature dependence in a cold-exposure vignette","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q22","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Apply electrochemical principles to high-yield clinical conditions such as demyelination, Uhthoff's phenomenon, local anesthetic use-dependent block, Na+ channel toxins, hyperkalemia, and hypokalemia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with known multiple sclerosis notices that her vision reliably worsens after a hot shower or vigorous exercise and improves once she cools down. Which mechanism best explains this reversible worsening?","choices":{"A":"Heat permanently destroys additional myelin with each episode, producing irreversible worsening","B":"Heat directly triggers formation of new autoimmune demyelinating plaques during each episode","C":"Heat increases the number of functional Na+ channels along the demyelinated internode","D":"Heat lowers extracellular Ca2+, producing tetany-like hyperexcitability in the optic nerve","E":"Heat reduces the already-diminished conduction safety factor, pushing demyelinated axons into block"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This is Uhthoff's phenomenon: demyelinated axons already conduct with a reduced safety margin, and because temperature-dependent channel kinetics speed up with heat while myelin's insulating function is already compromised, even a modest rise in body temperature is enough to push marginally conducting demyelinated segments into transient, reversible conduction block, producing temporary visual worsening that resolves with cooling. Heat does not destroy additional myelin or trigger new plaques during a single episode, it does not add functional channels to demyelinated internodes, and it does not act through the calcium surface-charge mechanism responsible for tetany.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Uhthoff's phenomenon, heat-induced reversible worsening of MS symptoms, is a classic named finding tested through hot shower or exercise vignettes. COMLEX: Recognizing this reversible, temperature-linked pattern helps distinguish a true relapse from a pseudo-relapse in MS management.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — Nernst-based prediction of ion movement, extended here to how temperature-dependent channel kinetics interact with reduced conduction safety factor in demyelination","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q23","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Apply electrochemical principles to high-yield clinical conditions such as demyelination, Uhthoff's phenomenon, local anesthetic use-dependent block, Na+ channel toxins, hyperkalemia, and hypokalemia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with end-stage renal disease misses two dialysis sessions and presents with generalized weakness. Serum potassium is markedly elevated, and the ECG shows peaked T waves with a widened QRS complex. Which mechanism best explains the weakness and slowed cardiac conduction?","choices":{"A":"Elevated extracellular K+ hyperpolarizes the resting membrane, making Na+ channels easier to activate","B":"Elevated extracellular K+ blocks the Na+ channel pore, like tetrodotoxin","C":"Chronic depolarization from elevated K+ forces many Na+ channels into an inactivated, unavailable state","D":"Elevated extracellular K+ selectively destroys myelin, producing an acute demyelinating process","E":"Elevated extracellular K+ depletes ATP, halting all active transport and channel gating simultaneously"},"correct":"C","explanation":"High extracellular K+ shifts the K+ equilibrium potential in a positive direction, partially depolarizing the resting membrane; this sustained depolarization drives a large fraction of voltage-gated Na+ channels into the inactivated state, leaving fewer available to open, which slows the action potential upstroke, reduces excitability, and produces both skeletal muscle weakness and the widened QRS reflecting slowed cardiac conduction. Hyperkalemia depolarizes rather than hyperpolarizes the membrane, it does not act as a physical pore blocker like tetrodotoxin, it is not a demyelinating process, and it does not primarily act by depleting ATP.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hyperkalemia's Na+ channel inactivation mechanism, causing paradoxical hypoexcitability despite membrane depolarization, is a classic board pairing with peaked T waves and widened QRS. COMLEX: Emergency stabilization with IV calcium works by restoring the margin between resting potential and threshold in this exact scenario.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — Nernst-based prediction of K+ movement and its equilibrium potential, extended here to hyperkalemia's depolarizing effect on excitable tissue","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q24","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Apply electrochemical principles to high-yield clinical conditions such as demyelination, Uhthoff's phenomenon, local anesthetic use-dependent block, Na+ channel toxins, hyperkalemia, and hypokalemia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old man develops sudden proximal muscle weakness the morning after a large carbohydrate-heavy meal and intense exercise the night before. Serum potassium is low during the episode, and he reports similar past episodes with a family history of the same condition. Which mechanism best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Low extracellular K+ directly opens voltage-gated Na+ channels, producing a normal, propagating action potential","B":"A mutated voltage sensor unblocks an abnormal Na+ leak pore, trapping the membrane in an inexcitable state","C":"Low extracellular K+ increases K+ conductance sharply, shortening the action potential and improving excitability","D":"Low extracellular K+ destroys the myelin sheath along affected motor axons, producing acute demyelinating weakness","E":"Low extracellular K+ activates the Na+/K+ ATPase to the point of exhausting cellular ATP stores entirely"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This is hypokalemic periodic paralysis: inherited mutations in the voltage-sensing domain of Na+ or Ca2+ channels create an abnormal gating pore that stays blocked at rest but unblocks when hypokalemia hyperpolarizes the membrane, allowing an aberrant depolarizing Na+ leak current that paradoxically traps the muscle membrane in a persistently depolarized, accommodated, and electrically inexcitable state rather than triggering a normal action potential. Low K+ does not directly open normal Na+ channels to produce ordinary firing, it decreases rather than increases K+ conductance in this disorder, it is not a demyelinating process, and ATP exhaustion via pump overactivity is not the described mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypokalemic periodic paralysis is a classic channelopathy vignette featuring exercise or carbohydrate-meal triggers, paradoxical depolarization, and a positive family history. COMLEX: Recognizing this pattern guides urgent potassium repletion and avoidance of high-carbohydrate meals and strenuous exertion as precipitants.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — Nernst-based prediction of K+ movement, extended here to the paradoxical excitability failure of hypokalemic periodic paralysis","isHighYield":true}],"4":[{"id":"sdl04_q01","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Explain how osmotic forces, impermeant intracellular proteins, sodium distribution, and aquaporin affect neuronal cell volume.","batch":1,"stem":"A neuron's intracellular proteins are far too large to cross the plasma membrane. What direct osmotic consequence follows from this impermeant protein pool?","choices":{"A":"The impermeant intracellular protein exerts an osmotic force that actively pumps water out of the cell against its own gradient","B":"The impermeant intracellular protein has no measurable osmotic effect since large molecules cannot influence water movement","C":"The impermeant intracellular protein exerts a continuous osmotic force that draws water into the cell along its gradient","D":"The impermeant intracellular protein raises the extracellular sodium concentration directly, which then draws water outward","E":"The impermeant intracellular protein converts into a freely permeant form once the resting membrane potential is reached"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Because cell proteins are trapped inside the neuron, they behave as osmotically active particles that continuously draw water inward down its concentration gradient, the van't Hoff relationship that opens every discussion of neuronal volume regulation. Water does not move outward in response to this protein pool, the effect is not negligible given typical intracellular protein concentrations of 200 to 300 milligrams per milliliter, protein does not directly raise extracellular sodium, and protein remains impermeant regardless of the membrane potential.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The impermeant intracellular protein pool is the starting osmotic problem every neuron must counteract to avoid swelling and lysis. COMLEX: This same principle explains why metabolically compromised cells swell once compensatory mechanisms fail.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — extending the ionic basis of the resting membrane potential to the osmotic and electrical forces that regulate neuronal cell volume","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q02","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Explain how osmotic forces, impermeant intracellular proteins, sodium distribution, and aquaporin affect neuronal cell volume.","batch":1,"stem":"Sodium is far more concentrated outside a resting neuron than inside it. How does this distribution help the cell counteract the osmotic pull of its own impermeant intracellular protein?","choices":{"A":"Keeping sodium impermeant and concentrated outside the cell lets its osmotic pull largely balance the inward pull from protein","B":"By keeping sodium freely permeant, it rushes into the cell continuously and directly neutralizes the charge on intracellular protein","C":"By keeping sodium concentrated inside the cell, it adds to the intracellular osmotic load and further worsens cell swelling","D":"By keeping sodium entirely absent from the extracellular fluid, chloride alone is left to balance the intracellular protein","E":"By keeping sodium bound irreversibly to intracellular protein, its osmotic activity is eliminated rather than balanced"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The neuron makes sodium an effectively impermeant, extracellular particle, so its steep outward-facing gradient exerts an osmotic pull that largely offsets the inward pull generated by impermeant intracellular protein, keeping the sum of osmotically active particles close to zero across the membrane. Sodium is not freely rushing inward at rest, it is not concentrated intracellularly, it is not entirely absent from the extracellular fluid, and it is not irreversibly bound to protein.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The steep transmembrane sodium gradient is not just an action-potential fuel source, it is also the cell's primary osmotic counterweight to intracellular protein. COMLEX: Loss of this balance, as in failed active transport, is the mechanism underlying cytotoxic cell swelling.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — extending the ionic basis of the resting membrane potential to the osmotic and electrical forces that regulate neuronal cell volume","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q03","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Explain how osmotic forces, impermeant intracellular proteins, sodium distribution, and aquaporin affect neuronal cell volume.","batch":1,"stem":"Aquaporin channels allow roughly three billion water molecules per second to cross a cell membrane while excluding all other solutes. What property of the aquaporin pore makes this selective, high-throughput transport possible?","choices":{"A":"A wide transmembrane pore that permits ions, water, and small proteins to pass together as a single bulk solution","B":"A narrow transmembrane pore that admits only single water molecules while excluding ions and other solutes","C":"A voltage-sensitive transmembrane pore that opens only once the membrane depolarizes past a defined threshold","D":"A ligand-activated transmembrane pore that opens only after binding a specific circulating neurotransmitter","E":"A gated transmembrane pore that opens only in response to a rise in intracellular calcium concentration"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Aquaporins form a semipermeable transmembrane pore narrow enough to permit only single water molecules to corkscrew through in sequence while excluding ions, sugars, and other solutes, which is what allows such an extraordinarily high rate of water flux without any accompanying solute movement. Aquaporin pores are not wide enough to admit ions or proteins, they are not gated by calcium, they are not voltage sensitive, and they are not activated by a circulating ligand.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Aquaporins are the prototypical semipermeable channel and a favorite way to test the concept of selective water-only transport. COMLEX: At least ten distinct aquaporins exist in humans, and loss of a specific isoform produces tissue-specific disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — extending the ionic basis of the resting membrane potential to the osmotic and electrical forces that regulate neuronal cell volume","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q04","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Recognize major ion channel families emphasized in Chapter 3, including K2P, voltage-gated Na+, voltage-gated Ca2+, voltage-gated K+, CNG, HCN, Piezo, TRP, ENaC/ASIC, GABA-A, glycine, nicotinic acetylcholine, and glutamate receptors.","batch":1,"stem":"A hippocampal pyramidal neuron fires a burst of action potentials. With each spike a little Ca2+ enters, and once intracellular Ca2+ builds up, a K+ current opens, drives the membrane into a prolonged hyperpolarization, and ends the burst. Which channel family carries this burst-terminating current?","choices":{"A":"Calcium-activated potassium channels","B":"Hyperpolarization-activated HCN channels","C":"Tandem pore domain K2P leak channels","D":"Cyclic nucleotide-gated CNG channels","E":"Acid-sensing ENaC and ASIC channels"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The Ca2+-activated K+ channel opens when a train of action potentials raises intracellular Ca2+, driving the membrane to hyperpolarized potentials and breaking off the train; this limits the number of spikes in a burst and helps prevent seizure-like activity. HCN channels conduct an inward Na+ and K+ current activated by hyperpolarization and set the rate of rhythmic firing, rather than ending bursts. K2P channels provide the constant resting K+ leak that sets the resting potential and do not depend on Ca2+. CNG channels link olfactory and visual second messengers to voltage, and ENaC and ASIC channels sense acid and carry Na+.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know each channel family's job: K2P sets rest, voltage-gated K+ ends the spike, Ca2+-activated K+ ends the burst, and HCN paces rhythmic firing. COMLEX: Channel-level control of burst firing is the basis for many antiseizure drug targets.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 5 — afterhyperpolarization after a spike, extended here to the slower Ca2+-dependent hyperpolarization that ends a burst","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q05","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Recognize major ion channel families emphasized in Chapter 3, including K2P, voltage-gated Na+, voltage-gated Ca2+, voltage-gated K+, CNG, HCN, Piezo, TRP, ENaC/ASIC, GABA-A, glycine, nicotinic acetylcholine, and glutamate receptors.","batch":1,"stem":"CNG and HCN channels share a similar structure and both conduct sodium and potassium, yet they serve very different functional roles. Which distinction best separates these two channel families?","choices":{"A":"CNG channels set the pacemaker rate of rhythmic firing, while HCN channels link sensory transduction to membrane voltage","B":"CNG channels conduct only calcium ions, while HCN channels conduct only chloride ions across the membrane","C":"CNG channels open only in response to membrane hyperpolarization, while HCN channels open only in response to depolarization","D":"CNG channels are restricted to skeletal muscle, while HCN channels are restricted to cardiac and smooth muscle tissue","E":"CNG channels link olfactory and visual signals to voltage, while HCN channels set the pacemaker rate of firing"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Both are cyclic nucleotide-regulated cation channels, but CNG channels transduce cyclic nucleotide signals generated by odorant or photon detection into a change in membrane voltage in sensory neurons, while HCN channels activate upon hyperpolarization to depolarize cells rhythmically and thereby set pacemaker firing rate. Reversing the two roles, restricting either channel to a single ion, misassigning their voltage dependence, and restricting them to specific muscle types all misstate the actual physiology of these two families.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CNG channels belong to sensory transduction, olfaction and vision, while HCN channels belong to intrinsic pacemaker activity, a pairing frequently tested together because of their shared structural motif. COMLEX: HCN current is the same current targeted by ivabradine to slow the cardiac sinus node.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 6 — extending peripheral fiber classification to the broader taxonomy of ion channel families that generate and shape neural signaling","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q06","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Recognize major ion channel families emphasized in Chapter 3, including K2P, voltage-gated Na+, voltage-gated Ca2+, voltage-gated K+, CNG, HCN, Piezo, TRP, ENaC/ASIC, GABA-A, glycine, nicotinic acetylcholine, and glutamate receptors.","batch":1,"stem":"A stimulus that stretches or deforms the plasma membrane directly opens a mechanosensitive cation channel to signal touch. Which ion channel family is most directly responsible for this mechanotransduction?","choices":{"A":"The GABA-A receptor family, a pentameric chloride channel activated by binding an inhibitory neurotransmitter","B":"The Piezo channel family, a large trimeric structure that opens directly in response to membrane stretch or deformation","C":"The glycine receptor family, a pentameric chloride channel activated by binding an inhibitory neurotransmitter in the cord","D":"The ENaC and ASIC family, an epithelial and acid-sensing channel family activated primarily by extracellular protons","E":"The nicotinic acetylcholine receptor family, a pentameric cation channel activated by binding acetylcholine at the junction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Piezo channels are large trimeric mechanosensitive channels that open directly in response to physical stretch or deformation of the surrounding membrane, making them the primary transducer of touch, including in Merkel cell mechanoreceptors and their afferent fibers. GABA-A and glycine receptors are ligand-gated chloride channels activated by neurotransmitter binding rather than mechanical force, the ENaC and ASIC family is primarily activated by extracellular acidity, and the nicotinic acetylcholine receptor is a ligand-gated cation channel activated at the neuromuscular junction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Piezo channels are the newest addition to the classic ion channel families and are increasingly tested as the mechanistic basis of touch and proprioception. COMLEX: Gain-of-function Piezo2 variants are linked to distal arthrogryposis, a congenital contracture syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 6 — extending peripheral fiber classification to the broader taxonomy of ion channel families that generate and shape neural signaling","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q07","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Explain how osmotic forces, impermeant intracellular proteins, sodium distribution, and aquaporin affect neuronal cell volume.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher is studying an ischemic neuron whose sodium-potassium ATPase has failed because of ATP depletion. Sodium leaks into the cell along its gradient and can no longer be actively extruded. Which osmotic consequence would be predicted from this loss of sodium impermeance?","choices":{"A":"The cell gains additional water because sodium now adds to, rather than balances, the protein osmotic load","B":"The cell osmotically loses water because sodium now leaves the cell more rapidly along its restored concentration gradient","C":"The cell reaches a new stable volume immediately because chloride alone fully compensates for the loss of the sodium gradient","D":"The cell's aquaporin channels close completely to prevent any further water movement once the sodium pump has failed","E":"The cell's intracellular protein becomes permeant and exits through the damaged membrane, restoring normal cell volume"},"correct":"A","explanation":"When the sodium pump fails, sodium accumulates inside the cell instead of remaining an effectively impermeant extracellular osmotic counterweight, so it now adds to the intracellular osmotic load created by impermeant protein rather than balancing it, and water is drawn further into the cell, the mechanism underlying cytotoxic edema in ischemia. Sodium does not leave more rapidly under these conditions, chloride cannot fully substitute for the lost sodium gradient, aquaporins do not simply close in response to pump failure, and intracellular protein does not become permeant.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cytotoxic edema in stroke is a direct, testable application of the osmotic balance between impermeant protein and impermeant sodium described in this objective. COMLEX: Recognizing that ATP depletion drives cell swelling links basic osmotic physiology directly to acute stroke imaging findings.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — connecting sodium-potassium ATPase failure and RMP collapse to the parallel osmotic consequence of unchecked intracellular sodium accumulation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q08","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Explain how osmotic forces, impermeant intracellular proteins, sodium distribution, and aquaporin affect neuronal cell volume.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-month-old infant is found to have congenital cataracts and is later discovered to lack a specific aquaporin isoform expressed in the lens. Which conclusion about aquaporin function is best supported by this case?","choices":{"A":"Aquaporins are a single uniform channel type, so any congenital aquaporin deficiency should impair every tissue in the body equally","B":"Aquaporins primarily transport sodium rather than water, so their loss would be expected to disrupt the resting membrane potential","C":"Aquaporins are gated by neurotransmitter binding, so their loss should specifically impair synaptic transmission rather than lens clarity","D":"Aquaporins are tissue-specific water channels, so loss of one isoform can impair osmotic equilibrium in a single organ alone","E":"Aquaporins are voltage-gated channels, so their loss would be expected to abolish the generation of action potentials in affected tissue"},"correct":"D","explanation":"At least ten distinct aquaporin isoforms exist in the human body, each distributed in a tissue-specific manner, so congenital absence of a single isoform such as AQP0 produces an isolated deficit, here cataracts from impaired lens osmotic regulation, rather than a generalized systemic problem. Aquaporins are not a single uniform channel, they transport water rather than sodium, they are not neurotransmitter-gated, and they are not voltage-gated action-potential channels.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tissue-specific aquaporin loss producing an isolated organ phenotype, cataracts from AQP0 or deafness from AQP4, is a classic vignette pattern linking channel biology to a single clinical finding. COMLEX: This same tissue-specific principle underlies AQP1's role in maintaining normal intraocular pressure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 8 — comparing this AQP0-driven cataract case with other electrochemical channelopathies applied to board-style clinical vignettes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q09","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Explain how osmotic forces, impermeant intracellular proteins, sodium distribution, and aquaporin affect neuronal cell volume.","batch":2,"stem":"A physiologist calculates that the osmotic pressure generated by the sodium concentration difference across a resting neuron's membrane is approximately 3.4 atmospheres, nearly matching the osmotic pressure generated by intracellular protein. What is the physiologic significance of these two forces being nearly equal in magnitude?","choices":{"A":"The two opposing osmotic forces add together, so the cell must rely entirely on the sodium pump alone to prevent continuous swelling","B":"The two opposing osmotic forces cancel only when the membrane is depolarized, so cell volume is unstable at the resting potential","C":"The two opposing osmotic forces are unrelated to cell volume and instead determine only the amplitude of the action potential","D":"The two opposing osmotic forces cancel only in glial cells, so neurons must rely on a completely separate volume-regulating mechanism","E":"The two opposing osmotic forces largely cancel, so the osmotic particle sum stays near zero and cell volume remains stable"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Because the outward sodium osmotic force is engineered to nearly match the inward osmotic force exerted by impermeant intracellular protein, the net osmotic pressure across the membrane approaches zero at steady state, which is precisely why a healthy resting neuron maintains a stable volume rather than continuously swelling or shrinking. The two forces do not simply add together, their near-cancellation is a steady-state property rather than one restricted to depolarization, they are directly relevant to volume rather than to action potential amplitude, and this balancing principle applies broadly rather than being unique to glial cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The near-perfect cancellation of protein and sodium osmotic forces is the quantitative payoff of the van't Hoff calculation and a favorite integrative exam concept. First Aid mnemonic for the opposite failure mode, correcting a low sodium too fast: From low to high, your pons will die, for osmotic demyelination syndrome. COMLEX: This balance is precisely what fails first in metabolically compromised or ischemic tissue, producing measurable cytotoxic swelling.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — linking this osmotic balance calculation to the same ionic gradients that generate the resting membrane potential","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q10","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Recognize major ion channel families emphasized in Chapter 3, including K2P, voltage-gated Na+, voltage-gated Ca2+, voltage-gated K+, CNG, HCN, Piezo, TRP, ENaC/ASIC, GABA-A, glycine, nicotinic acetylcholine, and glutamate receptors.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with generalized anxiety is prescribed a benzodiazepine, which enhances inhibitory chloride current at a specific postsynaptic receptor throughout the brain. A colleague with a spinal cord injury and painful muscle spasms is instead prescribed a different agent that acts at a distinct inhibitory chloride channel concentrated in the spinal cord. Which pair of channel families best matches these two drug targets?","choices":{"A":"Glycine receptors mediate the benzodiazepine's brain-wide inhibitory action, while GABA-A receptors mediate spinal cord inhibition for the spasms","B":"Nicotinic acetylcholine receptors mediate the benzodiazepine's action, while GABA-A receptors mediate the spinal cord inhibitory target","C":"ENaC and ASIC channels mediate the benzodiazepine's action, while ionotropic glutamate receptors mediate the spinal cord target","D":"GABA-A receptors mediate the benzodiazepine action broadly in the brain, while glycine receptors mediate the spinal cord inhibition for spasms","E":"CNG channels mediate the benzodiazepine's action, while HCN channels mediate the spinal cord inhibitory target instead"},"correct":"D","explanation":"GABA-A receptors are the most common inhibitory synaptic chloride channel throughout the central nervous system and are the site of benzodiazepine and barbiturate action, while glycine receptors are the most common inhibitory chloride channel specifically concentrated in the spinal cord, making them the logical target for an antispasticity agent acting at the cord level. Reversing the two receptor assignments, substituting a cation channel such as nicotinic acetylcholine for either inhibitory role, substituting the acid-sensing or glutamate families, or substituting the cyclic nucleotide-gated families all misassign these specific ligand-gated chloride channels.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GABA-A versus glycine is a frequently tested pairing distinguished by anatomic distribution, brain-wide versus spinal cord, and by their classic pharmacologic antagonists, bicuculline versus strychnine. COMLEX: Recognizing glycine receptor blockade by strychnine as the cause of exaggerated reflexes and spasms is a classic toxicology correlate.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 8 — connecting these ligand-gated chloride channel families to other high-yield electrochemical clinical correlates such as Na+ channel toxins","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q11","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Recognize major ion channel families emphasized in Chapter 3, including K2P, voltage-gated Na+, voltage-gated Ca2+, voltage-gated K+, CNG, HCN, Piezo, TRP, ENaC/ASIC, GABA-A, glycine, nicotinic acetylcholine, and glutamate receptors.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with myasthenia gravis has autoantibodies directed against a specific postsynaptic cation channel at the neuromuscular junction, producing fatigable weakness. A separate patient with a seizure disorder is found to have excessive activity at a different postsynaptic cation channel that mediates the majority of fast excitatory transmission in the brain. Which pair of channel families is implicated in these two conditions, respectively?","choices":{"A":"The ionotropic glutamate receptor family is implicated in myasthenia gravis, and the nicotinic acetylcholine receptor in the seizure disorder","B":"The GABA-A receptor is implicated in myasthenia gravis, and the glycine receptor is implicated in the seizure disorder instead","C":"The Piezo channel family is implicated in myasthenia gravis, and the TRP channel family is implicated in the seizure disorder instead","D":"The ENaC and ASIC family is implicated in myasthenia gravis, and the K2P channel family is implicated in the seizure disorder instead","E":"The nicotinic acetylcholine receptor is implicated in myasthenia gravis, and the ionotropic glutamate receptor family is implicated in the seizure disorder"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The nicotinic acetylcholine receptor is the postsynaptic cation channel at the neuromuscular junction targeted by autoantibodies in myasthenia gravis, while ionotropic glutamate receptors, including AMPA, kainate, and NMDA subtypes, mediate the majority of fast excitatory synaptic transmission in the central nervous system and are implicated in excessive excitatory activity during seizures. Reversing the two assignments, substituting the inhibitory GABA-A and glycine chloride channels, substituting the mechanosensitive and thermosensitive Piezo and TRP families, or substituting the acid-sensing and leak potassium families all misassign these two specific excitatory cation channels.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Nicotinic acetylcholine receptor autoimmunity at the neuromuscular junction and glutamate receptor-mediated excitotoxicity in seizures are two of the most heavily tested channelopathy vignettes in this chapter. COMLEX: Curare blocks the same nicotinic receptor implicated in myasthenia gravis, a useful pharmacologic anchor for this family.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 8 — applying electrochemical channel principles to board-relevant conditions, paired here with the classic clinical channelopathies of myasthenia gravis and seizure disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q12","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Recognize major ion channel families emphasized in Chapter 3, including K2P, voltage-gated Na+, voltage-gated Ca2+, voltage-gated K+, CNG, HCN, Piezo, TRP, ENaC/ASIC, GABA-A, glycine, nicotinic acetylcholine, and glutamate receptors.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher applies a mild acidic solution to a sensory nerve terminal and records an inward cation current that depolarizes the fiber, a response distinct from the terminal's mechanically activated stretch current. Which two channel families most plausibly account for these two separate responses, respectively?","choices":{"A":"The Piezo channel family accounts for the acid-evoked current, while the ENaC and ASIC family accounts for the stretch current","B":"The CNG channel family accounts for the acid-evoked current, while the HCN channel family accounts for the mechanically activated stretch current","C":"The ENaC and ASIC family accounts for the acid-evoked current, while Piezo channels account for the separate mechanically activated stretch current","D":"The GABA-A receptor accounts for the acid-evoked current, while the glycine receptor accounts for the mechanically activated stretch current","E":"The voltage-gated calcium channel family accounts for the acid-evoked current, while voltage-gated sodium accounts for the stretch current"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The epithelial sodium channel and acid-sensing ion channel family is directly gated by extracellular protons and produces an inward cation current in response to acidity, while the structurally distinct Piezo channel family is gated by membrane stretch and deformation, so the two currents recorded here arise from two separate, appropriately matched channel families. Reversing the two assignments, substituting the cyclic nucleotide-gated CNG and HCN families, substituting the inhibitory GABA-A and glycine chloride channels, or substituting the voltage-gated calcium and sodium families all misassign the specific stimulus, acid versus mechanical force, that gates each channel.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ASIC channels sensing tissue acidosis are increasingly tested in the context of ischemic pain, while Piezo channels remain the dedicated mechanotransduction family, and distinguishing the two by their activating stimulus is the key skill. COMLEX: ASIC-mediated acid sensing is one proposed mechanism for the pain of ischemic or inflamed tissue at the bedside.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 6 — comparing these specialized sensory channel families with the Aα, Aδ, and C fiber classification that carries their signals centrally","isHighYield":true}],"5":[{"id":"sdl05_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the sequence by which an arriving action potential produces neurotransmitter release at a chemical synapse.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the correct order of events at a chemical synapse after an action potential arrives at the presynaptic terminal?","choices":{"A":"Vesicle fusion, terminal depolarization, Ca2+ influx, opening of voltage-gated Ca2+ channels, and receptor binding","B":"Neurotransmitter exocytosis, Ca2+ influx, terminal depolarization, vesicle fusion, and opening of Ca2+ channels","C":"Opening of voltage-gated Na+ channels, Ca2+ efflux, vesicle fusion, receptor binding, and terminal repolarization","D":"Receptor binding, Ca2+ influx, terminal depolarization, opening of Ca2+ channels, and vesicle fusion","E":"Terminal depolarization, opening of voltage-gated Ca2+ channels, Ca2+ influx, vesicle fusion, and exocytosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The arriving action potential first depolarizes the presynaptic membrane, which opens voltage-gated Ca2+ channels concentrated at the active zone; the resulting Ca2+ influx then triggers vesicle fusion and exocytosis of transmitter into the cleft. The distractors scramble this sequence, place Ca2+ efflux instead of influx, or begin with receptor binding, which is a postsynaptic event that cannot precede presynaptic release.","boardPrep":"Step 1: This four-step sequence, depolarization, channel opening, calcium influx, exocytosis, is the backbone that every synaptic pharmacology vignette builds on. COMLEX: Knowing where a drug or toxin interrupts this sequence is the key to localizing its mechanism.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 5 — explaining threshold, depolarization, repolarization, hyperpolarization, and refractory periods, the electrical event that arrives at the presynaptic terminal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the sequence by which an arriving action potential produces neurotransmitter release at a chemical synapse.","batch":1,"stem":"Presynaptic voltage-gated Ca2+ channels that trigger vesicle fusion are concentrated at which specific structure of the nerve terminal?","choices":{"A":"The axon hillock, the site where summed potentials are integrated to generate an action potential","B":"The node of Ranvier, the exposed axonal segment that regenerates the action potential during conduction","C":"The postsynaptic density, the receptor-rich region on the target cell membrane facing the cleft","D":"The active zone, a specialized region of the presynaptic membrane closely apposed to the postsynaptic density","E":"The axon initial segment, the region with the highest density of voltage-gated Na+ channels"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The active zone is the specialized presynaptic membrane region where synaptic vesicles dock and voltage-gated Ca2+ channels cluster, ensuring that Ca2+ entry occurs immediately adjacent to primed vesicles for fast, precise release. The axon hillock and initial segment are sites of action potential generation rather than release, the node of Ranvier is a conduction structure, and the postsynaptic density is on the receiving side of the synapse.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Active zone anatomy explains why transmitter release is so fast and spatially precise, a detail examiners use to distinguish it from other axonal landmarks. COMLEX: Recognizing structure-function pairings like this one is a recurring pattern across neuroanatomy vignettes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 5 — explaining threshold, depolarization, repolarization, hyperpolarization, and refractory periods, the electrical event that arrives at the presynaptic terminal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the sequence by which an arriving action potential produces neurotransmitter release at a chemical synapse.","batch":1,"stem":"Once neurotransmitter is released into the synaptic cleft, how does it reach the postsynaptic membrane?","choices":{"A":"It is actively transported across the cleft by a dedicated carrier protein embedded in the presynaptic membrane","B":"It diffuses passively across the narrow synaptic cleft to bind receptors on the postsynaptic membrane","C":"It travels through gap junctions that directly connect the presynaptic and postsynaptic cytoplasm","D":"It is repackaged into a new vesicle that buds from the presynaptic membrane and fuses postsynaptically","E":"It requires a second action potential in the presynaptic terminal to be pushed across the cleft"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The synaptic cleft is only about 20 to 30 nanometers wide (Pawlina; Guyton gives 200 to 300 angstroms), narrow enough that released transmitter diffuses passively and rapidly to reach postsynaptic receptors within microseconds to milliseconds. There is no active carrier that shuttles transmitter across the cleft, no gap junction connects most chemical synapses, transmitter is not repackaged into a vesicle for transit, and a second action potential is not required for a single quantum of transmitter to diffuse and bind.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The narrow cleft width is why chemical synapses are fast compared with hormonal signaling despite still requiring diffusion. COMLEX: Because transmitter reaches its receptors by diffusion alone, its action is ended by diffusion away, enzymatic degradation, or reuptake, the clearance steps targeted by many drugs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 5 — explaining threshold, depolarization, repolarization, hyperpolarization, and refractory periods, the electrical event that arrives at the presynaptic terminal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain why presynaptic voltage-gated Ca2+ channels are essential for vesicle fusion and transmitter release.","batch":1,"stem":"Which intracellular protein directly senses the Ca2+ that enters the presynaptic terminal and triggers vesicle fusion?","choices":{"A":"Synaptobrevin, the vesicle membrane SNARE protein that forms the fusion complex","B":"Syntaxin, the target membrane SNARE protein anchored in the presynaptic plasma membrane","C":"SNAP-25, the target membrane SNARE protein that partners with syntaxin","D":"Neurexin, the presynaptic membrane protein that links to postsynaptic neuroligin","E":"Synaptotagmin, a vesicle-bound calcium-sensing protein"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Synaptotagmin is the calcium sensor on the vesicle membrane that binds incoming Ca2+ and then drives the SNARE complex to complete fusion. Synaptobrevin, syntaxin, and SNAP-25 form the SNARE fusion machinery itself but do not directly sense calcium, and neurexin is a trans-synaptic adhesion protein rather than a calcium sensor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Synaptotagmin as the calcium sensor and SNAREs as the fusion machinery is a distinction that shows up whenever botulinum or tetanus toxin mechanisms are tested. COMLEX: Knowing which protein senses calcium versus which protein executes fusion clarifies why calcium channel loss and SNARE cleavage produce the same functional endpoint through different steps.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — describing the roles of voltage-gated Na+ and K+ channels and the Na+/K+ ATPase, the same voltage-gated channel logic extended here to presynaptic Ca2+ channels","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain why presynaptic voltage-gated Ca2+ channels are essential for vesicle fusion and transmitter release.","batch":1,"stem":"A presynaptic terminal stores transmitter in two kinds of vesicles: small clear vesicles holding norepinephrine and large dense-core vesicles holding neuropeptide. A single action potential releases norepinephrine but no peptide. Which stimulus pattern is required to release the neuropeptide?","choices":{"A":"A single spike arriving while the terminal is slightly hyperpolarized","B":"An intense train of action potentials that raises terminal Ca2+ further","C":"Any subthreshold depolarization that opens Na+ channels in the terminal","D":"A single spike delivered after presynaptic Ca2+ channels are blocked","E":"Binding of the released norepinephrine to postsynaptic beta receptors"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Release of small clear vesicles is regulated by Ca2+, and a single action potential raises terminal Ca2+ enough to trigger their exocytosis. Dense-core vesicles exocytose only after an intense period of stimulation, a train of action potentials, which builds Ca2+ to the higher level needed for their release. A single spike on a hyperpolarized terminal adds no extra Ca2+. A subthreshold depolarization does not open enough voltage-gated Ca2+ channels to drive release at all, and blocking presynaptic Ca2+ channels abolishes release of both vesicle types. Postsynaptic beta receptors act on the target cell and do not trigger presynaptic peptide release.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Small clear vesicles carry small-molecule transmitters and release with single spikes; dense-core vesicles carry neuropeptides and need high-frequency trains. COMLEX: Peptide co-transmitters such as substance P are released mainly during intense nociceptor firing, which helps explain why they shape sustained pain states.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — describing the roles of voltage-gated channels, here the presynaptic Ca2+ channels whose summed opening during a spike train sets which vesicles fuse","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain why presynaptic voltage-gated Ca2+ channels are essential for vesicle fusion and transmitter release.","batch":1,"stem":"If presynaptic voltage-gated Ca2+ channels are completely blocked while the presynaptic action potential still occurs normally, what is the expected result?","choices":{"A":"Vesicle fusion and neurotransmitter release fail almost entirely despite normal terminal depolarization","B":"Neurotransmitter release proceeds normally because SNARE proteins fuse vesicles independent of calcium entry","C":"Release is enhanced because reduced calcium buffering allows spontaneous vesicle fusion to increase","D":"Only enzymatic degradation of transmitter in the cleft is affected while release itself remains unchanged","E":"Postsynaptic receptor sensitivity increases to compensate for the loss of presynaptic calcium entry"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because Ca2+ entry through presynaptic voltage-gated channels is the obligatory trigger for synaptotagmin-mediated vesicle fusion, blocking these channels abolishes release even though the action potential itself, and thus terminal depolarization, remains intact. SNARE proteins cannot complete fusion without the calcium signal, release is not enhanced by channel blockade, cleft enzymes are unrelated to the release step, and postsynaptic receptor sensitivity does not compensate for a presynaptic release failure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: This is the exact physiologic logic behind Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome, where autoantibodies destroy presynaptic calcium channels. COMLEX: Predicting the functional consequence of an isolated channel defect, without other steps failing, is a recurring reasoning pattern on integrated exams.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — describing the roles of voltage-gated Na+ and K+ channels and the Na+/K+ ATPase, the same voltage-gated channel logic extended here to presynaptic Ca2+ channels","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare fast ionotropic receptor signaling with slower metabotropic receptor signaling.","batch":1,"stem":"What structurally distinguishes an ionotropic receptor from a metabotropic receptor?","choices":{"A":"The metabotropic receptor's ligand-binding site is part of the same protein that forms the ion channel pore","B":"Ionotropic receptors are always intracellular while metabotropic receptors are always on the cell surface","C":"Metabotropic receptors contain four transmembrane domains arranged around a central conducting pore","D":"The ionotropic receptor's ligand-binding site is part of the same multimeric protein that forms the ion channel pore","E":"Ionotropic receptors require a heterotrimeric G protein intermediate to open their conducting pore"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ionotropic receptors are ligand-gated ion channels in which the neurotransmitter binding site and the channel pore are part of one multimeric protein, so binding directly and rapidly opens the channel. Metabotropic receptors are seven-transmembrane G protein-coupled receptors that lack an intrinsic pore and instead signal through a separate G protein intermediate, the reverse of the first distractor's claim; the remaining distractors misstate receptor location and structure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The single-protein-pore-plus-binding-site design of ionotropic receptors is why they open in a fraction of a millisecond, a fact tested against the multistep G protein cascade of metabotropic receptors. COMLEX: This structural distinction underlies why nicotinic acetylcholine receptor drugs act instantly while muscarinic drugs act over seconds.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — distinguishing graded potentials from action potentials, a fast versus slow signaling contrast that parallels ionotropic versus metabotropic transmission","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare fast ionotropic receptor signaling with slower metabotropic receptor signaling.","batch":1,"stem":"Why do metabotropic receptor responses last much longer than ionotropic receptor responses, even though ionotropic responses begin faster?","choices":{"A":"Metabotropic receptors physically remain bound to the neurotransmitter for tens of seconds after initial binding","B":"Ionotropic receptors are degraded immediately after channel opening, artificially shortening their response duration","C":"Metabotropic receptors use the same ion channel pore repeatedly, extending the response through repeated opening","D":"Ionotropic receptor channels remain open continuously once triggered, regardless of transmitter concentration","E":"Metabotropic receptors activate G proteins whose diffusible second messengers sustain and amplify signaling"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Metabotropic signaling engages a G protein that can activate enzymes such as adenylyl cyclase or phospholipase C, producing diffusible second messengers like cAMP or IP3 that amplify and prolong the signal for seconds to minutes, or even trigger long-term transcriptional changes. Ionotropic channels open and close in a fraction of a millisecond because opening is a direct, non-amplified conformational event, not because of prolonged transmitter binding, receptor degradation, or continuous channel opening.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Second-messenger amplification is the mechanistic reason metabotropic signaling outlasts ionotropic signaling by orders of magnitude. COMLEX: This concept explains the delayed but sustained onset of drugs acting on G protein-coupled receptors compared with drugs acting on ligand-gated channels.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — distinguishing graded potentials from action potentials, a fast versus slow signaling contrast that parallels ionotropic versus metabotropic transmission","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare fast ionotropic receptor signaling with slower metabotropic receptor signaling.","batch":1,"stem":"At the neuromuscular junction, acetylcholine acts on nicotinic receptors to produce a fast end-plate potential. Which receptor property makes this response so rapid?","choices":{"A":"The nicotinic receptor triggers a G protein cascade that opens a separate, nearby cation channel","B":"The nicotinic receptor generates cyclic AMP, which then phosphorylates and opens the muscle cation channel","C":"The nicotinic receptor is itself a ligand-gated cation channel that opens directly upon acetylcholine binding","D":"The nicotinic receptor requires calcium release from intracellular stores before the muscle membrane can depolarize","E":"The nicotinic receptor first activates phospholipase C before any change in membrane conductance can occur"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Nicotinic acetylcholine receptors are ionotropic ligand-gated cation channels, so acetylcholine binding directly opens the channel without any intermediate signaling step, producing the characteristically fast end-plate potential. The distractors all describe metabotropic-style intermediate steps, G protein cascades, cAMP generation, intracellular calcium release, or phospholipase C activation, none of which apply to the fast ionotropic nicotinic receptor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The neuromuscular junction is the textbook example of ionotropic speed, and confusing nicotinic with muscarinic signaling is a common wrong-answer trap. COMLEX: Muscarinic receptors, by contrast, are the slower metabotropic acetylcholine receptor family found at parasympathetic targets and in the CNS.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — distinguishing graded potentials from action potentials, a fast versus slow signaling contrast that parallels ionotropic versus metabotropic transmission","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish EPSPs from IPSPs and explain temporal and spatial summation.","batch":1,"stem":"Binding of glutamate to a postsynaptic AMPA receptor opens a cation channel that depolarizes the cell slightly. What is this localized change in membrane potential called?","choices":{"A":"An excitatory postsynaptic potential, produced by Na+ influx that brings the membrane closer to threshold","B":"An inhibitory postsynaptic potential, produced by Na+ influx that moves the membrane further from threshold","C":"An action potential, produced directly at the postsynaptic membrane without any further summation required","D":"A refractory potential, produced transiently while the postsynaptic membrane is temporarily unable to respond","E":"A resting potential shift, produced by simultaneous closure of postsynaptic K+ leak channels alone"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Glutamate opening a cation channel allows Na+ influx that partially depolarizes the postsynaptic membrane, defining an excitatory postsynaptic potential, or EPSP, which brings the cell closer to but not necessarily to the threshold needed for an action potential. Labeling this an IPSP misassigns the direction of the potential change, calling it an action potential ignores that a single EPSP is far too small to fire one, and the remaining distractors describe unrelated or incorrect phenomena.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A single EPSP is typically only 0.5 to 1.0 millivolts, well below threshold, which is exactly why summation is required and heavily tested. COMLEX: Recognizing an EPSP as a graded, localized event rather than an all-or-none action potential is fundamental to interpreting synaptic physiology vignettes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — explaining how ion concentration gradients and membrane permeability generate the resting membrane potential, the same ionic-gradient logic that produces EPSPs and IPSPs","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish EPSPs from IPSPs and explain temporal and spatial summation.","batch":1,"stem":"GABA binding to a postsynaptic GABA-A receptor opens a chloride channel. What effect does this have on the postsynaptic membrane potential?","choices":{"A":"Cl- efflux depolarizes the membrane, producing an excitatory postsynaptic potential that moves the cell toward threshold","B":"Cl- influx hyperpolarizes the membrane, producing an inhibitory postsynaptic potential that moves the cell away from threshold","C":"Na+ influx through the same channel depolarizes the membrane, producing a strong excitatory postsynaptic potential","D":"K+ efflux through the same channel repolarizes the membrane back to its resting value without net inhibition","E":"Cl- influx depolarizes the membrane transiently before a delayed hyperpolarizing phase begins several seconds later"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because extracellular Cl- concentration exceeds intracellular Cl- concentration in mature neurons, opening a GABA-A chloride channel allows Cl- to flow into the cell, making the interior more negative and producing an inhibitory postsynaptic potential, or IPSP, that moves the membrane further from the firing threshold. The distractors describe the wrong ion, the wrong direction of flow, or an incorrect delayed time course, none of which match GABA-A receptor physiology.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IPSPs from Cl- influx are the physiologic basis for benzodiazepine and barbiturate action at GABA-A receptors, both of which enhance this same hyperpolarizing current. COMLEX: Understanding the ionic basis of the IPSP explains why GABA-A positive allosteric modulators are sedating and anticonvulsant.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — explaining how ion concentration gradients and membrane permeability generate the resting membrane potential, the same ionic-gradient logic that produces EPSPs and IPSPs","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish EPSPs from IPSPs and explain temporal and spatial summation.","batch":1,"stem":"A single presynaptic terminal fires several action potentials in rapid succession, and the resulting postsynaptic potentials build upon one another because each one lasts up to 15 milliseconds. What is this phenomenon called?","choices":{"A":"Spatial summation, in which potentials from multiple terminals at different locations add together simultaneously","B":"Synaptic facilitation, in which the postsynaptic receptor becomes progressively more sensitive with each stimulus","C":"Long-term potentiation, in which repeated stimulation permanently strengthens the synapse over days to weeks","D":"Receptor desensitization, in which repeated exposure to transmitter progressively reduces the postsynaptic response","E":"Temporal summation, in which successive potentials from one terminal add together over time"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Temporal summation occurs when a single presynaptic terminal fires rapidly enough that each postsynaptic potential, which persists for several milliseconds, has not yet decayed before the next one arrives, allowing them to add together over time and potentially reach threshold. Spatial summation instead involves multiple different terminals firing simultaneously, synaptic facilitation and long-term potentiation describe distinct plasticity phenomena on different timescales, and desensitization describes a reduction rather than a summation of response.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Temporal versus spatial summation is one of the most consistently tested distinctions in synaptic physiology, defined by one terminal over time versus many terminals at once. COMLEX: Both summation types converge on the same principle, that the axon hillock integrates all incoming graded potentials to decide whether threshold is reached.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — explaining how ion concentration gradients and membrane permeability generate the resting membrane potential, the same ionic-gradient logic that produces EPSPs and IPSPs","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the major neurotransmitter classes and their common receptor families.","batch":1,"stem":"Which neurotransmitter is the primary excitatory transmitter in the central nervous system and acts on both ionotropic and metabotropic receptor families?","choices":{"A":"GABA, which binds GABA-A ionotropic chloride channels as well as metabotropic GABA-B receptors","B":"Glycine, which binds an ionotropic chloride channel found mainly in the spinal cord and lower brainstem","C":"Glutamate, which binds AMPA, NMDA, and kainate ionotropic receptors as well as metabotropic mGluRs","D":"Acetylcholine, which binds ionotropic nicotinic receptors as well as metabotropic muscarinic receptors","E":"Dopamine, which binds exclusively metabotropic D1 through D5 receptor family members"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Glutamate is the dominant excitatory transmitter in the brain, acting at ionotropic AMPA, kainate, and NMDA receptors for fast excitation and at metabotropic mGluRs for slower modulation. GABA and glycine are inhibitory rather than excitatory, acetylcholine does act on both receptor classes but is not the primary CNS excitatory transmitter, and dopamine acts exclusively through metabotropic receptors with no ionotropic family.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Knowing that glutamate is the transmitter at about 90% of excitatory synapses in the brain (SDL 5–6 reading) anchors nearly every excitotoxicity and antiseizure drug question. COMLEX: Its dual ionotropic and metabotropic receptor repertoire distinguishes it from single-family transmitters like glycine.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 → Obj 2 — comparing sympathetic, parasympathetic, and enteric divisions by neurotransmitter, previewing how these same transmitter classes organize autonomic output","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the major neurotransmitter classes and their common receptor families.","batch":1,"stem":"Acetylcholine is unusual among small-molecule neurotransmitters because it acts on two structurally distinct receptor families. What are they?","choices":{"A":"Ionotropic nicotinic receptors and metabotropic muscarinic receptors","B":"Ionotropic muscarinic receptors and metabotropic nicotinic receptors","C":"Ionotropic AMPA receptors and metabotropic NMDA receptors","D":"Ionotropic GABA-A receptors and metabotropic GABA-B receptors","E":"Ionotropic P2X purinergic receptors and metabotropic P2Y purinergic receptors"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Acetylcholine acts on ionotropic nicotinic receptors, found at the neuromuscular junction and autonomic ganglia, and on metabotropic muscarinic receptors, M1 through M5, found at parasympathetic targets and throughout the CNS, giving it both a fast and a slow signaling arm. The distractors reverse the nicotinic and muscarinic pairing or substitute unrelated glutamate, GABA, or purinergic receptor families that are not acetylcholine receptors at all.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Nicotinic versus muscarinic receptor pharmacology is among the most heavily tested receptor pairs, underlying atropine, succinylcholine, and organophosphate toxicity questions alike. COMLEX: Remembering nicotinic as fast ionotropic and muscarinic as slow metabotropic resolves most acetylcholine drug mechanism vignettes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 → Obj 2 — comparing sympathetic, parasympathetic, and enteric divisions by neurotransmitter, previewing how these same transmitter classes organize autonomic output","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the major neurotransmitter classes and their common receptor families.","batch":1,"stem":"Which group of transmitters includes norepinephrine, dopamine, and serotonin, all of which signal predominantly through metabotropic G protein-coupled receptors?","choices":{"A":"The amino acid transmitters, which include glutamate, GABA, and glycine","B":"The neuropeptides, which include the opioids and vasoactive intestinal polypeptide","C":"The purines, which include adenosine triphosphate and adenosine","D":"The monoamines, also called biogenic amines","E":"The gas transmitters, which include nitric oxide and carbon monoxide"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Norepinephrine, dopamine, and serotonin together make up the monoamine, or biogenic amine, class of transmitters, and with the exception of the ionotropic 5-HT3 receptor, all of their receptors are metabotropic G protein-coupled receptors. Amino acid transmitters, neuropeptides, purines, and gas transmitters are each distinct chemical classes with different synthesis, storage, and receptor characteristics from the monoamines.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Monoamine classification underlies the mechanism of essentially every antidepressant, antipsychotic, and stimulant drug tested on boards. COMLEX: The single ionotropic exception, 5-HT3, is worth memorizing because it explains why some antiemetics act instantly compared with the days-long onset of most monoamine-targeted drugs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 → Obj 2 — comparing sympathetic, parasympathetic, and enteric divisions by neurotransmitter, previewing how these same transmitter classes organize autonomic output","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain how synaptic signaling is terminated by reuptake, enzymatic degradation, diffusion, or metabolic conversion.","batch":1,"stem":"Which termination mechanism is primarily responsible for clearing dopamine, norepinephrine, and serotonin from the synaptic cleft?","choices":{"A":"Enzymatic hydrolysis in the synaptic cleft by acetylcholinesterase acting on each monoamine","B":"Passive diffusion alone, since no dedicated transporter exists for any of these three transmitters","C":"Reuptake through dedicated presynaptic transporters, DAT, NET, and SERT respectively","D":"Metabolic conversion by astrocyte glutamine synthetase into an inactive amino acid derivative","E":"Active transport directly into the postsynaptic cell followed by immediate lysosomal degradation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Dopamine, norepinephrine, and serotonin are each cleared primarily by reuptake through their own high-affinity presynaptic transporters, DAT, NET, and SERT, which return the transmitter to the terminal for repackaging or degradation. Acetylcholinesterase hydrolyzes acetylcholine, not monoamines, passive diffusion is only a minor contributor, glutamine synthetase acts on glutamate and GABA rather than monoamines, and postsynaptic lysosomal degradation is not the primary clearance route.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DAT, NET, and SERT are the direct pharmacologic targets of cocaine, amphetamines, and SSRIs, making this mechanism essential background for that entire drug class. COMLEX: Reuptake transporter specificity explains why SSRIs selectively raise serotonin without markedly affecting dopamine.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — describing the Na+/K+ ATPase as an active-transport pump that restores ionic gradients, the same active-transport logic used by reuptake transporters to terminate signaling","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain how synaptic signaling is terminated by reuptake, enzymatic degradation, diffusion, or metabolic conversion.","batch":1,"stem":"Acetylcholine signaling at the neuromuscular junction is terminated primarily by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Rapid enzymatic hydrolysis by acetylcholinesterase located in the synaptic cleft","B":"Reuptake through a dedicated presynaptic acetylcholine transporter analogous to DAT or SERT","C":"Simple diffusion out of the cleft, since no clearance enzyme is present at the neuromuscular junction","D":"Conversion by astrocyte glutamine synthetase into an inactive glutamine derivative","E":"Active transport into the postsynaptic muscle cell followed by lysosomal breakdown"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Acetylcholinesterase, anchored within the depths of the synaptic cleft at the neuromuscular junction, rapidly hydrolyzes acetylcholine into choline and acetate, ensuring that transmitter action lasts only about one millisecond. Intact acetylcholine itself is not taken back up by a dedicated transporter, diffusion is not the primary clearance route given the enzyme's high local concentration, glutamine synthetase acts on glutamate and GABA, and there is no postsynaptic lysosomal pathway for acetylcholine clearance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Acetylcholinesterase inhibition is the shared mechanistic thread linking myasthenia gravis treatment, organophosphate toxicity, and nerve agent poisoning. COMLEX: The choline byproduct of hydrolysis is recycled by high-affinity choline transporters back into the presynaptic terminal for new acetylcholine synthesis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — describing the Na+/K+ ATPase as an active-transport pump that restores ionic gradients, the same active-transport logic used by reuptake transporters to terminate signaling","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain how synaptic signaling is terminated by reuptake, enzymatic degradation, diffusion, or metabolic conversion.","batch":1,"stem":"Glutamate released at a synapse is largely cleared by astrocyte uptake and converted into an inert compound before being recycled to the presynaptic neuron. What is this termination pathway called?","choices":{"A":"Reuptake, in which glutamate is transported directly back into the presynaptic terminal without glial involvement","B":"Enzymatic degradation, in which glutamate is hydrolyzed into inactive fragments within the synaptic cleft itself","C":"Diffusion, in which glutamate simply drifts away from the cleft into the surrounding interstitial fluid","D":"The glutamate-glutamine cycle, a form of metabolic conversion carried out by glial cells","E":"Autoreceptor feedback, in which presynaptic receptors halt further glutamate release without clearing existing transmitter"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Astrocytes take up synaptic glutamate via EAAT transporters and convert it to glutamine using glutamine synthetase, consuming toxic ammonia in the process; this synaptically inert glutamine is then shuttled back to the neuron and reconverted to glutamate, defining the glutamate-glutamine cycle as a glial metabolic conversion pathway. Direct presynaptic reuptake without glial involvement, cleft enzymatic hydrolysis, simple diffusion, and autoreceptor feedback each describe different or incomplete mechanisms that do not capture this glial recycling loop.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The glutamate-glutamine cycle is a distinct, glia-dependent termination pathway that examiners contrast with the simpler monoamine reuptake model. COMLEX: This pathway's dependence on ammonia handling links directly to hepatic encephalopathy, where impaired ammonia clearance disrupts astrocyte glutamate handling.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — describing the Na+/K+ ATPase as an active-transport pump that restores ionic gradients, the same active-transport logic used by reuptake transporters to terminate signaling","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q19","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Apply synaptic mechanisms to board-relevant diseases, drugs, and toxins including myasthenia gravis, Lambert-Eaton syndrome, botulism, tetanus, organophosphate toxicity, seizures, Parkinson disease, antipsychotics, SSRIs, benzodiazepines, and barbiturates.","batch":1,"stem":"In myasthenia gravis, autoantibodies target which structure, and what is the resulting mechanism of muscle weakness?","choices":{"A":"Postsynaptic nicotinic acetylcholine receptors, which are cross-linked and degraded, reducing their number","B":"Presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels, which are destroyed, preventing acetylcholine release entirely","C":"Presynaptic SNARE fusion proteins, which are cleaved, blocking acetylcholine vesicle exocytosis","D":"Acetylcholinesterase enzyme molecules, which are inactivated, causing acetylcholine to accumulate excessively","E":"Postsynaptic muscarinic receptors, which are blocked, preventing parasympathetic activation of skeletal muscle"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Myasthenia gravis is caused by autoantibodies against postsynaptic nicotinic acetylcholine receptors at the neuromuscular junction, which cross-link the receptors and accelerate their endocytosis and destruction, reducing the number available to generate an adequate end-plate potential. Presynaptic calcium channel or SNARE protein loss instead describes Lambert-Eaton syndrome or botulism, acetylcholinesterase inactivation describes organophosphate toxicity, and skeletal muscle is controlled by nicotinic, not muscarinic, receptors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Myasthenia gravis as a postsynaptic nicotinic receptor antibody disease, worsening with repeated use, is a defining Step 1 pairing against Lambert-Eaton's opposite pattern. First Aid mnemonic for the order of the two lesions in the release pathway: L comes before M, Lambert-Eaton's presynaptic calcium channel step precedes myasthenia gravis's postsynaptic receptor step. COMLEX: Edrophonium and pyridostigmine improve strength by inhibiting acetylcholinesterase to flood the cleft with acetylcholine, compensating for fewer receptors.","crossRef":"📎 Differentiating upper motor neuron, lower motor neuron, neuromuscular junction, and myopathic patterns of weakness, the exam framework into which NMJ diseases like myasthenia gravis fit","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q20","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Apply synaptic mechanisms to board-relevant diseases, drugs, and toxins including myasthenia gravis, Lambert-Eaton syndrome, botulism, tetanus, organophosphate toxicity, seizures, Parkinson disease, antipsychotics, SSRIs, benzodiazepines, and barbiturates.","batch":1,"stem":"Botulinum toxin and tetanus toxin share an identical initial molecular mechanism but produce opposite clinical presentations. What accounts for this difference?","choices":{"A":"Botulinum toxin blocks postsynaptic receptors while tetanus toxin blocks presynaptic calcium channels instead","B":"Botulinum toxin causes spastic paralysis directly while tetanus toxin causes flaccid paralysis directly","C":"Botulinum toxin acts only on autonomic neurons while tetanus toxin acts only on skeletal motor neurons","D":"Botulinum toxin destroys acetylcholinesterase while tetanus toxin destroys monoamine oxidase in the same neurons","E":"Botulinum toxin cleaves SNAREs at motor terminals while tetanus toxin cleaves them in spinal inhibitory interneurons"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Both toxins are bacterial metalloproteinases that cleave SNARE fusion proteins, but botulinum toxin acts locally at the neuromuscular junction to block acetylcholine release and cause flaccid paralysis, whereas tetanus toxin travels retrogradely up the axon to the spinal cord, where it disables inhibitory interneurons that normally restrain motor neurons, producing spastic paralysis instead. The distractors misassign the target structures or enzymes, and the option giving botulinum spastic and tetanus flaccid paralysis reverses the correct pairing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Same enzyme family, same substrate, opposite clinical picture, purely because of where the toxin ends up, is a classic higher-order reasoning question. COMLEX: Risus sardonicus appears first because short-axon face and jaw motor neurons are reached early; opisthotonus follows as toxin reaches longer-axon trunk and limb motor pools.","crossRef":"📎 Differentiating upper motor neuron, lower motor neuron, neuromuscular junction, and myopathic patterns of weakness, the exam framework into which NMJ diseases like myasthenia gravis fit","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q21","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Apply synaptic mechanisms to board-relevant diseases, drugs, and toxins including myasthenia gravis, Lambert-Eaton syndrome, botulism, tetanus, organophosphate toxicity, seizures, Parkinson disease, antipsychotics, SSRIs, benzodiazepines, and barbiturates.","batch":1,"stem":"Organophosphate compounds, such as those found in certain pesticides and nerve agents, cause toxicity through which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Reversible competitive antagonism of postsynaptic nicotinic receptors, blocking acetylcholine from binding","B":"Selective destruction of presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels at cholinergic nerve terminals only","C":"Irreversible acetylcholinesterase inhibition, so acetylcholine floods muscarinic and nicotinic synapses","D":"Direct activation of postsynaptic GABA-A receptors, producing profound central nervous system sedation","E":"Selective inhibition of monoamine oxidase, causing toxic accumulation of dopamine and norepinephrine"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Organophosphates irreversibly inhibit acetylcholinesterase, allowing acetylcholine to accumulate massively at both muscarinic and nicotinic synapses throughout the body, producing the classic autonomic overstimulation and eventual neuromuscular paralysis of organophosphate toxicity. The other options describe unrelated mechanisms, reversible nicotinic antagonism, calcium channel destruction, GABA-A activation, or monoamine oxidase inhibition, none of which characterize organophosphate pharmacology.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SLUDGE and the muscarinic-plus-nicotinic overstimulation picture are the signature clinical presentation tied to irreversible AChE inhibition. COMLEX: Atropine is the first-line antidote because it blocks the excess acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors, though it does not reverse nicotinic overstimulation at the neuromuscular junction.","crossRef":"📎 Differentiating upper motor neuron, lower motor neuron, neuromuscular junction, and myopathic patterns of weakness, the exam framework into which NMJ diseases like myasthenia gravis fit","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q22","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use a mechanism-based algorithm to solve clinical questions involving chemical signaling.","batch":1,"stem":"When applying a mechanism-based algorithm to a synaptic pharmacology vignette, what is the first question that should be answered?","choices":{"A":"Whether the net clinical effect is excitatory or inhibitory, before any other feature of the case is considered","B":"Whether the lesion is presynaptic, at the synaptic cleft, or postsynaptic, before the transmitter is identified","C":"Which specific antidote should be administered, before the underlying mechanism has been established","D":"Which synapse and transmitter system is involved, such as the neuromuscular junction or a central pathway","E":"Whether the patient's symptoms began acutely or gradually, independent of which synapse is affected"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The first step of the mechanism-based algorithm is identifying the synapse and transmitter system at play, nicotinic or muscarinic acetylcholine, adrenergic, or a central pathway such as glutamate, GABA, or dopamine, because every subsequent step depends on knowing which system is being disrupted. Determining net excitatory versus inhibitory effect and localizing the lesion within the synaptic sequence are later steps that require the transmitter system to already be identified, and choosing an antidote or focusing on symptom onset skips the necessary mechanistic reasoning entirely.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Working systematically from transmitter identity, to lesion location, to net effect, to clinical correlation prevents jumping to a diagnosis prematurely. COMLEX: This stepwise habit mirrors the localization-first approach used throughout the neurologic examination.","crossRef":"📎 Constructing a concise neurologic localization statement from a clinical vignette, the same stepwise deductive habit applied here to synaptic pharmacology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q23","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use a mechanism-based algorithm to solve clinical questions involving chemical signaling.","batch":1,"stem":"After identifying the transmitter system involved in a synaptic pharmacology vignette, the next algorithm step is localizing the lesion. Which three locations should be systematically considered?","choices":{"A":"The axon hillock, the node of Ranvier, and the myelin sheath surrounding the presynaptic axon","B":"The cell body, the dendritic tree, and the nucleus of the presynaptic neuron only","C":"Presynaptic release machinery, the synaptic cleft clearance mechanism, and the postsynaptic receptor","D":"The blood-brain barrier, the choroid plexus, and the cerebrospinal fluid circulation pathway","E":"The dorsal root ganglion, the anterior horn cell, and the peripheral sensory receptor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The algorithm's second step systematically checks three synaptic locations: the presynaptic release machinery, such as calcium channels or SNARE proteins, the synaptic cleft clearance mechanisms, such as degrading enzymes or reuptake transporters, and the postsynaptic receptor, which may be blocked, destroyed, or allosterically modulated. The distractors substitute unrelated axonal conduction structures, cell body components, cerebrospinal fluid anatomy, or sensory pathway structures that are not part of the synaptic transmission sequence itself.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sorting a drug or disease into presynaptic, cleft, or postsynaptic categories is the single most useful organizing habit for this entire topic. COMLEX: This three-part localization scheme applies equally well whether the synapse under discussion is central or peripheral.","crossRef":"📎 Constructing a concise neurologic localization statement from a clinical vignette, the same stepwise deductive habit applied here to synaptic pharmacology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q24","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use a mechanism-based algorithm to solve clinical questions involving chemical signaling.","batch":1,"stem":"The final step of the mechanism-based algorithm asks the student to match the net electrochemical effect to a clinical presentation. Which pairing correctly illustrates this final step?","choices":{"A":"Excess EPSPs in the cerebral cortex are matched to flaccid paralysis as the expected clinical presentation","B":"Excess EPSPs in the cerebral cortex are matched to seizure activity as the expected clinical presentation","C":"Reduced EPSPs at the neuromuscular junction are matched to seizure activity as the expected clinical presentation","D":"Excess IPSPs in the cerebral cortex are matched to tachycardia and hypertension as the expected clinical presentation","E":"Reduced IPSPs at the neuromuscular junction are matched to profound sedation as the expected clinical presentation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The final algorithm step synthesizes the net effect with anatomic location: excess excitatory drive, meaning excess EPSPs, in the cerebral cortex produces hyperexcitability and seizures, while reduced EPSPs at the neuromuscular junction instead produce flaccid paralysis and excess sympathetic receptor activation produces tachycardia and hypertension. The distractors mismatch the excitatory or inhibitory direction with the wrong anatomic location or clinical outcome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: This final matching step is where all the earlier reasoning pays off, converting a mechanism into a predictable bedside finding. COMLEX: Practicing this pairing across multiple locations, cortex, neuromuscular junction, and autonomic targets, builds flexibility for varied vignette settings.","crossRef":"📎 Constructing a concise neurologic localization statement from a clinical vignette, the same stepwise deductive habit applied here to synaptic pharmacology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q25","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the sequence by which an arriving action potential produces neurotransmitter release at a chemical synapse.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher applies a toxin to an isolated nerve terminal preparation and finds that action potentials still arrive normally and depolarize the terminal, but no vesicles fuse with the plasma membrane and no neurotransmitter is detected in the bath. Which step in the normal sequence of neurotransmitter release is most likely disrupted?","choices":{"A":"The initial arrival and conduction of the action potential into the presynaptic terminal itself","B":"The step linking presynaptic Ca2+ entry to vesicle fusion, since depolarization is intact","C":"The diffusion of neurotransmitter across the synaptic cleft once it has already been released","D":"The binding of neurotransmitter to postsynaptic receptors on the target cell membrane","E":"The generation of the resting membrane potential in the presynaptic neuron before any stimulus arrives"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because the action potential still arrives and depolarizes the terminal normally, the defect must lie downstream of depolarization but upstream of the diffusion and binding steps, precisely the calcium-triggered fusion step that links electrical depolarization to vesicle exocytosis. Impaired action potential arrival or resting potential generation would prevent depolarization itself, which is stated to be normal, and cleft diffusion or receptor binding cannot be evaluated because no transmitter is ever released to begin with.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vignettes that describe normal depolarization but absent release are testing whether students can localize the defect specifically to the calcium-triggered fusion step. COMLEX: This reasoning pattern, isolating which link in a known sequence has failed, is the same deductive skill used throughout neurologic localization.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 5 — the depolarization, threshold, and repolarization mechanics that generate the action potential arriving at this terminal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q26","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the sequence by which an arriving action potential produces neurotransmitter release at a chemical synapse.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old graduate student is studying synaptic transmission in a model synapse. She applies a drug that prevents the fusion pore from ever forming between the vesicle membrane and the presynaptic plasma membrane, even though calcium enters the terminal normally and synaptotagmin binds it appropriately. Which downstream component of the release sequence is most likely being blocked?","choices":{"A":"The voltage-gated calcium channel that allows extracellular calcium to enter the presynaptic terminal","B":"The initial depolarization of the presynaptic membrane produced by the arriving action potential","C":"The SNARE complex assembly of synaptobrevin, syntaxin, and SNAP-25 that completes membrane fusion","D":"The diffusion of neurotransmitter across the synaptic cleft toward the postsynaptic membrane","E":"The postsynaptic receptor that would normally bind neurotransmitter once it reaches the target cell"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Since calcium entry and synaptotagmin binding are both stated to be intact, the defect must be downstream of calcium sensing, at the level of the SNARE complex itself, which physically zippers the vesicle and plasma membranes together to form the fusion pore. Calcium channel or depolarization defects would prevent calcium entry from occurring at all, which is explicitly not the case here, and cleft diffusion or postsynaptic receptor function are irrelevant because no transmitter is ever released for those steps to apply.","boardPrep":"Step 1: This is precisely the mechanism botulinum and tetanus toxins exploit, cleaving SNARE proteins downstream of an intact calcium signal. COMLEX: Recognizing that a normal upstream signal with a blocked downstream event points to the fusion machinery itself is a transferable diagnostic habit.","crossRef":"📖 Differentiating neuromuscular junction weakness patterns from upper motor neuron, lower motor neuron, and myopathic patterns, the exam framework this fusion defect would ultimately present within","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q27","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the sequence by which an arriving action potential produces neurotransmitter release at a chemical synapse.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher studies a neuromuscular junction exposed to a toxin that blocks clathrin-mediated endocytosis but leaves action potentials, Ca2+ entry, and SNARE-mediated fusion intact. During continuous stimulation, end-plate potentials are normal at first, then shrink steadily until transmission fails. Which of the following best explains this time course?","choices":{"A":"Acetylcholinesterase accumulates in the cleft and degrades transmitter faster with each stimulus","B":"Postsynaptic nicotinic receptors desensitize permanently after their first exposure to transmitter","C":"Presynaptic voltage-gated Ca2+ channels inactivate during the first seconds of stimulation","D":"Synaptotagmin loses its Ca2+ sensitivity after repeated binding during the stimulus train","E":"Fused vesicle membrane cannot be retrieved and refilled, so the releasable pool runs out"},"correct":"E","explanation":"After full fusion, NSF dissociates the SNARE complex and the vesicle membrane is retrieved by clathrin-mediated endocytosis; the new empty vesicles are refilled with transmitter and returned to the synaptic store. If retrieval is blocked, release proceeds normally from the vesicles already docked and stored, but each round of exocytosis uses up vesicles that cannot be replaced, so end-plate potentials shrink as the pool is depleted. Acetylcholinesterase is anchored in the cleft and does not build up with stimulation. Receptor desensitization, Ca2+ channel inactivation, and loss of synaptotagmin sensitivity are excluded by the stated normal Ca2+ entry and fusion machinery, and none would produce a steady decline tied to vesicle use.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The vesicle cycle is docking, Ca2+-triggered fusion, clathrin-mediated retrieval, refilling, and return to the pool; a defect late in the cycle causes use-dependent rundown. COMLEX: A decremental response on repetitive nerve stimulation means less transmitter is available with each impulse, whatever the cause.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 4 — distinguishing graded potentials from action potentials, since the end-plate potential that shrinks here is a graded response to the quantity of transmitter released","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q28","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain why presynaptic voltage-gated Ca2+ channels are essential for vesicle fusion and transmitter release.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man is treated with intravenous gentamicin, an aminoglycoside antibiotic, for a severe gram-negative infection. Several hours into the infusion he develops worsening generalized muscle weakness, and his family notes he has a history of mild, previously undiagnosed myasthenia gravis. Which pharmacologic property of aminoglycosides best explains why they can precipitate a myasthenic crisis in a susceptible patient?","choices":{"A":"Aminoglycosides irreversibly inhibit acetylcholinesterase, causing excess acetylcholine to accumulate and desensitize remaining receptors","B":"Aminoglycosides bind postsynaptic nicotinic receptors as competitive antagonists, directly blocking acetylcholine binding","C":"Aminoglycosides partially inhibit presynaptic voltage-gated Ca2+ channels, further reducing the already-limited acetylcholine release in myasthenia","D":"Aminoglycosides cleave presynaptic SNARE proteins, mechanically preventing acetylcholine vesicle fusion entirely","E":"Aminoglycosides increase acetylcholine reuptake into the presynaptic terminal, lowering effective cleft concentration"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Aminoglycoside antibiotics have a known presynaptic side effect of partially blocking voltage-gated Ca2+ channels at the neuromuscular junction, which reduces Ca2+-dependent acetylcholine release; in a patient whose postsynaptic receptor pool is already reduced by myasthenia gravis, this additional presynaptic impairment can tip the balance into clinically significant weakness. The other options describe mechanisms of organophosphates, curare-like competitive antagonists, and botulinum toxin, none of which characterize aminoglycoside pharmacology, and there is no acetylcholine reuptake transporter at the neuromuscular junction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Drug-induced worsening of myasthenia gravis by aminoglycosides is a classic high-yield pharmacology interaction question that hinges on the presynaptic calcium channel mechanism from this objective. COMLEX: This interaction is a practical reason to use aminoglycosides cautiously in any patient with a known or suspected neuromuscular junction disorder.","crossRef":"📖 Differentiating neuromuscular junction weakness patterns from upper motor neuron, lower motor neuron, and myopathic patterns, the framework needed to recognize this drug-induced myasthenic worsening","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q29","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain why presynaptic voltage-gated Ca2+ channels are essential for vesicle fusion and transmitter release.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with a 40-pack-year smoking history presents with progressive proximal leg weakness that improves temporarily after several minutes of sustained muscle contraction. Chest imaging reveals a mass consistent with small cell lung carcinoma. Serum antibody testing later confirms autoantibodies against a presynaptic ion channel. Which channel is targeted, and why does repeated muscle activity transiently improve her strength?","choices":{"A":"Presynaptic voltage-gated Na+ channels are targeted, and repeated firing recruits additional channels that were previously in an inactivated state","B":"Postsynaptic nicotinic receptor channels are targeted, and repeated firing increases receptor synthesis rapidly enough to restore strength","C":"Presynaptic voltage-gated K+ channels are targeted, and repeated firing prolongs the action potential enough to compensate for channel loss","D":"Postsynaptic muscarinic receptor channels are targeted, and repeated firing shifts skeletal muscle to use parasympathetic transmission instead","E":"Presynaptic voltage-gated Ca2+ channels are targeted, and repeated firing lets residual channels admit enough Ca2+ to restore release briefly"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This presentation describes Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome, a paraneoplastic disorder most classically associated with small cell lung carcinoma, in which autoantibodies target presynaptic voltage-gated Ca2+ channels; because some channels remain functional, repeated nerve firing allows cumulative Ca2+ entry with each successive action potential, transiently facilitating enough transmitter release to improve strength, the opposite pattern from myasthenia gravis. The distractors substitute the wrong channel or receptor and describe implausible rapid compensatory mechanisms that do not occur within minutes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lambert-Eaton syndrome improving with repeated activity, versus myasthenia gravis worsening with repeated activity, is one of the most reliably tested contrasts in neuromuscular pharmacology. COMLEX: The paraneoplastic association with small cell lung cancer should prompt a malignancy workup whenever this presentation is recognized.","crossRef":"📖 Differentiating neuromuscular junction weakness patterns from upper motor neuron, lower motor neuron, and myopathic patterns, into which Lambert-Eaton syndrome is classified as a presynaptic NMJ disorder","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q30","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain why presynaptic voltage-gated Ca2+ channels are essential for vesicle fusion and transmitter release.","batch":2,"stem":"In an experimental preparation, a black widow spider venom component called alpha-latrotoxin inserts into the presynaptic membrane and forms nonspecific cation pores that flood the terminal cytoplasm with Ca2+ independent of any voltage-gated channel activity. What is the expected immediate effect on neurotransmitter release, and why does the effect eventually reverse into complete synaptic failure?","choices":{"A":"Neurotransmitter release is immediately and completely blocked because nonspecific cation pores prevent any physiologic calcium signaling from occurring","B":"Neurotransmitter release remains unchanged because synaptotagmin only responds to calcium entering through voltage-gated channels specifically","C":"Neurotransmitter release increases briefly and then stabilizes at a new, permanently higher steady-state level indefinitely","D":"Massive vesicle release occurs first because Ca2+ influx bypasses channel regulation, followed by failure once the vesicle pool is exhausted","E":"Neurotransmitter release is redirected exclusively toward dense-core vesicles while small clear vesicles stop responding entirely"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Because synaptotagmin senses cytoplasmic Ca2+ regardless of its entry route, the nonspecific flooding produced by alpha-latrotoxin triggers massive, uncoordinated vesicle fusion and neurotransmitter depletion; once the readily releasable and reserve vesicle pools are exhausted, the terminal can no longer support further release, producing eventual complete synaptic failure rather than sustained elevated output. The remaining distractors incorrectly claim immediate blockade, no effect, a stable new steady state, or vesicle-type-specific redirection, none of which reflect the pool-exhaustion mechanism actually described.","boardPrep":"Step 1: This vignette illustrates that synaptotagmin's calcium sensitivity is not channel-specific, a subtle but testable extension of Objective 2's core principle. COMLEX: Alpha-latrotoxin toxicity produces a clinical picture of initial cramping and sweating followed by progressive weakness as vesicle stores are depleted.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — the roles of voltage-gated channels and the Na+/K+ ATPase in maintaining normal ionic gradients, the regulated system that alpha-latrotoxin's nonspecific pore formation bypasses entirely","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q31","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare fast ionotropic receptor signaling with slower metabotropic receptor signaling.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman is started on a new medication for major depressive disorder. Her physician explains that unlike a fast-acting sedative that produces effects within minutes, this medication requires several weeks of continuous use before its full clinical benefit is apparent. Which receptor signaling property most directly explains this delayed onset?","choices":{"A":"The medication acts on metabotropic G protein-coupled pathways that require gradual adaptive changes in receptor expression","B":"The medication acts on ionotropic ligand-gated ion channels that require weeks to synthesize enough new channel protein","C":"The medication acts on metabotropic receptors that physically require several weeks to bind their neurotransmitter for the first time","D":"The medication acts on ionotropic receptors whose channel pore takes several weeks to fully open after initial binding","E":"The medication acts on presynaptic voltage-gated Ca2+ channels that require several weeks to upregulate in number"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Drugs that act through metabotropic, G protein-coupled receptor pathways, as most antidepressants do, produce their initial pharmacologic effect quickly but their full clinical benefit only after slower adaptive changes in receptor sensitivity, gene expression, and downstream signaling accumulate over days to weeks, unlike the millisecond-scale onset of ionotropic channel opening. The distractors incorrectly attribute the delay to ionotropic channel synthesis or opening kinetics, to an implausible weeks-long binding delay, or to calcium channel upregulation, none of which reflect the true mechanism of delayed metabotropic adaptation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The days-to-weeks onset of SSRIs and other antidepressants is a hallmark exam fact directly explained by the slow, adaptive nature of metabotropic signaling covered in this objective. COMLEX: This same slow-onset, long-duration principle applies broadly to any drug acting primarily through G protein-coupled receptors rather than ligand-gated channels.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 2 — comparing sympathetic, parasympathetic, and enteric divisions by their neurotransmitters and receptor types, most of which are also metabotropic and share this same slow, adaptive signaling logic","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q32","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare fast ionotropic receptor signaling with slower metabotropic receptor signaling.","batch":2,"stem":"In a hippocampal slice, glutamate is puffed onto a neuron held at its resting potential while AMPA receptors are pharmacologically blocked. Almost no current flows through the remaining NMDA receptors. When the same neuron is first depolarized toward 0 mV and glutamate is applied again, a large Ca2+-carrying current appears. Which of the following best explains this voltage dependence?","choices":{"A":"NMDA receptors are metabotropic and need depolarization to activate their G protein","B":"Depolarization raises the glutamate affinity of the NMDA ligand-binding domain","C":"NMDA receptors are voltage-gated Ca2+ channels that ignore the glutamate puff","D":"Extracellular Mg2+ plugs the NMDA pore at rest until depolarization drives it out","E":"Depolarization reverses the Ca2+ gradient so that Ca2+ can now enter the cell"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The NMDA receptor is an ionotropic glutamate receptor, but extracellular Mg2+ binds within its channel at the resting potential, so no current passes even when glutamate is bound. The cell must be depolarized long enough to drive the Mg2+ out of the pore, after which a large Ca2+ influx occurs. NMDA receptors are ligand-gated channels, not G protein-coupled receptors, and the pore block is the voltage-sensitive step rather than a change in binding affinity. They need glutamate to open, so they are not simple voltage-gated Ca2+ channels. Depolarization lowers, not reverses, the inward Ca2+ driving force, since the Ca2+ equilibrium potential is far positive.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The NMDA receptor needs both glutamate and depolarization (Mg2+ block relief), which makes it a coincidence detector for long-term potentiation. COMLEX: Excess NMDA-mediated Ca2+ entry drives excitotoxic injury in stroke; the NMDA antagonists ketamine and memantine act at this receptor.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 4 — graded versus all-or-none potentials, here the depolarization that must precede NMDA receptor current","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q33","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare fast ionotropic receptor signaling with slower metabotropic receptor signaling.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with hypertension is prescribed a beta-1 adrenergic receptor antagonist. His physician explains that the drug will reduce heart rate and contractility gradually as it takes effect over the following hour, rather than instantaneously blocking any single ion channel. Which feature of beta-1 receptor signaling best explains this time course?","choices":{"A":"Beta-1 receptors are ionotropic ligand-gated calcium channels that simply take longer to close once norepinephrine has been displaced","B":"Beta-1 receptors are metabotropic, acting through a G protein-adenylyl cyclase-cAMP cascade that modulates cardiac channels indirectly","C":"Beta-1 receptors are ionotropic sodium channels whose blockade requires several successive heartbeats to become clinically apparent","D":"Beta-1 receptors are metabotropic but signal exclusively through phospholipase C, which acts more slowly than adenylyl cyclase","E":"Beta-1 receptors are ionotropic potassium channels that require gradual receptor internalization before any effect is seen"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Beta-1 adrenergic receptors are metabotropic, seven-transmembrane G protein-coupled receptors that signal through a Gs-adenylyl cyclase-cAMP cascade to modulate cardiac calcium and other channels indirectly, and blocking this receptor removes an ongoing modulatory influence rather than instantly closing an intrinsic channel, producing a gradual rather than instantaneous hemodynamic effect. The distractors incorrectly describe beta-1 receptors as ionotropic channels of various types or misattribute their signaling to phospholipase C rather than the correct adenylyl cyclase pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Recognizing beta receptors as metabotropic Gs-coupled receptors acting through cAMP is essential background for every cardiovascular and pulmonary pharmacology question involving beta blockers or beta agonists. COMLEX: This same Gs-cAMP logic explains why beta-agonist bronchodilators also have an onset measured in minutes rather than milliseconds.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — the roles of voltage-gated Na+ and K+ channels and the Na+/K+ ATPase in cardiac and neuronal excitability, the ion channels that beta-1 receptor signaling modulates indirectly rather than forming directly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q34","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish EPSPs from IPSPs and explain temporal and spatial summation.","batch":2,"stem":"A physiologist records from a postsynaptic neuron that receives input from ten different excitatory presynaptic terminals distributed across its dendritic tree. When only one terminal fires, no action potential results, but when all ten terminals fire simultaneously, an action potential is generated at the axon hillock. Which phenomenon does this experiment best demonstrate?","choices":{"A":"Spatial summation, in which EPSPs from multiple different terminals combine simultaneously to reach threshold","B":"Temporal summation, in which repeated firing from one terminal over time combines to reach threshold","C":"Synaptic facilitation, in which a single terminal's response grows stronger with each successive stimulus","D":"Long-term potentiation, in which the synapse becomes permanently more responsive after repeated coactivation","E":"Receptor desensitization, in which postsynaptic receptors become progressively less responsive to repeated stimulation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because the summation described here arises from many different presynaptic terminals firing at the same moment, rather than one terminal firing repeatedly over time, this is spatial summation, the process by which EPSPs generated at different locations on the postsynaptic membrane add together to reach threshold. Temporal summation instead requires repeated firing from a single terminal, synaptic facilitation and long-term potentiation describe distinct plasticity phenomena unrelated to this single-trial experiment, and receptor desensitization would reduce rather than enable the observed response.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Distinguishing spatial from temporal summation by counting terminals versus counting time is a reliable strategy for these paired-concept questions. COMLEX: Both summation types converge on the same axon hillock, which integrates all incoming graded potentials into a single all-or-none decision.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — how ion concentration gradients and membrane permeability generate the resting membrane potential, the same ionic baseline against which every summed EPSP is measured","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q35","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish EPSPs from IPSPs and explain temporal and spatial summation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy has a spinal cord injury that damages a population of inhibitory interneurons providing tonic glycinergic input to motor neurons in the affected segment. Which change in postsynaptic potential would be expected at the motor neurons that previously received this input, and what functional consequence follows?","choices":{"A":"Loss of IPSPs removes a depolarizing influence, making the motor neurons less excitable and prone to flaccidity","B":"Loss of EPSPs removes a hyperpolarizing influence, making the motor neurons more excitable and prone to spasticity","C":"Loss of IPSPs removes a hyperpolarizing influence, making the motor neurons more excitable and prone to spasticity","D":"Gain of additional IPSPs adds a hyperpolarizing influence, making the motor neurons less excitable and prone to flaccidity","E":"Loss of IPSPs removes a hyperpolarizing influence, but motor neuron excitability remains completely unchanged overall"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Glycine normally produces IPSPs by opening chloride channels that hyperpolarize the motor neuron, keeping it further from threshold; losing this tonic inhibitory input removes that hyperpolarizing brake, leaving the motor neuron relatively more excitable and prone to the increased tone and hyperreflexia characteristic of spasticity. The distractors incorrectly reverse the polarity of glycine's effect, substitute EPSPs for IPSPs, describe a gain rather than a loss of inhibitory input, or claim no functional consequence despite the clear physiologic change described.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Loss of inhibitory tone producing spasticity is the same conceptual thread connecting glycine loss here to the mechanism of tetanus toxin, which similarly removes inhibitory interneuron output. COMLEX: Recognizing hyperreflexia and increased tone as signs of disinhibition rather than direct motor neuron damage is central to upper versus lower motor neuron localization.","crossRef":"📖 Differentiating upper motor neuron, lower motor neuron, neuromuscular junction, and myopathic patterns of weakness, the framework used to recognize spasticity as a sign of disinhibited motor neurons","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q36","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish EPSPs from IPSPs and explain temporal and spatial summation.","batch":2,"stem":"A dendrite's GABA-A synapses open Cl- channels, and in this neuron the Cl- equilibrium potential equals the resting potential, so opening them causes no visible change in membrane voltage. Even so, when these inhibitory synapses are active, nearby excitatory inputs no longer bring the axon hillock to threshold. Which of the following best explains this inhibition?","choices":{"A":"Cl- channel opening reverses the direction of nearby AMPA receptor current","B":"Added Cl- conductance lowers resistance and shortens the length constant","C":"GABA-A activation closes presynaptic Ca2+ channels on the excitatory terminals","D":"Cl- entry raises the Na+ equilibrium potential above the threshold voltage","E":"GABA-A receptors act through G proteins that open K+ channels at the hillock"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Opening Cl- channels increases the membrane conductance and so lowers the dendrite's membrane resistance, which shortens its length constant. Any nearby excitatory postsynaptic potential then decays rapidly as it spreads and has less influence on whether an action potential is triggered at the axon initial segment, even though the inhibitory input itself produces little or no hyperpolarization. AMPA current still flows inward because its reversal potential is unchanged. GABA-A receptors are postsynaptic ligand-gated channels and do not close presynaptic Ca2+ channels. Cl- movement does not alter the Na+ equilibrium potential, and K+ channels opened through G proteins describe GABA-B, not GABA-A, signaling.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Inhibition can work by conductance increase, shunting excitatory current, as well as by hyperpolarization; both effects reduce summation at the hillock. COMLEX: Benzodiazepines and barbiturates enhance this GABA-A Cl- conductance, which is why they suppress seizures.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — resting membrane permeability and conductance, here a Cl- conductance change that shapes EPSP spread","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q37","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the major neurotransmitter classes and their common receptor families.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman with treatment-resistant hypertension is found to have a pheochromocytoma. Preoperative medical management includes a drug that inhibits tyrosine hydroxylase, the rate-limiting enzyme in catecholamine synthesis, to reduce the risk of an intraoperative catecholamine surge. Which neurotransmitter class does this enzyme ultimately produce, and which receptor family predominantly mediates its downstream effects?","choices":{"A":"The amino acid class, including glutamate and GABA, which act through a mix of ionotropic and metabotropic receptors","B":"The cholinergic class, including acetylcholine, which acts through both ionotropic nicotinic and metabotropic muscarinic receptors","C":"The neuropeptide class, including enkephalin and substance P, which act exclusively through metabotropic opioid receptors","D":"The purine class, including ATP and adenosine, which act through both ionotropic P2X and metabotropic P2Y receptors","E":"The monoamine class, including norepinephrine and dopamine, which act mainly through metabotropic G protein-coupled receptors"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Tyrosine hydroxylase catalyzes the rate-limiting step in catecholamine synthesis, producing the monoamine transmitters norepinephrine, epinephrine, and dopamine, all of which act predominantly through metabotropic adrenergic and dopaminergic G protein-coupled receptors, explaining the risk of a profound and sustained catecholamine surge during tumor manipulation. The amino acid, cholinergic, neuropeptide, and purine classes each involve different synthetic pathways and different receptor families that are not the direct product of tyrosine hydroxylase activity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tyrosine hydroxylase inhibition as pheochromocytoma management is a classic pharmacology tie-in to catecholamine synthesis, a favorite integration point between endocrine and neuroscience content. COMLEX: Alpha-methyltyrosine is the specific tyrosine hydroxylase inhibitor used clinically to deplete catecholamine stores before surgery.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 2 — comparing sympathetic, parasympathetic, and enteric divisions by their neurotransmitters, since norepinephrine and epinephrine are the principal transmitters of sympathetic output","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q38","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the major neurotransmitter classes and their common receptor families.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old man develops severe nausea and vomiting during chemotherapy. His oncologist prescribes a medication that blocks a specific receptor on vagal afferent neurons activated by serotonin released from enterochromaffin cells in the gut. Given that this is the one exception among biogenic amine receptors, which receptor is targeted, and what distinguishes it from nearly all other serotonin receptors?","choices":{"A":"The 5-HT3 receptor is targeted, and unlike nearly every other serotonin receptor, it is ionotropic rather than metabotropic","B":"The 5-HT1D receptor is targeted, and unlike nearly every other serotonin receptor, it is ionotropic rather than metabotropic","C":"The 5-HT2A receptor is targeted, and unlike nearly every other serotonin receptor, it is metabotropic rather than ionotropic","D":"The 5-HT3 receptor is targeted, and unlike nearly every other serotonin receptor, it is metabotropic rather than ionotropic","E":"The 5-HT7 receptor is targeted, and unlike nearly every other serotonin receptor, it is ionotropic rather than metabotropic"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The 5-HT3 receptor is the single ionotropic exception among serotonin receptors, functioning as a ligand-gated cation channel on vagal afferents that, when activated by gut-derived serotonin, drives the nausea and vomiting reflex, making it the target of antiemetic 5-HT3 antagonists such as ondansetron-class drugs. The distractors either name the wrong receptor subtype, such as 5-HT1D or 5-HT2A or 5-HT7, or correctly name 5-HT3 but reverse its defining ionotropic property, incorrectly labeling it metabotropic.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-HT3 as the lone ionotropic serotonin receptor is a frequently tested exception that examiners like to pair against the otherwise all-metabotropic serotonin receptor family. COMLEX: This mechanism also explains why 5-HT3 antagonists act quickly, consistent with the fast kinetics of ionotropic receptor blockade.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 4 — distinguishing graded potentials from action potentials, since the 5-HT3 receptor produces a fast, graded ionotropic potential unlike the slower metabotropic serotonin receptors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q39","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the major neurotransmitter classes and their common receptor families.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with chronic insomnia has been self-treating with an over-the-counter antihistamine for sedation. His physician explains that this drug's sedating effect comes from blocking a receptor for a biogenic amine transmitter that is also involved in wakefulness and allergic response. Which neurotransmitter and receptor family is responsible for this sedating effect?","choices":{"A":"Histamine, acting through ionotropic H1 receptors in the central nervous system","B":"Serotonin, acting through metabotropic 5-HT3 receptors in the central nervous system","C":"Dopamine, acting through metabotropic D2 receptors in the central nervous system","D":"Histamine, acting through metabotropic H1 receptors in the central nervous system","E":"Acetylcholine, acting through ionotropic nicotinic receptors in the central nervous system"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Histamine is a monoamine transmitter involved in wakefulness and allergic responses, and first-generation antihistamines cross the blood-brain barrier to block central metabotropic H1 receptors, producing sedation as a side effect of reduced histaminergic wakefulness signaling. Histamine H1 receptors are metabotropic, not ionotropic, serotonin's 5-HT3 receptor is the ionotropic exception rather than the sedating target here, dopamine D2 blockade produces antipsychotic rather than sedating antihistamine effects, and acetylcholine nicotinic blockade is not the mechanism of antihistamine sedation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Histamine H1 receptor blockade as the mechanism of first-generation antihistamine sedation is a frequently tested pharmacology fact that pairs naturally with this objective's receptor family survey. COMLEX: Many first-generation antihistamines also have anticholinergic activity, which can compound sedation and cause additional side effects such as dry mouth and urinary retention.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — the voltage-gated channels and Na+/K+ ATPase governing baseline neuronal excitability, the substrate upon which histaminergic wakefulness signaling ultimately acts","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q40","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain how synaptic signaling is terminated by reuptake, enzymatic degradation, diffusion, or metabolic conversion.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher measures how long substance P keeps acting after release from dense-core vesicles in the dorsal horn. Blocking DAT, NET, SERT, and the glial glutamate transporters has no effect on the duration of its action. Which mechanism most likely ends neuropeptide signaling at this synapse?","choices":{"A":"Hydrolysis by peptidases in the synaptic cleft","B":"Reuptake into the terminal by a peptide transporter","C":"Hydrolysis by acetylcholinesterase in the cleft","D":"Oxidation by monoamine oxidase in the terminal","E":"Conversion to glutamine within nearby astrocytes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Neuropeptides released from dense-core vesicles bind G protein-coupled receptors with large ligand-binding sites, and their signal is ended by peptide hydrolysis by endopeptidases in the synaptic cleft. Unlike small-molecule transmitters, peptides are not recaptured for reuse, and they cannot be made locally in the terminal; new peptide must be synthesized in the soma and shipped down the axon. A dedicated presynaptic peptide reuptake transporter does not carry out this termination. Acetylcholinesterase is specific to acetylcholine, monoamine oxidase degrades catecholamines and serotonin, and astrocytic conversion to glutamine handles glutamate and GABA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Termination by transmitter: acetylcholine by acetylcholinesterase, monoamines by reuptake then MAO and COMT, glutamate and GABA by glial uptake and the glutamine cycle, neuropeptides by extracellular peptidases. COMLEX: Neprilysin inhibitors such as sacubitril work by slowing peptidase breakdown of signaling peptides.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 5 — neurotransmitter classes and receptor families, including the neuropeptides whose metabotropic receptors are discussed there","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q41","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain how synaptic signaling is terminated by reuptake, enzymatic degradation, diffusion, or metabolic conversion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with Parkinson disease is started on a monoamine oxidase type B inhibitor as adjunct therapy alongside levodopa. His neurologist explains that this drug prolongs the action of dopamine that remains in the brain. Which termination mechanism does this drug interfere with, and where does that mechanism normally act?","choices":{"A":"Reuptake, since monoamine oxidase is itself a component of the DAT transporter that normally clears dopamine from the cleft","B":"Enzymatic degradation, since monoamine oxidase normally oxidizes excess catecholamine within the presynaptic terminal and postsynaptic cell","C":"Diffusion, since monoamine oxidase normally accelerates the diffusion of dopamine away from the synaptic cleft","D":"Metabolic conversion by glia, since monoamine oxidase is expressed exclusively within astrocytes surrounding dopaminergic synapses","E":"Vesicular repackaging, since monoamine oxidase normally loads dopamine back into synaptic vesicles for reuse"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Monoamine oxidase, particularly the MAO-B isoform relevant to dopamine, is an enzyme that oxidizes and degrades excess catecholamine that has been taken back up into the presynaptic terminal or escaped into the postsynaptic cell, and inhibiting it allows more intact dopamine to remain available, complementing levodopa therapy. MAO is not a component of the DAT transporter itself, does not accelerate diffusion, is not exclusively astrocytic, and does not participate in vesicular repackaging, which is instead performed by VMAT.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MAO-B inhibitors as Parkinson disease adjuncts are a direct clinical application of the enzymatic degradation termination mechanism, distinct from reuptake blockade. COMLEX: Selegiline is the prototypical MAO-B inhibitor used in this context, chosen for its relative selectivity for dopamine over serotonin and norepinephrine at typical doses.","crossRef":"📖 Differentiating upper motor neuron, lower motor neuron, and other movement-related patterns, relevant background for recognizing the parkinsonian movement findings this therapy targets","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q42","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain how synaptic signaling is terminated by reuptake, enzymatic degradation, diffusion, or metabolic conversion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman with a seizure disorder is prescribed tiagabine, which is described to her as a drug that blocks the GABA transporter responsible for returning GABA to presynaptic terminals and surrounding astrocytes. Which termination mechanism does this drug target, and what is the expected effect on inhibitory signaling?","choices":{"A":"Enzymatic degradation is blocked, allowing GABA to accumulate specifically inside the presynaptic terminal rather than the cleft","B":"Diffusion is blocked, trapping GABA entirely within synaptic vesicles before it can ever be released","C":"Metabolic conversion is blocked, preventing GABA from ever being synthesized from its glutamate precursor","D":"Reuptake is blocked, allowing GABA to persist longer in the synaptic cleft and prolong inhibitory postsynaptic signaling","E":"Vesicular loading is blocked, preventing any further GABA from being packaged into presynaptic vesicles"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Tiagabine inhibits the GAT1 GABA transporter responsible for reuptake of GABA into presynaptic terminals and astrocytes, so blocking this reuptake mechanism allows GABA to remain in the synaptic cleft longer, prolonging its inhibitory action on GABA-A and GABA-B receptors and raising seizure threshold. Tiagabine does not act on an enzyme, does not trap GABA within vesicles, does not block GABA synthesis from glutamate, and does not interfere with vesicular loading, all of which describe different steps in GABA handling that are unaffected by this drug.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tiagabine as a selective GABA reuptake inhibitor is a favorite way to test whether students can distinguish reuptake blockade from enzymatic degradation blockade, such as vigabatrin's inhibition of GABA transaminase. COMLEX: Both tiagabine and vigabatrin ultimately increase synaptic GABA availability but through mechanistically distinct steps in the termination pathway.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — the voltage-gated channel and active-transport framework underlying inhibitory signaling, since GABA-A channel opening is the downstream event this prolonged GABA availability enhances","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q43","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Apply synaptic mechanisms to board-relevant diseases, drugs, and toxins including myasthenia gravis, Lambert-Eaton syndrome, botulism, tetanus, organophosphate toxicity, seizures, Parkinson disease, antipsychotics, SSRIs, benzodiazepines, and barbiturates.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after a suicide attempt involving a large ingestion of both a benzodiazepine and a barbiturate. His breathing becomes progressively shallow and he requires intubation. His treating physician explains that the danger of combining these two drug classes lies in the fact that they act at two distinct allosteric sites on the same receptor, producing an effect that is more than additive. Which receptor and site pairing explains this synergistic danger?","choices":{"A":"The GABA-B receptor, where benzodiazepines act as agonists and barbiturates act as positive allosteric modulators at the same single binding site","B":"The NMDA receptor, where benzodiazepines block the magnesium site and barbiturates block the glycine coagonist site simultaneously","C":"The GABA-A receptor, where benzodiazepines increase channel opening frequency and barbiturates prolong opening duration at separate sites","D":"The nicotinic acetylcholine receptor, where benzodiazepines and barbiturates both bind the same site to produce prolonged desensitization","E":"The glycine receptor, where benzodiazepines increase channel opening frequency and barbiturates block the channel pore entirely"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Both benzodiazepines and barbiturates act on the ionotropic GABA-A receptor but at two distinct allosteric sites, benzodiazepines at the alpha and gamma subunit interface, which increases the frequency of chloride channel opening, and barbiturates at a separate intrachannel site, which prolongs the duration each channel stays open; because these are independent mechanisms converging on the same chloride current, their combined effect on respiratory drive is more than additive and can be fatal. The GABA-B receptor is metabotropic and not the shared site described, the NMDA and nicotinic receptor pairings are fabricated mechanisms that do not describe actual benzodiazepine or barbiturate pharmacology, and the glycine receptor option incorrectly describes barbiturate action as simple channel blockade.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The distinct frequency-versus-duration mechanism of benzodiazepines versus barbiturates at GABA-A is one of the highest-yield pharmacology pairings tested on Step 1. COMLEX: This same synergy is the pharmacologic basis for why flumazenil, a GABA-A benzodiazepine site antagonist, does not reliably reverse barbiturate-induced respiratory depression, since barbiturates act at a separate site.","crossRef":"📖 Recognizing depressed consciousness and respiratory patterns as part of a comprehensive neurologic assessment, relevant when evaluating a patient with combined sedative-hypnotic overdose","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q44","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Apply synaptic mechanisms to board-relevant diseases, drugs, and toxins including myasthenia gravis, Lambert-Eaton syndrome, botulism, tetanus, organophosphate toxicity, seizures, Parkinson disease, antipsychotics, SSRIs, benzodiazepines, and barbiturates.","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old man with schizophrenia is started on haloperidol. Within days he develops bradykinesia, rigidity, and a resting tremor that closely resembles idiopathic Parkinson disease. Which receptor mechanism explains both the drug's antipsychotic benefit and this movement disorder side effect?","choices":{"A":"D1 dopamine receptor agonism in the mesolimbic pathway provides antipsychotic benefit, while D2 antagonism in the nigrostriatal pathway produces drug-induced parkinsonism","B":"D2 dopamine receptor agonism in the mesolimbic pathway provides antipsychotic benefit, while D1 antagonism in the nigrostriatal pathway produces drug-induced parkinsonism","C":"5-HT2A receptor antagonism in the mesolimbic pathway provides antipsychotic benefit, while D2 agonism in the nigrostriatal pathway produces drug-induced parkinsonism","D":"D2 dopamine receptor antagonism in the nigrostriatal pathway provides antipsychotic benefit, while the same antagonism in the mesolimbic pathway produces drug-induced parkinsonism","E":"D2 dopamine receptor antagonism in the mesolimbic pathway provides antipsychotic benefit, while the same antagonism in the nigrostriatal pathway produces drug-induced parkinsonism"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Haloperidol and similar first-generation antipsychotics act as D2 dopamine receptor antagonists, and blocking excess dopaminergic transmission in the mesolimbic pathway is responsible for their antipsychotic effect, while that same D2 blockade in the nigrostriatal pathway, which normally relies on intact dopaminergic tone for smooth movement, produces drug-induced parkinsonism. The distractors incorrectly assign agonism where antagonism belongs, swap D1 for D2, misattribute the antipsychotic effect to 5-HT2A antagonism alone, or reverse which pathway is responsible for the therapeutic versus the side effect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: D2 antagonism producing both antipsychotic benefit in the mesolimbic pathway and parkinsonism in the nigrostriatal pathway is a canonical two-pathway pharmacology question. COMLEX: This same nigrostriatal D2 blockade principle previews the basal nuclei circuitry covered later in the block, where dopamine's net effect on direct and indirect pathways determines movement facilitation or suppression.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 43 Obj 9 — recognizing antipsychotic-induced parkinsonism as a key pharmacologic correlation within basal nuclei circuit dysfunction, the anatomic circuit this drug-induced syndrome will be revisited within","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q45","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Apply synaptic mechanisms to board-relevant diseases, drugs, and toxins including myasthenia gravis, Lambert-Eaton syndrome, botulism, tetanus, organophosphate toxicity, seizures, Parkinson disease, antipsychotics, SSRIs, benzodiazepines, and barbiturates.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old man is exposed to a nerve agent during an industrial accident and develops excessive salivation, lacrimation, urination, diarrhea, and muscle fasciculations progressing to weakness. Emergency treatment includes atropine and pralidoxime. Which statement best explains why atropine alone is insufficient to fully reverse this patient's presentation?","choices":{"A":"Atropine blocks only nicotinic receptor overstimulation, leaving muscarinic receptor overstimulation, which causes the fasciculations and weakness, unaddressed","B":"Atropine blocks only muscarinic receptor overstimulation, leaving nicotinic receptor overstimulation, which causes the fasciculations and weakness, unaddressed","C":"Atropine has no effect on acetylcholine receptors at all and instead works by directly reactivating inhibited acetylcholinesterase enzyme","D":"Atropine blocks both muscarinic and nicotinic receptors equally, so its insufficiency is due entirely to a short half-life rather than receptor selectivity","E":"Atropine blocks presynaptic calcium channels to reduce further acetylcholine release, which does not address existing excess acetylcholine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Organophosphate poisoning causes acetylcholine to accumulate at both muscarinic and nicotinic synapses, but atropine is a selective muscarinic antagonist that relieves the autonomic symptoms, salivation, lacrimation, urination, and diarrhea, without addressing nicotinic receptor overstimulation at the neuromuscular junction, which drives the fasciculations and progressive weakness; pralidoxime is added specifically to reactivate acetylcholinesterase and address the underlying enzyme inhibition more broadly. The distractors reverse which receptor type atropine blocks, incorrectly claim atropine has no receptor activity at all, misattribute its limitation to half-life rather than receptor selectivity, or invent a nonexistent calcium channel mechanism for atropine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Atropine's muscarinic selectivity, and its resulting inability to reverse nicotinic neuromuscular findings, is a frequently tested nuance in organophosphate toxicity management. COMLEX: Pralidoxime works only if administered before the phosphorylated enzyme undergoes aging, an irreversible chemical change that eliminates the drug's ability to reactivate acetylcholinesterase.","crossRef":"📖 Differentiating neuromuscular junction weakness patterns from other patterns of weakness, the framework needed to recognize the nicotinic component of this toxidrome as a distinct NMJ process","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q46","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use a mechanism-based algorithm to solve clinical questions involving chemical signaling.","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after using an unknown street drug and is found to be agitated, diaphoretic, tachycardic, and hyperthermic with dilated pupils. Toxicology later confirms methamphetamine use. Applying the mechanism-based algorithm, which sequence of reasoning correctly localizes and explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"The synapse involved is cholinergic muscarinic, the lesion is at the synaptic cleft due to acetylcholinesterase inhibition, the net effect is excess inhibition, and the clinical match is bradycardia with pinpoint pupils","B":"The synapse involved is GABAergic, the lesion is postsynaptic due to allosteric receptor blockade, the net effect is reduced inhibition, and the clinical match is sedation with hypothermia","C":"The synapse involved is glutamatergic, the lesion is postsynaptic due to NMDA receptor upregulation, the net effect is reduced excitation, and the clinical match is flaccid paralysis","D":"The synapse involved is adrenergic and dopaminergic, the lesion is presynaptic via VMAT2 and transporter reversal, the net effect is excess release, and the clinical match is sympathetic overactivity with agitation","E":"The synapse involved is adrenergic and dopaminergic, the lesion is postsynaptic due to receptor downregulation, the net effect is reduced transmitter action, and the clinical match is sedation with bradycardia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Following the algorithm, methamphetamine acts at adrenergic and dopaminergic synapses, the lesion is presynaptic because the drug blocks VMAT2 vesicular storage and drives reverse transport through DAT and NET, the net effect is excess catecholamine release into the cleft, and this matches the clinical picture of sympathetic overactivity, agitation, tachycardia, hyperthermia, and mydriasis. The distractors describe unrelated cholinergic or GABAergic or glutamatergic systems, invent a receptor upregulation mechanism, or reverse the net effect to reduced transmitter action, none of which match this clearly sympathomimetic toxidrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Working through all four algorithm steps in order, transmitter, lesion location, net effect, and clinical match, is the most reliable way to avoid picking a superficially plausible but mechanistically wrong answer on a toxidrome vignette. COMLEX: Amphetamine toxicity's sympathomimetic picture is a direct clinical application of the reverse-transport release mechanism first introduced in the objectives on release and termination.","crossRef":"📖 Constructing a concise neurologic localization statement from a clinical vignette, the same stepwise reasoning habit applied here to localize this toxidrome to a specific synaptic mechanism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q47","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use a mechanism-based algorithm to solve clinical questions involving chemical signaling.","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old college student develops severe muscle rigidity, high fever, and autonomic instability two days after starting an antipsychotic medication, along with markedly elevated creatine kinase. Applying the mechanism-based algorithm, which sequence best explains this presentation as distinct from simple antipsychotic-induced parkinsonism?","choices":{"A":"The synapse involved is dopaminergic, the lesion is postsynaptic D2 receptor blockade, the net effect is a profound loss of dopaminergic tone, and the clinical match is neuroleptic malignant syndrome","B":"The synapse involved is serotonergic, the lesion is presynaptic excess serotonin release, the net effect is serotonin excess, and the clinical match is neuroleptic malignant syndrome","C":"The synapse involved is dopaminergic, the lesion is presynaptic excess dopamine release, the net effect is dopamine excess, and the clinical match is neuroleptic malignant syndrome","D":"The synapse involved is GABAergic, the lesion is postsynaptic receptor upregulation, the net effect is excess inhibition, and the clinical match is neuroleptic malignant syndrome","E":"The synapse involved is dopaminergic, the lesion is postsynaptic D2 receptor blockade, the net effect is a profound loss of dopaminergic tone, and the clinical match is serotonin syndrome with hyperreflexia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome results from the same dopaminergic postsynaptic D2 blockade mechanism as drug-induced parkinsonism, but it represents an unusually severe and abrupt loss of dopaminergic tone that produces life-threatening rigidity, hyperthermia, and autonomic instability rather than the milder, more gradual parkinsonian picture. The distractors incorrectly substitute serotonergic or GABAergic mechanisms, reverse presynaptic and postsynaptic localization, describe dopamine excess rather than the correct loss of dopaminergic tone, or mislabel the final clinical match as serotonin syndrome despite an antipsychotic rather than serotonergic drug being described.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Distinguishing neuroleptic malignant syndrome, caused by D2 blockade, from serotonin syndrome, caused by excess serotonergic activity, is a classic side-by-side comparison question that rewards careful mechanism tracing. COMLEX: Recognizing the severity gradient from drug-induced parkinsonism to full neuroleptic malignant syndrome along the same D2 blockade mechanism illustrates how the same lesion can produce a spectrum of clinical presentations.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 43 Obj 9 — recognizing antipsychotic-induced parkinsonism and related pharmacologic correlations within basal nuclei circuit dysfunction, the same D2 blockade mechanism that, taken to its extreme, produces neuroleptic malignant syndrome","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q48","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use a mechanism-based algorithm to solve clinical questions involving chemical signaling.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-week-old infant presents with poor feeding, a weak cry, constipation, and generalized hypotonia after honey was added to her formula. Applying the mechanism-based algorithm, which sequence correctly explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"The synapse involved is cholinergic muscarinic in the autonomic ganglia, the lesion is postsynaptic receptor blockade, the net effect is reduced IPSPs, and the clinical match is ascending spastic paralysis","B":"The synapse involved is GABAergic in the spinal cord, the lesion is postsynaptic receptor blockade, the net effect is reduced IPSPs, and the clinical match is descending flaccid paralysis","C":"The synapse involved is cholinergic nicotinic at the neuromuscular junction, the lesion is presynaptic SNARE protein cleavage, the net effect is reduced EPSPs, and the clinical match is descending flaccid paralysis","D":"The synapse involved is cholinergic nicotinic at the neuromuscular junction, the lesion is postsynaptic receptor destruction by antibodies, the net effect is reduced EPSPs, and the clinical match is descending flaccid paralysis","E":"The synapse involved is glycinergic in the spinal cord, the lesion is presynaptic SNARE protein cleavage, the net effect is increased EPSPs, and the clinical match is spastic paralysis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This presentation describes infant botulism from ingested Clostridium botulinum spores in honey, in which botulinum toxin cleaves presynaptic SNARE proteins at the cholinergic neuromuscular junction, preventing acetylcholine vesicle fusion and reducing EPSPs at the motor end plate, producing the descending, symmetric flaccid paralysis and poor feeding characteristic of this diagnosis. The distractors incorrectly substitute muscarinic, GABAergic, or glycinergic synapses, describe antibody-mediated postsynaptic destruction, which would instead suggest myasthenia gravis rather than a toxin-mediated presynaptic defect, or invert the net effect to increased EPSPs with spastic rather than flaccid paralysis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Infant botulism from honey ingestion is a classic pediatric vignette that tests the same presynaptic SNARE mechanism as adult botulism but in a distinct clinical context. COMLEX: The honey exposure history is the single most important clue distinguishing this diagnosis from other causes of infantile hypotonia on a board-style vignette.","crossRef":"📖 Differentiating neuromuscular junction weakness patterns from upper motor neuron, lower motor neuron, and myopathic patterns, the framework this infant's flaccid, descending weakness fits within","isHighYield":true}]};
